{"request_id":"3179a0420d39f2739a865c6c","rewritten_text":"“Keeping firmly before us the particulars to which I have directed your notice—the singular voice, the extraordinary agility, and the astonishing want of motive for a murder of such exceptional atrocity—let us consider the slaughter itself. A woman has been strangled by the strength of hands and forced head foremost up a chimney. Common murderers do not kill in this fashion; still less do they dispose of their victims so. You must admit that this thrusting of the corpse into the chimney has something excessively strange about it, something wholly at variance with our ordinary conception of human conduct, even if we imagine the persons concerned to be the most abandoned of men. Reflect also upon the strength required to drive a body up that opening with such force that several people together could scarcely pull it down.

“Consider next the other signs of a most extraordinary exertion of strength. Upon the hearth lay thick locks—exceedingly thick locks—of grey human hair, torn out by the roots. You know the considerable force required to wrench even twenty or thirty hairs at once from the head. You examined those locks as I did. Fragments of the scalp still clung to their roots—a dreadful sight, and certain evidence of the enormous power employed in tearing out perhaps half a million hairs together. The elderly woman’s throat had not simply been cut: her head had been completely separated from her body, and the weapon was nothing more than a razor. Notice, too, the brutal savagery of these acts. I pass over the bruises on Madame L’Espanaye’s body. Monsieur Dumas and his estimable colleague Monsieur Etienne have declared them to be the work of a blunt instrument, and in this they are entirely right. That instrument was plainly the stone paving of the courtyard, upon which the victim fell from the window overlooking the bed. Simple as this explanation may now appear, the police missed it for the very reason that they overlooked the width of the shutters: the matter of the nails had completely closed their minds to the possibility that the windows could ever have been opened.

“If, together with all this, you have duly considered the chamber’s peculiar disorder, we have now brought into conjunction these ideas: astonishing agility, strength beyond the human, savage ferocity, slaughter without motive, a grotesqueness of horror wholly foreign to humanity, and a voice whose tone was unfamiliar to listeners of many nations, and which contained no distinct or intelligible articulation of syllables.”"}

{"request_id":"e1d2fc991075b096ad03c51f","rewritten_text":"It was after two when a sudden turn brought me close to the lake, near a cove where a little rowing boat lay moored. Our house was not far away, and I could see my father and aunt walking on the lawn. I jumped into the boat and, being thoroughly accustomed to such adventures, pushed off from the shore and put all my strength into rowing swiftly across. My father has often told me that, approaching in my white dress with only my tartan rachan wrapped around me, my hair streaming over my shoulders and the boat flying faster than anyone could have thought I could propel it, I looked more like a spirit than a human girl. As I reached the shore, my father steadied the boat; I sprang lightly out and a moment later was in his arms.

Now my life truly began. Everything around me changed from a dull sameness into a brilliant scene of joy and delight. The happiness of being with my father went far beyond even my most hopeful expectations. We were constantly together, and never ran out of things to discuss. He had spent his sixteen years away among peoples almost unknown in Europe. He had traveled through Persia, Arabia, and northern India, entering the homes of the inhabitants with a freedom granted to few Europeans. His accounts of their customs, his stories, and his descriptions of the countryside filled delightful hours whenever we grew tired of discussing our own plans for the future.

Affection was so unfamiliar to me that I listened in delight to every word when he described his feelings for me during those long years of seeming neglect. “At first,” he said, “I could not bear to think of my poor little girl. But later, as my grief diminished and hope returned, you were the one person my thoughts could turn to. Among cities and deserts, your little fairy figure, as I imagined it, was always drifting before my eyes. The northern breeze felt sweeter and gentler as it refreshed me, because it seemed to bring something of your spirit with it. I often thought of returning at once and taking you away with me to some fertile island where we could live in peace forever. On my journey home, so many fears broke in upon my passionate hopes that my impatience became almost unbearable. I did not dare imagine that the sun might shine and the moon rise over your grave instead of your living form. But no, that has not happened. I have my Mathilda, my comfort and my hope.”"}

{"request_id":"8fdf178d0fba641be1e34035","rewritten_text":"Few could claim a heart so unblemished, or such true humility of spirit united with a steadfast trust in her own honour and a faith in the honour of others. She had led a secluded existence from her earliest years. Her mother had died while she was very young, but her father had given himself entirely to overseeing her education. He held many singular opinions, and these shaped the plan he followed in bringing her up. She knew well the heroes of Greece and Rome, and those English heroes who had flourished some centuries earlier, yet knew almost nothing of the events unfolding around her. Few of the authors she had read had written within the preceding fifty years, though apart from this limitation her reading was exceedingly wide. Thus, while she seemed less familiar than he with the secrets of society and life, her knowledge went deeper and rested upon sounder ground; and even had her loveliness and gentleness failed to enchant him, her mind would always have kept his captive. He regarded her as his guide, and adored her so completely that he took pleasure in heightening, in his own thoughts, the feeling of inferiority she sometimes inspired.

His mother died when he was nineteen. At this loss he departed from college, and, setting aside his former companions for a time, withdrew to the neighbourhood where his Diana lived. All his comfort came from her gentle voice and her still dearer caresses. This brief absence from his friends gave him the resolution to declare his independence. He felt that, however much they might mock his proposed marriage, they would not venture to show their scorn once it was accomplished. He therefore sought his guardian's permission, which he gained with some trouble, and that of his beloved's father, which was granted more readily. Without informing anyone else of his design, he had made Diana his wife by the time he reached his twentieth birthday.

He loved her ardently, and her tenderness exercised a charm over him that left no room for thoughts of anyone else. He asked some college friends to visit, but their shallowness revolted him. Diana had rent the veil that had hitherto confined him to boyhood: now he was a man. He wondered that he could ever have shared the stock expressions and opinions of his fellow students, or for an instant have dreaded the disapproval of such people. He relinquished those earlier friendships, not through inconstancy, but because they were truly beneath him."}

{"request_id":"4ba7dfeef32b2d28ef3bc4e5","rewritten_text":"I uncovered it, and a light breeze soon blew it into flame. Noticing this, I made a fan from branches and used it to revive the embers whenever they nearly went out. When night returned, I was pleased to discover that the fire gave light as well as warmth. I also learned that it could improve my food. Some scraps the travelers had left behind were roasted, and these tasted much better than the berries I gathered from the trees. So I tried cooking my food the same way, putting it on the glowing embers. This ruined the berries but greatly improved the nuts and roots.

“Food grew scarce, however, and I often spent an entire day searching without success for a few acorns to ease my hunger. Because of this, I decided to leave the place where I had been living and look for somewhere I could meet my few needs more easily. I deeply regretted having to leave the fire behind. I had found it by accident and did not know how to make another. I spent several hours thinking hard about this problem, but had to give up trying to solve it. Wrapping myself in my cloak, I set off through the wood toward the setting sun. I wandered for three days before finally reaching open country. Heavy snow had fallen the night before, leaving the fields entirely white. The scene was bleak, and the cold, wet substance covering the ground chilled my feet.

“It was about seven in the morning, and I desperately wanted food and shelter. At last I saw a small hut on a rise in the ground, no doubt built for a shepherd’s use. I had never seen anything like it and examined the building with great curiosity. Its door was open, so I went inside. An old man sat near a fire, preparing his breakfast over it. Hearing a noise, he turned around. When he saw me, he screamed, fled the hut, and ran across the fields at a speed his frail body hardly seemed capable of. His appearance was unlike anyone’s I had seen before, and both it and his flight surprised me somewhat. But the sight of the hut delighted me. Here the snow and rain could not reach me, and the ground was dry. At that moment it seemed as wonderful and heavenly a refuge as Pandemonium had seemed to the demons of hell after their suffering in the lake of fire."}

{"request_id":"2179abaf80810ca5f85af46c","rewritten_text":"Indeed, I had no worries about her. She was what people call a capable woman and could manage her affairs without my help. In truth, I believe she always thought of me as a lazy boy, a useless extra, good only for building castles in the air, and was rather pleased to see me go. It was a dark night when I said goodbye. Taking along as assistants the three creditors who had caused me so much trouble, I carried the balloon, its basket, and its equipment with them by an indirect route to the place where we had stored the other supplies. Everything was still untouched, and I immediately set to work.

“It was the first of April. As I have said, the night was dark, without a single visible star. Drizzling rain fell now and then, making us very uncomfortable. My main worry, though, was the balloon. Despite its protective varnish, it was becoming heavy with moisture, and the powder could also be damaged. I therefore kept my three creditors hard at work, packing ice around the middle barrel and stirring the acid in the others. They constantly bothered me with questions about what I meant to do with all this equipment, and complained bitterly about the exhausting work. They could not see, they said, what good could come from getting soaked to the skin simply to take part in such dreadful spells. I grew anxious and worked with all my strength. I honestly believe the fools thought I had made a bargain with the devil and that my present activities were thoroughly wicked. I was therefore terribly afraid they would abandon me altogether. Still, I managed to calm them by promising to pay every debt in full as soon as I finished the business at hand. Naturally, they interpreted my promises in their own way. They doubtless imagined that, whatever else happened, I would acquire a vast amount of ready cash. Provided I paid everything I owed them, with a little extra for their work, I dare say they cared very little what became of either my soul or my body.

“After about four and a half hours, I found the balloon inflated enough. I attached the basket and loaded all my tools into it. I made sure to include the condensing equipment, a plentiful supply of water, and a large store of food, such as pemmican, which packs a great deal of nourishment into a fairly small amount of space."}

{"request_id":"795941397b932c1a400821dd","rewritten_text":"I come from a small land surrounded by sea and shaded by clouds. When I picture the whole globe, with its boundless ocean and unexplored continents, my homeland seems only a tiny speck in that immense whole. Yet in intellectual power it far outweighed countries with more land and more people. It is indeed the human mind alone that created everything good or great for humanity; Nature herself was only its chief servant. England, far to the north in the restless sea, now appears in my dreams as a vast ship with a full crew, mastering the winds and riding proudly over the waves. In my boyhood she was my entire universe. Standing on my native hills, I saw plains and mountains reach as far as my eyes could follow. My countrymen’s homes dotted them, and their labor had made the land fertile. To me, that spot was the very center of the earth. The rest of the world seemed like a story that neither my imagination nor my understanding would have struggled to forget.

From the beginning, my fortunes have shown how powerfully change can shape the course of a person’s life. In my case, this was almost an inheritance. Nature had showered my father with the enviable gifts of wit and imagination, then left these winds to drive his boat through life without reason to steer it or judgment to guide the voyage. He came from an obscure family, but circumstances brought him to public attention early. He soon spent the small property inherited from his father in the glittering world of fashion and luxury he had entered. During his brief, thoughtless youth, the fashionable idlers adored him. Among his greatest admirers was the young king, who escaped political intrigue and the difficult duties of ruling to find unfailing amusement and renewed spirits in my father’s company. My father could never control his impulses. They constantly led him into trouble from which only his ingenuity could rescue him. His growing mountain of gambling and business debts would have crushed anyone else, but he bore it cheerfully, with unstoppable good humor. His presence was so essential at the dinners and gatherings of the rich that they treated his failings as forgivable and welcomed him with praise that went to his head.

Popularity of this kind, like all popularity, soon fades. Meanwhile, the difficulties he faced in every direction increased at a frightening rate, far beyond his limited means of escaping them."}

{"request_id":"bb7e5b70ed047b83750f2dfb","rewritten_text":"You will be glad to hear that the beginning of this enterprise, which has filled you with such dark expectations, has brought no disaster. I arrived here yesterday, and my first duty is to assure my dear sister that I am well and increasingly confident of success.

I am already far north of London. Walking through the streets of Petersburg, I feel a cold northern breeze against my cheeks that steadies my nerves and fills me with delight. Can you understand that feeling? This breeze has traveled from the regions I am approaching and offers me a first taste of their icy climate. Encouraged by this promising wind, my daydreams grow more intense and vivid. I cannot persuade myself to picture the pole as a place of frost and desolation. In my imagination it remains a realm of beauty and pleasure. There, Margaret, the sun is always visible, its broad disk grazing the horizon and casting an unending splendor. There—for, with your permission, sister, I will place some trust in earlier navigators—snow and frost are banished. Sailing across a calm sea, we may be carried to a land whose wonders and beauty surpass every inhabited region yet discovered. Its natural life and landscape may be unique, as the behavior of the heavenly bodies undoubtedly is in those unexplored solitudes. What might we not expect in a country of eternal light? There I may discover the marvelous force that draws the compass needle, and bring order to a thousand astronomical observations whose apparent irregularities need only this voyage to become consistent forever. I will satisfy my burning curiosity by seeing a part of the world no one has visited, and may walk on ground no human foot has ever marked. These prospects draw me onward. They are enough to overcome every fear of danger or death and make me begin this demanding voyage with the delight of a child setting off in a little boat with holiday companions to explore his native river. But even if all these guesses prove false, you cannot deny the immeasurable benefit I will bring humanity, down to its final generation, by finding a passage near the pole to countries that currently take many months to reach. Or I may uncover the secret of magnetism, which, if it can be discovered at all, can only be revealed through an undertaking like mine.

These thoughts have driven away the anxiety with which I began this letter. My heart glows with an enthusiasm that lifts me to heaven, for nothing calms the mind so well as a firm purpose: a point on which the soul can fix its intellectual gaze."}

{"request_id":"0ca74bd5c6bb15dbfb6b19e7","rewritten_text":"Yet even then I had not drained the bitter cup to its last drop; I had not fully believed my loss; I did not yet know in every pulse, every nerve, every thought, that I alone survived of my race—that I was the LAST MAN.

Clouds had gathered over the day, and at sunset a fine rain began to fall. Even the everlasting heavens shed tears, I thought; why, then, should mortal man be ashamed to exhaust himself in weeping? I recalled the ancient tales in which human creatures melt through their tears into fountains that flow forever. Ah, if only it could be so! Then my fate would in some measure resemble the watery deaths of Adrian and Clara. Oh, grief has strange fancies; it spins a web on which every shape and change around it inscribes the story of its sorrow. It mingles with all living nature and draws nourishment from every object; like light, it enters everything, and like light, lends everything its own hues.

My search had carried me some distance from the place where I had been cast ashore, until I reached one of the watchtowers standing at regular intervals along the Italian coast. I welcomed shelter, and welcomed a structure made by human hands after gazing so long upon nature’s desolate emptiness. Entering, I climbed the rough spiral stairs to the guardroom. Fate was kind this far: no distressing remnant of its former occupants remained. A few boards resting on two iron trestles, scattered with dried maize leaves, offered me a bed. An open chest holding some half-moldy biscuits stirred an appetite that perhaps had existed earlier, though I had not recognized it until now. A fierce, scorching thirst also tormented me, caused by the seawater I had swallowed and my body’s exhaustion. Kindly nature had attached pleasure to the satisfaction of these needs, so that I—even I!—grew refreshed and calmer while eating that wretched food and drinking a little sour wine from a half-filled flask left in the deserted dwelling. Then I lay down upon the bed, which no shipwrecked sufferer could scorn. After the loathsome smell of seaweed, the earthy scent of the dry leaves soothed my senses. I forgot my solitude. I looked neither behind me nor ahead; my senses settled into quiet rest. I slept and dreamed of beloved inland scenes: haymakers, a shepherd whistling to summon his dog’s help in driving the flock into the fold, and the sights and sounds of my childhood among the mountains, which I had long since forgotten."}

{"request_id":"2aa0c827c2b400d64beec80d","rewritten_text":"“Madame and Mademoiselle L’Espanaye were not killed by spirits. Those responsible were corporeal beings and departed by corporeal means. How, then? Happily, there is only one way to reason on this question, and it must bring us to a definite conclusion. Let us consider the possible exits one at a time. Plainly, when the party came up the stairs, the assassins were either in the room where Mademoiselle L’Espanaye was discovered or in the adjoining chamber. Our search for outlets is therefore confined to those two rooms. The police have exposed the floors, ceilings, and wall masonry in every direction. No concealed passage could have eluded their scrutiny. Nevertheless, unwilling to rely upon their eyes, I inspected the place with my own. There were, indeed, no hidden exits. The two doors opening into the passage were firmly locked, their keys on the inside. Consider next the chimneys. Though of the usual width for eight or ten feet above the fireplaces, neither could accommodate a large cat along its entire length. Escape by the means already considered being absolutely impossible, we are left with the windows. No one could have left through the front-room windows unseen by the people in the street. The murderers must consequently have used the windows of the rear chamber. Having reached this conclusion so unambiguously, we cannot, as reasoning men, dismiss it because it appears impossible. Our task is simply to demonstrate that these seeming ‘impossibilities’ are not, in fact, impossible.

“There are two windows in that room. Furniture obstructs neither the view nor the approach to one, which is entirely exposed. The lower part of the second is concealed by the head of the cumbersome bedstead pushed against it. The first was found fastened securely from inside and withstood the greatest efforts to lift it. A large hole had been bored with a gimlet into the left of its frame, and a particularly stout nail occupied it, driven nearly to its head. Inspection of the second window revealed a similar nail placed in precisely the same manner; energetic efforts to raise that sash were equally unsuccessful. The police felt completely assured that no escape had occurred in either direction. Removing the nails and opening the windows was therefore judged an unnecessary labor.

“My examination went rather further, for the reason I have just explained: I knew that it was here that every apparent impossibility must be shown to have no real existence.”"}

{"request_id":"9730dcd6162aeed1c535bf38","rewritten_text":"I passed several funeral processions, each with only a few mourners. People watching saw them as signs of terrible things to come. Some stared with a wild eagerness; others hurried away in fear; some cried aloud.

After arranging immediate help for the sick, Adrian’s main effort had been to hide the signs and spread of the plague from London’s people. He knew that fear and gloomy expectations helped disease take hold, and that despair and constant worry made the body especially vulnerable to infection. So no distressing sights were visible. Most shops stayed open, and there were still some people in the streets. Yet although London did not look like a plague-stricken city, it seemed greatly changed to me, since I had not seen it since the outbreak began. There were no carriages, and tall grass grew in the streets. The houses looked abandoned, with most shutters closed. The people I passed wore pale, frightened expressions, so unlike Londoners’ usual busy manner. My lone carriage drew attention as it rattled toward the Protector’s Palace. The fashionable streets around it looked even emptier and more depressing. I found Adrian’s waiting room crowded, for this was the hour when he received visitors. Unwilling to interrupt his work, I waited and watched people come and go. They were from the middle and lower classes, and had lost their livelihoods when trade and all the various forms of money-making so characteristic of our country had stopped. Those arriving looked anxious and sometimes terrified. Their faces were strikingly different from the calm, even satisfied expressions of those who had already seen him. In their livelier movements and happier faces I could see my friend’s influence. At two o’clock, no more people were admitted. Those who had missed their chance left in sadness or resentment, and I entered the room where Adrian received them.

I was struck by how much healthier he looked. He no longer drooped like a pampered spring flower that has grown beyond its strength and bends beneath the weight of its own blossoms. His eyes were bright, his face calm. His whole bearing showed a focused energy quite unlike his old weakness. He sat at a table with several secretaries, who were sorting requests or recording the notes taken during that day’s meetings. Two or three people were still waiting for help. I admired his fairness and patience. Anyone who could support themselves outside London was advised to leave immediately, and he provided the means for them to do so."}

{"request_id":"3f4e75438abc41c62536f2bf","rewritten_text":"“Each compares it, not to someone speaking a national language he knows, but to precisely the reverse. The Frenchman believes the voice belonged to a Spaniard, and says he ‘might have made out words had he known Spanish.’ The Dutchman insists it was a French voice; yet the report tells us that, ‘because he understood no French, the witness was questioned through an interpreter.’ The Englishman supposes the speaker German, although he ‘knows no German.’ The Spaniard ‘is certain’ it was an English voice, but bases his opinion entirely ‘on the intonation,’ since ‘he is unacquainted with English.’ The Italian takes it for a Russian voice, though he ‘has never spoken with a Russian native.’ Furthermore, another Frenchman contradicts the first, declaring positively that the voice was Italian; but as he does not know Italian, he too, like the Spaniard, ‘relies upon the intonation.’ How exceedingly strange that voice must have been to produce testimony of this description! Even its tones offered nothing recognizable to inhabitants of Europe’s five great divisions. You may suggest an Asiatic or an African speaker. Neither Asiatics nor Africans are numerous in Paris; without disputing your inference, however, I shall draw your attention to just three points. One witness describes the voice as ‘harsh rather than high-pitched.’ Two more characterize it as ‘rapid and uneven.’ Not one witness mentions distinguishing words, or even sounds that resembled words.

“I cannot tell,” Dupin went on, “what effect my reasoning has thus far had upon you. Nevertheless, I readily assert that sound deductions from this part of the evidence alone—the statements concerning the harsh and the shrill voices—suffice to create a suspicion that ought to guide every subsequent stage of the inquiry. I have said ‘sound deductions,’ but that does not quite convey my intention. I mean that these deductions are the only correct ones, and that this suspicion follows from them necessarily, as their sole outcome. I shall not yet disclose the suspicion itself. I ask you only to remember that it struck me powerfully enough to give my investigation of the room a particular shape and direction.

“Let us now imagine ourselves inside that chamber. What is the first thing we should look for? The route by which the murderers escaped. I think I may safely say that neither of us accepts supernatural occurrences.”"}

{"request_id":"346ee94e196f91ad841f3443","rewritten_text":"Each witness compares it to a speaker of a language he does not know, rather than one he understands. The Frenchman thinks it was a Spaniard's voice, and says he ‘might have recognized some words if he knew Spanish.’ The Dutchman insists it belonged to a Frenchman. Yet we are told that, ‘because this witness does not understand French, he was questioned through an interpreter.’ The Englishman believes it was a German voice, though he ‘does not understand German.’ The Spaniard ‘is certain’ it was an Englishman, but judges entirely ‘by the tone,’ since ‘he knows no English.’ The Italian thinks it was a Russian, although he ‘has never spoken with anyone from Russia.’ A second Frenchman disagrees with the first and is sure the speaker was Italian. But he does not know Italian, and, like the Spaniard, bases his certainty on the tone. How extraordinarily strange that voice must have been to produce evidence like this! People from five major divisions of Europe could recognize nothing familiar even in its tones. You may suggest that it belonged to someone from Asia or Africa. There are few Asians or Africans in Paris, but without dismissing that possibility, I ask you to notice three things. One witness describes the voice as ‘harsh rather than high-pitched.’ Two others call it ‘rapid and uneven.’ None of the witnesses could identify any words, or even any sounds that resembled words.

“I do not know,” Dupin continued, “what you have made of my argument so far. But I can confidently say that sound reasoning from this part of the evidence alone—the testimony about the deep and high-pitched voices—is enough to raise a suspicion that should guide every further step in investigating the mystery. Even ‘sound reasoning’ does not fully express what I mean. These are the only conclusions that can properly be drawn, and they lead inevitably to one particular suspicion. I will not yet tell you what that suspicion is. For now, just remember that it was strong enough to give my examination of the room a definite purpose and direction.

“Let us now imagine that we are inside that room. What should we look for first? The way the murderers got out. I think I can safely say that neither of us believes in supernatural events.”"}

{"request_id":"9bc5d2b3b38e23090745ea3a","rewritten_text":"I removed its covering, and a light breeze soon stirred it into flame. Taking note of this as well, I fashioned a fan from branches, with which I revived the embers whenever they were almost dead. At the next coming of night I discovered with delight that the fire furnished light as well as warmth. This element proved useful also in preparing my food: some scraps abandoned by the travellers had been roasted, and I found them far more appetising than the berries I plucked from the trees. I therefore attempted to cook my own provisions similarly, laying them upon the glowing coals. This treatment ruined the berries, but greatly improved the nuts and roots.

“Food grew scarce, however, and I often devoted an entire day to seeking, unsuccessfully, a handful of acorns to quiet my hunger. Accordingly I determined to leave the spot where I had until then resided and find another where my few needs might be met more readily. In making this departure I deeply regretted losing the fire, which chance had given me and which I had no knowledge of creating anew. For several hours I earnestly considered this obstacle, but at last had to abandon every attempt to overcome it. Drawing my cloak about me, I set off through the wood in the direction of the sinking sun. Three days passed in such wanderings before I finally reached open country. Heavy snow had fallen during the previous night, leaving all the fields uniformly white. The prospect was cheerless, and the cold, damp material spread over the earth chilled my feet.

“It was roughly seven in the morning, and I yearned for something to eat and a place of refuge. Presently I discerned a little hut upon a rise, plainly erected for a shepherd's use. Never having beheld such a thing, I studied its construction with considerable curiosity. The door stood open, and I went inside. An old man sat by a fire, preparing his morning meal over it. Hearing a sound, he turned; at the sight of me he screamed, fled the hut, and raced over the fields at a pace his feeble frame would hardly have suggested. Both his appearance, unlike any I had previously encountered, and his flight astonished me somewhat. But the hut itself delighted me. Neither rain nor snow could enter; its floor was dry. To me it seemed as perfect and heavenly a sanctuary as Pandæmonium must have seemed to the demons of hell after their torments in the fiery lake.”"}

{"request_id":"b8da31c3ec75be5bc6959be1","rewritten_text":"It now showed my elevation to be 132,000 feet, or twenty-five miles; thus at that moment I surveyed no less than one three-hundred-and-twentieth of the earth’s entire surface. By nine o’clock the land to the east had again vanished from view, though not before I perceived that the balloon was drifting swiftly north-northwest. The curvature of the ocean below was strikingly clear, although drifting banks of cloud repeatedly obscured the prospect. I noticed that even the frailest vapors rose no higher than ten miles above sea level.

“At half past nine I experimented by casting a handful of feathers out through the valve. Rather than floating as I had anticipated, they plunged straight downward together, like a bullet, with tremendous speed, and disappeared within a few seconds. At first I could find no explanation for this remarkable occurrence, since I could not believe my ascent had suddenly accelerated to such an extraordinary degree. Soon, however, it struck me that the air had become too thin to support even feathers. They had indeed fallen rapidly, just as they seemed to, and what astonished me was the combined speed of their descent and my rise.

“By ten I discovered that little required my immediate attention. Everything proceeded admirably, and I believed the balloon was ascending at a speed that increased every moment, though I could no longer measure the rate of that increase. I felt neither pain nor discomfort of any sort, and my spirits were better than at any time since leaving Rotterdam. I occupied myself alternately with inspecting my several instruments and renewing the atmosphere inside the chamber. I resolved to perform this latter task regularly every forty minutes, less because such frequent renewal was strictly necessary than to safeguard my health. Meanwhile I could not resist imagining what lay ahead. My fancy delighted in the moon’s wild, dreamlike regions. Imagination, freed for once from her restraints, wandered wherever she pleased through the shifting marvels of an indistinct and changeable land. At one moment there were ancient, venerable forests, jagged cliffs, and waterfalls crashing noisily into bottomless chasms. At the next I found myself abruptly in hushed noonday wildernesses, where no breath of wind ever entered, and where immense fields of poppies and slender flowers resembling lilies stretched to a weary distance, forever motionless and silent."}

{"request_id":"9a8f7703ac41640d96fbb07b","rewritten_text":"Elizabeth was sad and hopeless. Her usual activities no longer pleased her. Any pleasure seemed disrespectful to the dead, and she believed that endless grief and tears were the only proper tribute to innocence so cruelly destroyed. She was no longer the happy girl who had walked beside the lake with me when we were younger, speaking eagerly of our future. She had suffered the first of those sorrows that loosen our attachment to life, and its shadow had put out her sweetest smiles.

“When I think, my dear cousin,” she said, “of Justine Moritz's terrible death, the world and everything in it look different to me. Before this, the stories of cruelty and injustice I read in books or heard from others seemed to belong to the distant past, or to describe imaginary evils. At least, they were far away: I understood them in my mind without truly picturing them. Now suffering has reached our home, and people seem like monsters hungry for each other's blood. Yet surely I am unfair. Everyone thought the poor girl was guilty. If she had committed the crime for which she died, she would indeed have been among the most wicked people alive. To kill the son of her friend and benefactor for a few jewels—a child she had cared for since birth and seemed to love as her own! I could never agree to anyone's execution, but I would certainly have thought such a person unfit to live among others. Yet she was innocent. I know it; I feel it. You believe it too, and that strengthens my certainty. Oh, Victor, when a lie can look so much like truth, who can feel sure of lasting happiness? I feel as though I am walking along a cliff's edge, with thousands of people pressing toward me, trying to push me into the depths below. William and Justine were murdered, and the killer goes unpunished. He walks freely through the world, perhaps even respected. But even if I were condemned to die on the scaffold for those same crimes, I would never exchange places with that wretched man.”

Listening to her caused me unbearable pain. Though I had not committed the acts myself, I was in effect the real murderer. Elizabeth saw my anguish in my face. She gently took my hand and said, “My dearest friend, you must try to be calm. God knows how deeply these events have hurt me, but I am not as miserable as you are.”"}

{"request_id":"e5920e86c98411652037b7f4","rewritten_text":"Spirits of the storm, receive me! Powers of destruction, open your arms and hold me forever! Unless a kinder power has chosen another end, so that after long suffering I may receive my reward and feel my heart beating close to another heart like mine again.

The Tiber, a road laid by nature's own hand through her continent, lay at my feet, and many boats were tied along its banks. With a few books, some provisions, and my dog, I would take one of them and drift down the river to the sea. Then, staying close to land, I would follow the beautiful shores and sunny headlands of the blue Mediterranean, pass Naples and travel along Calabria, and brave the twin dangers of Scylla and Charybdis. Fearlessly—for what had I to lose?—I would cross the sea toward Malta and the more distant Cyclades. I would avoid Constantinople: its familiar towers and inlets belonged to a life utterly different from mine now. I would follow Asia Minor and Syria, pass the Nile's seven mouths, and turn north again. Leaving forgotten Carthage and deserted Libya behind, I would reach the pillars of Hercules. And then—wherever I might go—the muddy caves and silent depths of the sea might become my home before this long voyage ended. Disease might strike my heart as I drifted alone on the restless Mediterranean. Or, somewhere I landed, I might find what I sought—a companion. If that could not be, the solitary wanderer would keep raising his sail and gripping the tiller forever, frail and grey-haired, his youth already buried with those he loved. Still following heaven's breezes, he would round one headland after another, anchor in bay after bay, and cross the barren ocean. Leaving the green land of his native Europe behind, I might sail down Africa's tawny coast, survive the Cape's fierce seas, and moor my battered little boat in a creek shaded by fragrant spice groves on distant islands of the Indian Ocean.

These are wild dreams. Yet they have ruled my imagination ever since they came to me a week ago, as I stood high on St. Peter's. I have chosen my boat and put my few supplies aboard. I have selected a handful of books, chiefly Homer and Shakespeare. But the world's libraries stand open to me, and I can replenish my collection in any port. I do not expect any change for the better; yet I cannot endure the sameness of the present."}

{"request_id":"eeeee0757b17ac062bc61a9f","rewritten_text":"My father had been the life of my life, and his memory was so still. I could feel gratitude toward someone else, but I could never again love or hope as I once had. Everything was suffering; even my pleasures were endured, not enjoyed. I was like a lonely place among mountains, enclosed on every side by steep black cliffs, where no warmth could enter and no path led out to sunnier fields. So, although friendship soothed me for a time, it could not restore me. It came like a gentle visitor; it left, and I scarcely felt its loss. The will to live was dead within me. Do not be surprised, then, that I welcomed heaven's best gift—a friend—with so little joy, or mourned its departure with so little bitterness.

My friend's name was Woodville. I will briefly tell his story, so that you may judge how cold my heart must have been to resist his eloquent words and tender sympathy. You will also see how, since he too was deeply unhappy, we might have comforted each other, had misery not turned me to stone like the head of Medusa. Woodville's troubles did not strike at the heart's deepest core as mine did. His grief was natural, capable of purifying the heart rather than destroying it. When its shadow passed, he might emerge brighter and happier than before.

Woodville was the son of a poor clergyman and had been educated in the classics. He was among those very few people whom fortune favours from birth, showering them without limit with gifts of mind and body. Under her special protection, no imperfection, however slight, and no disappointment, however brief, seemed permitted to touch him. She appeared to have made his mind from excellence that nothing base could stain, and given him an understanding that no error could lead astray. His genius was extraordinary; when it rose like a bright star in the east, everyone turned toward it in admiration. He was a poet. That name has been cheapened so often that it cannot convey everything he was. He resembled an ancient poet whom the Muses had crowned in his cradle and whose lips had nourished bees. As he walked among other men, a heavenly glow seemed to surround him, setting him apart and raising him above them. His exceptional beauty, the dazzling fire in his eyes, and the rich sound of his words, which left listeners silent with delighted wonder, made him tower above everyone else. In his presence, they seemed to have been created only to serve his greater excellence."}

{"request_id":"51b58cfa58a75de7bc310aee","rewritten_text":"“I then reasoned backward from the evidence. The murderers had escaped through one of these windows. If so, they could not have fastened the windows from inside afterward, as they were found fastened. This apparently obvious objection had brought the police investigation of the windows to a halt. Yet they were fastened. They must therefore be capable of fastening themselves. There was no avoiding that conclusion. I went to the unobstructed window, pulled out the nail with some difficulty, and tried to lift the sash. As I had expected, it resisted every effort. I now knew there must be a concealed spring, and this confirmation convinced me that my initial reasoning, at least, was sound, however mysterious the nails remained. A careful search soon revealed the hidden spring. I pressed it and, satisfied with what I had discovered, left the sash down.

“I then put the nail back and examined it closely. Anyone climbing out through this window could have shut it afterward, allowing the spring to catch, but could not have replaced the nail. The conclusion was clear and narrowed my investigation further. The murderers must have escaped through the other window. Assuming the springs on both windows were alike, as seemed probable, there must be some difference between the nails, or at least in the way they were fitted. Climbing onto the bed's fabric support, I examined the second window closely over the headboard. I slid my hand down behind the board and easily found and pressed the spring. As I had supposed, it worked exactly like the other. I then inspected the nail. It was just as thick as its counterpart and apparently fitted in the same way, driven in almost to its head.

“You will say I was baffled; but if you think that, you have misunderstood the reasoning. To borrow a phrase from hunting, I had never once lost the trail. Not for an instant had I lost the scent. Every link in the chain was sound. I had followed the mystery to its final point, and that point was the nail. As I say, in every respect it looked identical to the nail in the other window. But however conclusive that resemblance might appear, it counted for absolutely nothing beside the fact that the trail ended here, at precisely this spot. ‘There must be something wrong with the nail,’ I told myself. I touched it, and the head, together with about a quarter of an inch of the shaft, came away in my fingers."}

{"request_id":"bb3e88bbbd2f5271586f72e0","rewritten_text":"‘I then reasoned backward from the facts. The murderers had escaped through one of these windows. If that was true, they could not have fastened the windows again from inside, as we found them. This obvious difficulty had stopped the police from investigating further in that direction. Yet the windows were fastened. They must therefore be able to fasten themselves. There was no avoiding that conclusion. I went to the window that was not blocked, pulled out its nail with some difficulty, and tried to lift the sliding frame. It would not move, however hard I tried, just as I had expected. Now I knew there must be a hidden spring. This confirmation persuaded me that at least my starting assumptions were right, although the nails remained a mystery. A careful search soon revealed the spring. I pressed it and, satisfied with my discovery, left the window closed.

‘I then put the nail back and studied it closely. Someone climbing out through this window could have closed it behind them, allowing the spring to catch. But they could not have replaced the nail. The conclusion was clear, and once again it narrowed my search. The murderers must have escaped through the other window. If both frames had the same springs, as seemed likely, then there must be a difference between the nails, or at least in how they were fitted. I climbed onto the bed's canvas support and examined the second window closely over the headboard. Reaching down behind the board, I easily found and pressed the spring. As I had expected, it was just like the other one. Next I examined the nail. It was equally thick and seemed to be fitted in exactly the same way, driven in almost to its head.

‘You may say I must have been puzzled. If so, you have misunderstood my reasoning. To borrow a term from hunting, I had never once lost the trail. Not for an instant had the scent disappeared. Every link in the chain of reasoning was sound. I had followed the secret to its final point, and that point was the nail. As I have said, it looked in every way like the nail in the other window. But that fact, convincing though it might seem, counted for nothing beside the knowledge that the trail ended here, at this very spot. “There must be something wrong with the nail,” I said. I touched it, and the head, with about a quarter of an inch of the shaft, came away in my fingers.’"}

{"request_id":"df873da2b10f21d0e9e19665","rewritten_text":"The following day he remained indoors, spending, in fact, nearly all his time in his own room, sick with a frantic dread of death and yet unconcerned with life itself. The sense of being pursued, trapped, hunted down was beginning to possess him. A mere stirring of the tapestry in the wind made him tremble. Dead leaves blowing against the leaded windows seemed like his own squandered resolutions and desperate regrets. Whenever he shut his eyes, the sailor's face reappeared, peering through the mist-clouded glass, and horror seemed to press its hand against his heart again.

Yet perhaps only his fancy had summoned vengeance from the darkness and placed those dreadful shapes of punishment before him. Real life was confusion, but imagination possessed a terrible logic. Imagination made remorse follow at the heels of sin; imagination gave every crime its deformed offspring. In the ordinary realm of facts, evil went unpunished and goodness unrewarded. The strong were granted success, the weak burdened with failure. That was everything. Besides, if a stranger had been skulking about the house, surely a servant or keeper would have noticed him. If footprints had appeared in the flowerbeds, the gardeners would have mentioned them. Yes, it was nothing but fancy. Sibyl Vane's brother had not returned to murder him. He had gone away aboard his ship, to perish in some wintry sea. From that man, at least, he was secure. Why, the fellow did not know his identity, could not possibly know it. His youthful mask had rescued him.

And still, if it had been no more than an illusion, how dreadful to reflect that conscience could summon such terrifying spectres, clothe them in visible shapes, and set them moving before one's eyes! What kind of existence awaited him if, night and day, the shadows of his crime watched from quiet corners, jeered from hidden places, whispered into his ear while he sat at a feast, or roused him with frozen fingers in his sleep? As this thought stole through his mind, terror drained his face, and the air seemed suddenly to grow colder. Oh, in what a frenzied hour of madness had he murdered his friend! How hideous even the recollection of that scene! It all unfolded before him once more. Every dreadful detail returned with a fresh intensity of horror. From time's black cavern arose the image of his sin, terrible and wrapped in scarlet."}

{"request_id":"25206fa36999ea2c58b91225","rewritten_text":"Throughout his present brief visit, Emma had seen scarcely anything of him, though enough to feel that the first encounter was behind her, and to conclude that the blend of resentment and self-importance now marking his manner was no improvement. Indeed, she was beginning to marvel that she had ever found him agreeable. The sight of him was bound so closely to unpleasant sensations that, save as a moral exercise—a penance, a lesson, a salutary humiliation of her pride—she would gladly have learned that she need never see him again. She wished him every happiness; but he caused her pain, and his prosperity at twenty miles’ distance would have pleased her best.

His marriage, however, must surely soften the discomfort of his continuing to live in Highbury. It would forestall many needless anxieties and ease many awkward situations. A Mrs. Elton would justify any alteration in their intercourse; their old familiarity could diminish unnoticed. Their civil relations would almost begin afresh.

Emma gave little thought to the lady herself. Doubtless she was sufficiently good for Mr. Elton, sufficiently accomplished for Highbury, sufficiently handsome—probably to appear plain beside Harriet. Concerning her connections Emma felt entirely secure, convinced that, after boasting of his own claims and scorning Harriet’s, he had gained nothing. On this subject the truth seemed discoverable. What sort of person she was remained uncertain, but who she was could be established; and apart from the ten thousand pounds, she seemed in no respect Harriet’s superior. She contributed neither a distinguished name nor ancestry nor alliance. Miss Hawkins was the younger of two daughters of a Bristol—merchant, as of course one must call him. Yet the whole proceeds of his commercial career appeared so modest that one might fairly suppose the dignity of his trade equally modest. She had customarily spent part of each winter in Bath, but Bristol was her home, the very centre of Bristol. Although her parents had died some years earlier, an uncle survived—in the law. No more precisely honourable description had been ventured than that he was in the law, and the daughter had lived with him. Emma imagined him some attorney’s overworked subordinate, too dull to advance. The entire magnificence of the connection seemed to rest upon the elder sister, who had married very well indeed: a gentleman living in great style near Bristol, who maintained two carriages! There the account concluded; there lay Miss Hawkins’ glory."}

{"request_id":"969957fe71e40d3839707a33","rewritten_text":"Yet even then I had not swallowed the last of that bitter draught. I was not fully convinced of my loss; I did not yet feel in every heartbeat, every nerve, every thought, that I alone remained of my race—that I was the last man.

Clouds had gathered during the day, and at sunset a fine rain began to fall. Even the everlasting heavens weep, I thought; why should a mortal man be ashamed to exhaust himself in tears? I recalled the ancient stories in which human beings dissolve through weeping into fountains that flow forever. If only it could be so! Then my fate would share something with the drowning deaths of Adrian and Clara. How strange grief is! From every shape and change around it, grief weaves a fabric on which to record its sorrow. It merges with all living nature and draws nourishment from every object. Like light, it enters everything; like light, it gives everything its own colours.

My search had taken me some distance from the place where I had washed ashore, and I reached one of the watchtowers spaced along the Italian coast. I welcomed the shelter, glad to discover something made by human hands after gazing so long at nature’s desolate emptiness. I entered and climbed the rough spiral staircase to the guardroom. Fate was merciful enough to have left no distressing trace of its former occupants. A few boards resting across two iron trestles and scattered with dried corn leaves offered me a bed. An open chest containing some half-mouldy biscuits awakened an appetite that perhaps had already existed, though I had not noticed it until now. I was also tormented by fierce, parching thirst, caused by the seawater I had swallowed and my body’s exhaustion. Kindly nature had made the satisfaction of these needs a source of pleasure, so that I—even I!—felt refreshed and soothed as I ate this wretched food and drank a little sour wine from a half-full flask left in the deserted tower. Then I lay down on the bed, which a shipwrecked man had no reason to despise. After the hateful smell of seaweed, the earthy scent of the dried leaves comforted my senses. I forgot my loneliness. I looked neither into the past nor toward the future; my senses settled into quiet. I fell asleep and dreamed of beloved inland scenes: haymakers, the shepherd whistling for his dog to help drive the flock into its pen, and the sights and sounds of my mountain childhood that I had long forgotten."}

{"request_id":"ccbc7cb0c407a1017f8138fd","rewritten_text":"Once this was done, I detached a few more loops from the rim, worked in another section of cloth, and fastened the loose loops to their corresponding buttons. In this way I could insert the entire upper portion of the bag between the netting and the hoop. Clearly the hoop would now fall inside the basket, leaving the buttons alone to support the full weight of the basket and everything it contained. At first this might seem an inadequate support, but it was nothing of the kind. The buttons were exceptionally strong and placed so closely together that each bore only a very small fraction of the total load. Indeed, I would have felt no concern even if the basket and its contents had weighed three times as much. I then lifted the hoop again inside the rubber covering and supported it at almost its previous height with three lightweight poles prepared for this purpose. Naturally, this kept the top of the bag spread open and held the lower part of the netting in its proper position. All that remained was to seal the opening of the enclosure. I easily accomplished this by gathering the folds of material together and twisting them tightly from inside with a sort of fixed tourniquet.

“Three circular panes of thick, clear glass had been fitted into the sides of the covering now surrounding the basket. These allowed me an unobstructed view in every horizontal direction. A fourth window of the same kind was set into the fabric beneath me, aligned with a small opening in the basket's floor. Through it I could look straight down. However, I had been unable to fit any similar arrangement overhead because of the particular way the upper opening was closed and the resulting folds in the cloth. I therefore could not expect to see anything directly above me. This scarcely mattered, of course: even if I had managed to install a window at the top, the balloon itself would have blocked the view and made it useless.

“About a foot beneath one of the side windows was a circular opening eight inches across. It was fitted with a brass rim whose inner edge was threaded to receive a screw. Into this rim I screwed the condenser's large tube, while the body of the machine remained, of course, inside the rubber chamber."}

{"request_id":"6b0588ab787a2c3f6fd62a7d","rewritten_text":"It is only four in the afternoon, but in this winter season the sun has already gone down. No clouds hang in the clear, freezing sky to catch its slanting rays, yet the air itself holds a faint pink glow, reflected in turn upon the snow covering the earth. I live in an isolated cottage on a vast, deserted heath; no sound of living voices reaches me. The empty plain stretches white before my eyes, broken only by a few dark patches where the midday sun has cleared the tops of pointed hillocks. There the snow slipped as it fell and settled less thickly than on level ground. A few birds peck at the solid ice over the pools, for the frost has lasted a long time.

My state of mind is strange. I am alone—utterly alone—in the world. Misfortune has swept across my life and left me withered. I know I am going to die, and I feel happy—joyful. I take my pulse: it is racing. I press my thin hand to my cheek: it burns. A fragile, restless spirit within me is giving off its final sparks. I will never see another winter's snow; I truly believe I will never again feel the life-giving warmth of a summer sun. With this conviction I begin to set down my tragic history. Perhaps a story like mine ought to die with me, but an indefinable feeling urges me forward, and I am too weak in mind and body to resist even the slightest impulse. When life still ran strongly through me, I believed my story possessed a sacred horror that made speaking of it impossible. Now, as death approaches, I profane its mysterious terrors. It resembles the grove of the Eumenides, which only the dying may enter; and Oedipus is approaching death.

What am I writing? I must gather my thoughts. I do not know whether anyone will read these pages besides you, my friend, who will receive them when I die. I will not address them to you alone, because it will comfort me to describe our friendship in ways that would be unnecessary if you were my only reader. I shall therefore tell my story as though writing for strangers. You have often asked why I live alone, why I weep, and above all why I maintain this impenetrable, unkind silence. During life I could not dare explain; in death I reveal the mystery. Others may turn these pages carelessly. To you, Woodville, my kind and loving friend, they will be precious: the treasured record of a brokenhearted girl who, even dying, still feels the warmth of gratitude toward you. Your tears will fall upon the words describing my misfortunes—I know they will—and while I am alive, I thank you for your compassion."}

{"request_id":"2b2a3ad9240b7e9fb7908719","rewritten_text":"When my tears had ceased, reason and recollection came back to me, and I began more calmly to consider what had occurred and what conduct now became me. Only a few hours had elapsed, yet an immense change had overtaken my existence—the ordinary labour of years had been accomplished since morning. My father was dead to me, and for an instant I felt as though he lay white-haired in his coffin, while I, my youth departed and age approaching, mourned his death at its appointed season. Yet this was false: I was still young, alas, much too young, and to others he was not dead. But I, most wretched, must never again look upon him or address him. I must flee him more eagerly than I would my bitterest enemy; neither in lonely places nor amid cities might I see him more. This thought stole my breath with anguish, and fastening upon my imagination prevented me for a time from pursuing any connected reflection. Henceforward, I thought, I would inhabit the bleakest solitude. I would withdraw to the Continent and take the veil, not from religious feeling, since I was no Catholic, but to be shut away from the world for ever. There I might find a solitude in which to weep, beyond the reach of living voices.

But my father—my dear and utterly miserable father? Would he perish? Could he never conquer the violent passion that now ruled him without pity? Might he not, after many, many years, when age had extinguished the burning feelings he suffered now, become my father once again? This thought smoothed my forehead, and I felt—and wept to feel—a smile, half sorrowful, loosen the expression of pain upon my lips. I ventured to entertain happier expectations of my future. Years must intervene, but hope would lend them wings and carry them swiftly by; or though they dragged, they would nevertheless pass, and my father was not lost to me eternally. Let him endure another sixteen years of lonely wandering. Let him pour out his wild lamentations once again to the boundless forests and mighty waterfalls of a foreign land. Let him face anew terrible perils and hardships that subdued the soul. Let the southern sun again scorch his passion-wasted cheeks, and cold nocturnal rains descend upon him and chill his blood.

To such a life, unhappy father, I condemn thee! Go! Spend thy days among savages and thy nights beneath the arch of heaven! Let thy limbs grow weary and thy heart grow cold, until all youth within thee is extinguished!"}

{"request_id":"300cf9679caf3e1cc8526049","rewritten_text":"At the top of the stairs he regained his composure and decided to let out his famous devilish laugh. He had found it extremely useful several times before. People said it had turned Lord Raker's wig grey overnight, and it had certainly made three of Lady Canterville's French governesses announce their resignations before their first month was over. So he gave his most horrible laugh, making the old vaulted ceiling echo again and again. But the terrifying sound had barely faded when a door opened and Mrs. Otis appeared in a pale blue dressing gown. 'I'm afraid you're feeling quite unwell,' she said, 'so I've brought a bottle of Dr. Dobell's medicine. If you have indigestion, it's an excellent treatment.' The ghost glared furiously at her and immediately prepared to turn into a large black dog. This was a skill for which he was rightly famous; the family doctor always blamed it for the permanent loss of reason suffered by Lord Canterville's uncle, the Hon. Thomas Horton. But approaching footsteps made him hesitate over his dreadful plan. Instead, he gave off a faint glow and disappeared with a deep, graveyard groan, just as the twins reached him.

Once in his room, he lost control completely and became terribly upset. The twins' bad manners and Mrs. Otis's stubbornly practical attitude were naturally very annoying. What truly distressed him, however, was his failure to wear the suit of armour. He had hoped even modern Americans would be stirred by the sight of a Ghost in Armour, if only out of respect for their national poet Longfellow. He himself had passed many a weary hour enjoying that poet's graceful, appealing verse while the Cantervilles were away in London. Besides, the armour belonged to him. He had worn it to great effect at the Kenilworth tournament, where the Virgin Queen herself had praised it highly. Yet when he tried putting it on now, the enormous breastplate and steel helmet proved far too heavy. He had crashed onto the stone floor, badly scraping both knees and bruising the knuckles of his right hand.

For several days afterward he was extremely ill. He barely left his room, except to keep the bloodstain in good condition. By taking excellent care of himself, however, he recovered and decided to make a third attempt to frighten the United States Minister and his family."}

{"request_id":"be5cec788f6ff286a074be29","rewritten_text":"He also had to consider Sybil's parents, who were fairly old-fashioned and might object to the marriage if there were even a hint of scandal. Still, he felt sure that if he explained all the facts, they would be the first to appreciate his motives. He therefore had every reason to choose poison. It was safe, reliable, and quiet, and removed any need for distressing scenes, which, like most Englishmen, he deeply disliked.

He knew absolutely nothing about the science of poisons, however. Since the waiter seemed unable to find anything in the library except Ruff's Guide and Bailey's Magazine, he searched the shelves himself. Eventually he discovered a handsomely bound Pharmacopoeia and a copy of Erskine's Toxicology, edited by Sir Mathew Reid, President of the Royal College of Physicians. Sir Mathew was also one of the Buckingham's longest-standing members, having been elected in mistake for someone else. This mishap so infuriated the Committee that when the intended candidate appeared, they unanimously voted against him. Lord Arthur found the technical vocabulary in both books quite bewildering and was beginning to regret neglecting his classical studies at Oxford when, in Erskine's second volume, he came upon a fascinating, detailed account of aconitine's properties, written in reasonably clear English. It struck him as precisely the poison he needed. Its action was rapid—indeed, almost instantaneous—and entirely painless. Taken in a gelatin capsule, as Sir Mathew advised, it was not at all unpleasant to swallow. He therefore noted on his shirt cuff the quantity required for a fatal dose, returned the books to their shelves, and strolled up St. James's Street to the distinguished chemists Pestle and Humbey. Mr. Pestle, who always served aristocratic customers himself, was considerably startled by the order and murmured with great deference that a medical certificate was required. But Lord Arthur explained that he needed it for a large Norwegian mastiff he was obliged to dispose of: the animal was showing early symptoms of rabies and had already bitten the coachman twice on the calf. Mr. Pestle then declared himself entirely satisfied, praised Lord Arthur's remarkable knowledge of toxicology, and had the prescription prepared at once.

Lord Arthur placed the capsule in a lovely little silver sweet box he spotted in a Bond Street shop window, discarded Pestle and Hambey's unattractive pillbox, and immediately drove to Lady Clementina's."}

{"request_id":"d93fcc96fd37deda5f5e79ae","rewritten_text":"He had, moreover, to consider Sybil's father and mother, who were somewhat old-fashioned, and might well oppose the marriage if anything resembling a scandal arose; though he was convinced that, once acquainted with all the circumstances, they would be the first to understand the motives by which he had been governed. Everything, then, favoured his choice of poison. It was safe, certain, and noiseless, and dispensed with the need for those painful scenes to which he, in common with most Englishmen, entertained a deep objection.

Of the science of poisons, nevertheless, he was entirely ignorant, and since the waiter appeared incapable of discovering anything in the library beyond Ruff's Guide and Bailey's Magazine, he searched the shelves for himself. At last he found a richly bound edition of the Pharmacopoeia and a volume of Erskine's Toxicology, edited by Sir Mathew Reid, President of the Royal College of Physicians and one of the Buckingham's oldest members. He had been elected under the impression that he was somebody else—a contretemps which so angered the Committee that, when the proper gentleman presented himself, they unanimously black-balled him. Lord Arthur was considerably perplexed by the technical expressions in both works, and was beginning to wish he had attended more closely to his classics at Oxford, when the second volume of Erskine supplied a most interesting and thorough description of the properties of aconitine in tolerably plain English. Here, he thought, was the very poison he required. Its effect was swift—almost instantaneous, indeed—wholly painless, and, administered in a gelatine capsule, the method Sir Mathew recommended, it was by no means disagreeable to the taste. Accordingly, he wrote upon his shirt-cuff the quantity needed for a fatal dose, restored the books to their places, and sauntered up St. James's Street to the eminent chemists, Pestle and Humbey's. Mr. Pestle, who invariably served the aristocracy in person, was much astonished by his order, and murmured, with the greatest respect, something concerning the necessity of a medical certificate. Once Lord Arthur explained, however, that it was intended for a large Norwegian mastiff which he was compelled to destroy, as it exhibited the first signs of rabies and had twice bitten the coachman in the calf, he professed himself completely satisfied, congratulated Lord Arthur upon his admirable knowledge of Toxicology, and ordered the prescription to be made up without delay.

Lord Arthur transferred the capsule to a charming little silver bonbonnière he noticed in a Bond Street shop-window, discarded Pestle and Hambey's hideous pill-box, and drove straight to Lady Clementina's."}

{"request_id":"88df7a0f0bf7ebe5f1898619","rewritten_text":"As I put my hand through the valve with a cup of water for the old cat, my shirt-sleeves caught in the loop supporting the basket, and in an instant detached it from the bottom. Had the entire thing dissolved into air, it could not have disappeared from my view more suddenly or completely. Indeed, not a tenth of a second could have elapsed between the release of the basket and its utter disappearance, together with everything it held. I sent my good wishes after it towards the earth, but naturally entertained no hope that either the cat or her kittens would survive to relate their unhappy adventure.

“At six o'clock I noticed that a large part of the earth's visible surface to the east lay beneath a dense shadow. This moved forward with remarkable speed, until, at five minutes to seven, night covered the whole expanse before me. Yet the light of the setting sun continued to shine upon the balloon until long afterwards; and although I had, of course, expected this, the circumstance afforded me a very great pleasure. Plainly, in the morning I should see the sun rise at least several hours before the inhabitants of Rotterdam, despite their position so much farther east. Thus, with every day, and in proportion to my increasing elevation, I should enjoy sunlight for a longer and longer interval. I resolved now to maintain a journal of my voyage, counting each day continuously from one hour to twenty-four, without regard to periods of darkness.

“At ten o'clock, growing drowsy, I decided to lie down for the remainder of the night. Here, however, a difficulty arose which, obvious though it may seem, had entirely escaped me until the moment I describe. If I slept as intended, how was the air within the chamber to be renewed meanwhile? It would be impossible to breathe it for longer than an hour at most; and even if that time could be stretched to an hour and a quarter, the most disastrous effects might follow. This predicament occasioned me considerable uneasiness. It will scarcely be credited that, after all the dangers I had already encountered, I regarded this problem so gravely as to abandon every hope of achieving my ultimate purpose, and at last to conclude that I must descend. My uncertainty, however, endured only for a moment."}

{"request_id":"758a7f7774bad95e596a14d6","rewritten_text":"“Oh, it is nothing at all! It gives me pleasure. I do not labour for money; my art is everything to me.”

Lord Arthur placed £4, 2s. 6d. upon the table, thanked the little German for his obliging assistance, and, after successfully refusing an invitation to meet several Anarchists at a meat-tea the next Saturday, left the house and made his way to the Park.

During the following two days his excitement was extreme, and at noon on Friday he drove to the Buckingham to await intelligence. Throughout the afternoon the impassive hall-porter posted telegrams from different districts of the country, announcing racing results, decisions in divorce cases, the condition of the weather, and similar matters. Meanwhile the tape delivered tiresome particulars of an all-night sitting of the House of Commons and a minor panic upon the Stock Exchange. At four the evening newspapers arrived, and Lord Arthur retired into the library carrying the Pall Mall, the St. James's, the Globe, and the Echo. This greatly enraged Colonel Goodchild, who wished to see the accounts of a speech he had made that morning at the Mansion House concerning South African Missions and the desirability of appointing black Bishops in every province, and who, for some unexplained reason, cherished a decided prejudice against the Evening News. Not one of the papers, however, made the faintest reference to Chichester, and Lord Arthur concluded that his attempt had miscarried. The disappointment was dreadful, and for a while quite robbed him of his composure. Herr Winckelkopf, whom he visited on the following day, was profuse in his apologies, offering either a second clock without charge or a case of nitro-glycerine bombs at cost. Lord Arthur, however, had ceased to trust explosives altogether, and Herr Winckelkopf himself conceded that adulteration was now so universal that one could scarcely obtain even pure dynamite. Yet the little German, though admitting that the mechanism must have suffered some fault, still entertained hopes of the clock exploding. He cited a barometer he had once dispatched to the military Governor of Odessa, which had been set to explode after ten days but had waited nearly three months. True, when it finally went off it achieved no more than blowing a housemaid to pieces, the Governor having left town six weeks earlier; but it at least demonstrated that dynamite, when directed by machinery, remained a potent destructive agent, if a somewhat tardy one."}

{"request_id":"3305cb0453d61731adeab860","rewritten_text":"You see, I'd stored my handkerchief inside my helmet, along with a few other things. But this was the kind of helmet you couldn't remove without help. That hadn't crossed my mind when I put it there; in fact, I hadn't known. I'd thought it would be a wonderfully convenient place. So knowing it was right there, so close and handy but impossible to reach, only made the misery worse. The thing you can't have is generally the thing you want most; everyone has noticed that. It drove every other thought from my head and fixed my attention entirely on the helmet. Mile after mile, my mind stayed there, imagining that handkerchief, seeing it before me. Meanwhile salty sweat kept running into my eyes, and I couldn't get at the thing. It was maddening. Written down, it sounds like a small problem, but it wasn't small at all. It was real, thorough misery, and I wouldn't say so if it weren't true. I decided that next time I'd bring a little handbag, however it looked and whatever anybody said. Of course those iron-clad fashion plates of the Round Table would consider it disgraceful and perhaps raise hell about it. But I wanted comfort first and style afterward. We kept jogging along, sometimes crossing dusty stretches where clouds rose around us. The dust filled my nose and made me sneeze and weep, and naturally I said things I shouldn't have. I don't deny it. I'm no better than anyone else.

We couldn't seem to find a soul in this deserted Britain, not even an ogre. Given my mood, that was lucky for the ogre—especially if he carried a handkerchief. Most knights would have cared only about taking his armour. I'd have let him keep all his metalwork if I could just have his bandanna.

Meanwhile, it grew hotter and hotter inside my suit. The sun kept beating down, heating the iron more with every minute. When you're that hot, every little thing gets on your nerves. At a trot I rattled like a box of crockery, which annoyed me. And I could hardly bear the shield flapping and banging, first against my chest and then around my back. If I slowed to a walk, the joints of the armour squeaked and screeched like an irritating wheelbarrow. At that speed we stirred no breeze, so I felt about to fry inside my stove. Besides, the more slowly we moved, the heavier the iron settled upon me, until every minute I seemed to weigh several more tons."}

{"request_id":"2008ffa4a6a07ecc89b75698","rewritten_text":"I cannot describe the joy I felt in turning away from political quarrels in my country and bodily suffering abroad to my own beloved home. Here was a place devoted to goodness and love, to peace and the sharing of our deepest feelings. Had I never left Windsor, I would not have felt this so strongly. But in Greece I had suffered fear and terrible upheaval. After a time of anxiety and sorrow there, I had lost two people whose very names represented greatness and virtue. Surely such troubles could never invade the family circle still left to me, while we lived quietly, sheltered in our dear forest. The passing years did bring a few changes, of course. Time, as always, left reminders of death upon our pleasures and hopes. Idris, the most affectionate wife, sister, and friend, was also a tender, loving mother. Motherhood was no pastime to her, as it is to many; it was a passion. We had had three children. The second had died while I was in Greece. This loss had mixed her triumphant joy in motherhood with grief and fear. Before it happened, the little creatures born from her, who would carry on her brief life, had seemed certain to live. Now she feared that merciless death might take her remaining darlings as it had taken their brother. The smallest illness terrified her. Any separation from them made her miserable. She had placed all her happiness in their fragile lives and watched constantly, afraid that the stealthy thief would again steal her precious treasures. Fortunately, she had little reason to worry. Alfred, now nine, was an honest, brave little boy with a bright face, gentle eyes, and a nature both kind and independent. Our youngest was still a baby, but his soft cheeks glowed with health, and his tireless energy filled our home with innocent laughter.

Clara had grown beyond the age whose inability to understand or speak caused Idris such anxiety. She was dear to Idris and to everyone. Intelligence blended in her with innocence, sensitivity with patience, and seriousness with unfailing good humor. Her extraordinary beauty came with such lovable simplicity that she seemed a pearl among our treasured possessions, wonderful and precious.

At the start of winter, our nine-year-old Alfred went to Eton for the first time. He saw this as his first step toward manhood and was delighted. Studying and playing with other boys brought out his finest qualities: steady determination, generosity, and firmness tempered by self-control."}

{"request_id":"88c1a7db665d6089b613419e","rewritten_text":"The stars still shone brightly when I woke, and Taurus high in the southern sky told me it was midnight. My dreams had been troubled. I imagined myself invited to Timon’s final feast. I arrived hungry; the dishes were uncovered, and hot water released its uninviting steam. I fled the host’s anger, but the host had become Raymond. To my feverish imagination, the vessels he hurled after me overflowed with foul vapor, while my friend’s body twisted into a thousand forms and swelled into an enormous apparition, the mark of plague upon its forehead. The growing shadow rose higher and higher, filling the unbreakable dome that arched above the world, supporting and enclosing it, and then seeming to strain beyond it. The nightmare became torture. With a fierce effort I shook off sleep and restored my mind to its ordinary workings. My first thought was of Perdita. I must return to her and sustain her, finding whatever nourishment despair could offer her wounded heart. Through the stern demands of duty and the gentle tenderness of remembrance, I must call her back from grief’s wild extremes.

The stars were my only guide. I turned away from the dreadful ruins of the Golden City and, after great exertion, managed to escape its boundaries. Outside the walls I met a company of soldiers, borrowed a horse from one of them, and hurried to my sister. In this short time the plain had changed. The camp had been dismantled, and scattered remnants of the disbanded army gathered in small groups. Every face was troubled; every movement expressed shock and dismay.

I entered the palace with a heavy heart and stood afraid to move, speak, or look. Perdita sat in the middle of the hall on the marble floor, her head bowed against her chest, her hair disordered, her fingers twisting restlessly together. She was as pale as the marble, and anguish tightened every feature. She noticed me and looked up questioningly. The faint hope in her glance was agony to see. The words died before I could speak them, and I felt a ghastly smile crease my lips. She understood my expression. Her head dropped again, and her fingers resumed their restless movements. At last I found my voice, but its sound terrified her. The unhappy girl had read my face; nothing could make her willing to hear her terrible misfortune given shape and confirmed in hard, irreversible words. She even seemed eager to turn my thoughts away from it. Rising from the floor, she whispered, “Hush! Clara has cried so much, and now she is sleeping. We must not wake her.”"}

{"request_id":"85c7ee189aac309795d6a5e4","rewritten_text":"During his brief visit, Emma had hardly seen him. Still, she had seen enough to get their first meeting over with and to feel that the mixture of wounded pride and self-importance in his manner did him no favors. In fact, she was beginning to wonder how she had ever found him pleasant. Seeing him brought up so many uncomfortable feelings that she would have welcomed the promise of never meeting him again, except for the moral benefit of the experience: a penance, a lesson, a useful blow to her pride. She wished him well, but he caused her pain. Knowing he was happy twenty miles away would have pleased her most.

His marriage, however, would certainly make his continued presence in Highbury less painful. It would remove many pointless worries and ease many awkward situations. A Mrs. Elton would provide a reason for any change in their relations. Their former closeness could fade without comment. They could almost begin their polite acquaintance over again.

Emma gave little thought to the woman herself. No doubt she was good enough for Mr. Elton, educated and talented enough for Highbury, and pretty enough—though probably plain beside Harriet. As for her family connections, Emma felt completely satisfied. Despite all his boasts about his own importance and his contempt for Harriet, she was certain he had gained nothing there. The facts on that subject seemed possible to discover. What sort of woman she was remained uncertain, but her family background could be investigated. Apart from her ten thousand pounds, she did not seem superior to Harriet at all. She brought no famous name, distinguished ancestry, or important relations. Miss Hawkins was the younger of two daughters of a Bristol man whom one must, of course, call a merchant. Yet the modest total earnings of his business life made it reasonable to suppose his trade had been equally modest in status. She usually spent part of each winter in Bath, but Bristol was her home—the very center of Bristol. Her parents had died some years earlier, yet an uncle remained, employed “in the law.” No one ventured any more respectable description of his work than that. She had lived with him. Emma guessed he did menial work for an attorney and lacked the intelligence to advance. All the family’s grandeur seemed to rest on the elder sister, who had married very well: a prosperous gentleman near Bristol who owned two carriages! That completed the story. That was the great glory of Miss Hawkins."}

{"request_id":"53f16f24e33bd5a4c3306204","rewritten_text":"A great many American ladies, upon quitting their own country, assume a look of habitual invalidism, believing it to mark European elegance; Mrs. Otis, however, had never made this mistake. Her constitution was superb, and her stock of high spirits truly extraordinary. Indeed, she was thoroughly English in several respects, admirably illustrating how much we now share with America in everything save, naturally, our language. Her eldest boy, named Washington during a patriotic moment which his parents bestowed upon him and he never ceased regretting, was a blond, fairly handsome young man. Three consecutive seasons of leading the German at the Newport Casino had prepared him for American diplomacy, and even London knew him as a first-rate dancer. His only frailties were gardenias and the aristocracy; in other matters he was remarkably sensible. Miss Virginia E. Otis, a small girl of fifteen, was graceful and lovely as a young deer, with a splendid independence in her great blue eyes. An admirable horsewoman, she had once ridden her pony twice around the park against old Lord Bilton, beating him by a length and a half just before the Achilles statue. This so delighted the young Duke of Cheshire that he offered marriage immediately, whereupon his guardians sent him back to Eton that same evening, weeping copiously. After Virginia came the twins, generally styled 'The Stars and Stripes,' because they were so frequently caned. They were charming boys and, apart from the worthy Minister, the family's sole genuine republicans.

Canterville Chase stood seven miles from Ascot, its nearest railway station, so Mr. Otis had wired for a waggonette to collect them. They began the drive in excellent spirits. The July evening was beautiful, the air delicately scented by pine woods. Occasionally they heard a wood pigeon lingering over its own melodious notes, or glimpsed a pheasant's gleaming breast among the whispering ferns. Little squirrels watched their passage from the beeches, while rabbits darted through the undergrowth and across moss-covered hillocks, white tails raised. On their entering the avenue to Canterville Chase, however, clouds abruptly spread across the sky. An odd hush settled upon the air, a great flock of rooks flew soundlessly overhead, and several heavy raindrops had fallen before they reached the house.

An elderly woman waited upon the steps to welcome them, neatly attired in black silk with a white apron and cap."}

{"request_id":"3a9f4a78ce79710c6a77811d","rewritten_text":"I cannot understand why she ever wanted to be close to me. Still, if it all happened again, I am sure I would be taken in again. Caroline did not return my visit until yesterday, and in all that time I received not a single note or line from her. When she finally came, she clearly took no pleasure in it. She offered a brief, formal apology for not visiting sooner, said nothing about wanting to see me again, and seemed so completely changed that by the time she left, I had firmly decided to end the friendship. I feel sorry for her, although I cannot help blaming her too. She was wrong to single me out as she did. I can honestly say that every step toward friendship began with her. But I pity her because she must know she has behaved badly, and because I am sure concern for her brother explains it. I need not say more. We know she has nothing to worry about, but if she does worry, that would easily explain her treatment of me. Her brother deserves her affection, and any concern she feels for him is natural and kind. Even so, I wonder why she still has these fears. If he had cared for me at all, we would have met long ago. Something she said convinced me that he knows I am in town. Yet the way she spoke suggested that she was trying to convince herself he really prefers Miss Darcy. I cannot understand it. If I were not afraid of judging too harshly, I would almost say that the whole affair looks very dishonest. I will try to put every painful thought aside and think only of what makes me happy: your love, and our dear uncle and aunt's constant kindness. Please write very soon. Miss Bingley mentioned that her brother might never return to Netherfield and might give up the house, though she sounded uncertain. We had better say nothing about it. I am so glad you have had such pleasant news from our friends at Hunsford. Do go and visit them with Sir William and Maria. I am sure you will be very comfortable there.

"Yours, etc."

The letter distressed Elizabeth, but she felt better when she reflected that at least the sister would no longer deceive Jane. By now, all hope connected with the brother had completely ended."}

{"request_id":"f19c53451483e81be3ad520a","rewritten_text":"Mrs. Musgrove took the first chance to speak to Anne alone. “Oh, Miss Anne, I do wish Mrs. Charles had some of your skill with those children. They behave like entirely different creatures with you! But normally they're so spoiled! It's a shame you can't teach your sister how to manage them. They're as handsome and healthy as any children could be, the poor little dears—and I say that without favoritism. But Mrs. Charles has no idea how to deal with them! Good heavens, they can be such a nuisance. I assure you, Miss Anne, it keeps me from wanting them at our house as often as I otherwise would. I think Mrs. Charles is rather displeased that I don't invite them more, but it's very trying to have children around whom you must constantly correct: ‘Don't do this,’ and ‘Don't do that.’ Or else you can only keep them reasonably well behaved by giving them more cake than is good for them.”

Anne also heard this from Mary: “Mrs. Musgrove considers all her servants so dependable that questioning it would amount to high treason. But I'm certain, without exaggerating, that her senior housemaid and laundry maid spend all day wandering about the village instead of working. I meet them everywhere, and I declare I never visit my nursery twice without seeing one of them. If Jemima weren't the most trustworthy, reliable creature alive, they'd be enough to ruin her. She tells me they're always trying to persuade her to go walking with them.” Mrs. Musgrove, meanwhile, said, “I make it a rule never to interfere in my daughter-in-law's affairs, because I know that wouldn't do. But I'll tell you, Miss Anne, since you may be able to put things right, that I don't think much of Mrs. Charles's nursery maid. I hear strange stories about her. She's forever running about, and I know myself that she dresses so extravagantly she could spoil any servant she comes near. Mrs. Charles has complete faith in her, I know. But I give you this warning so you can keep an eye out. If you notice anything wrong, you needn't hesitate to mention it.”

Mary also complained that when they dined at the Great House with other families, Mrs. Musgrove often failed to give her the precedence her rank deserved. She saw no reason why being treated as one of the household should deprive her of her proper place."}

{"request_id":"3bebefa2df85f7095cfa5b9e","rewritten_text":"Four days after these remarkable events, a funeral procession left Canterville Chase at about eleven at night. Eight black horses drew the hearse, each bearing upon its head a great cluster of swaying ostrich feathers. Over the leaden coffin lay a sumptuous purple pall, embroidered in gold with the Canterville arms. Servants carrying flaming torches walked beside the hearse and carriages, and the entire procession made a wonderfully solemn spectacle. Lord Canterville, who had travelled specially from Wales for the funeral, acted as chief mourner and shared the first carriage with little Virginia. The United States Minister and his wife followed; after them came Washington and the three boys, with Mrs. Umney occupying the last carriage. Everyone agreed that, after being terrified by the ghost for over fifty years, she was entitled to witness the end of him. A deep grave had been prepared in a corner of the churchyard, directly beneath the ancient yew, and the Reverend Augustus Dampier delivered the service with the greatest solemnity. At the close of the ceremony, the servants put out their torches in accordance with a long-standing Canterville custom. While the coffin was lowered, Virginia came forward and placed upon it a large cross of pink and white almond blossoms. At that instant the moon emerged from behind a cloud and bathed the little churchyard in its quiet silver light, while a nightingale began singing in a distant thicket. Remembering the ghost's account of the Garden of Death, she felt tears cloud her eyes and said scarcely anything on the journey home.

Next morning, before Lord Canterville departed for town, Mr. Otis spoke with him about the jewels the ghost had bestowed upon Virginia. They were extraordinarily splendid, particularly a ruby necklace in an old Venetian setting, a truly exquisite example of sixteenth-century craftsmanship. Their immense value made Mr. Otis seriously hesitate to permit his daughter to keep them.

“My lord,” he said, “I understand that in your country mortmain extends to ornaments as well as to land, and it seems perfectly evident that these jewels are, or ought to be, heirlooms of your family. I must therefore ask you to carry them to London, regarding them merely as part of your own property returned to you in somewhat unusual circumstances. My daughter, after all, is only a child, and I am pleased to say that she has so far shown little concern for such accessories of idle extravagance."}

{"request_id":"361d5e42a0e27225002f9cac","rewritten_text":"I then drilled a hole in the end of the keg above the pitcher and fitted it with a soft wooden plug shaped like a cone. By pushing the plug farther in or pulling it out, I adjusted its tightness. After a few attempts, I found the exact position that let water drip into the pitcher below at a rate that would fill it to the brim in sixty minutes. This was quick and easy to work out: I only had to note how much of the pitcher filled in a certain time. With that arranged, the rest of my plan was clear. I set up my bed on the basket floor so that, when I lay down, my head was directly beneath the pitcher's spout. At the end of an hour, the full pitcher would overflow through that spout, which was slightly lower than its rim. The water would fall more than four feet onto my face. Clearly, this would wake me immediately, even from the deepest sleep imaginable.

“It was eleven by the time I finished these preparations. I went straight to bed, completely confident that my invention would work. I was not disappointed. Every sixty minutes, exactly on time, my faithful clock woke me. I emptied the pitcher back through the hole at the top of the keg, attended to the condenser, and returned to bed. These repeated breaks in my sleep bothered me even less than I had expected. When I finally got up for the day, it was seven o'clock, and the sun stood many degrees above my horizon.

“April 3. I found that the balloon had reached a truly enormous height, and the earth's curve looked much more pronounced. Below me, a group of black dots lay in the ocean. They were undoubtedly islands. Far to the north, at the edge of the horizon, I could see a thin, dazzling white streak. I had no doubt that this was the southern edge of the ice covering the Polar Sea. The sight stirred my curiosity greatly. I hoped to travel much farther north and thought I might eventually find myself directly over the Pole. I regretted that, if this happened, my great height would prevent me from examining it as closely and accurately as I wished."}

{"request_id":"190c172b05c294b345ef802e","rewritten_text":"You will be glad to hear that the undertaking you feared so much has begun without disaster. I arrived here yesterday, and my first duty is to reassure my dear sister that I am well and increasingly confident of success.

I am already far north of London. Walking through the streets of Petersburgh, I feel a cold northern breeze on my cheeks, strengthening me and filling me with delight. Can you understand that feeling? The wind has come from the very regions I am heading toward, giving me a taste of their icy weather. Encouraged by this promising breeze, my daydreams grow more vivid and intense. I try without success to believe that the pole is a place of ice and emptiness. In my imagination, it always appears beautiful and delightful. There, Margaret, the sun never disappears: its broad circle moves along the horizon and fills the world with constant light. There—if you will allow me to trust earlier explorers—snow and frost are absent. A peaceful sea may carry us to a land more wonderful and beautiful than any yet discovered in the inhabited world. Its natural life and landscape may be unlike anything known, just as the movements of heavenly bodies surely are in those unexplored spaces. What might we not find in a country of endless daylight? I may discover the mysterious force that draws the compass needle. I may also explain a thousand observations of the heavens whose apparent irregularities need only this voyage to become clear and consistent forever. I will satisfy my eager curiosity by seeing a part of the world nobody has visited and perhaps walking on land no human foot has ever touched. These hopes attract me strongly enough to overcome every fear of danger or death. They send me on this difficult voyage with the same joy a child feels setting off in a little boat with holiday friends to explore the river where he lives. Even if all my guesses prove false, you cannot deny the enormous benefit I would bring humanity, through all future generations, by discovering a route near the pole to countries that now take months to reach. Or I may discover the secret of magnetism, which, if it can be discovered at all, requires an expedition like mine.

Thinking of these things has calmed the agitation with which I began this letter. My heart now glows with an enthusiasm that seems to lift me to heaven. Nothing brings the mind so much peace as a firm purpose, a single point on which the soul can focus its thoughts."}

{"request_id":"e89645b4391d74b1e2094f2d","rewritten_text":"What kind of life could they have together while Fate still held this dreadful outcome in the balance? The wedding must be delayed, whatever the cost. On that point he was completely determined. He loved the girl passionately; when they sat together, the slightest touch of her fingers sent exquisite pleasure through every nerve. Even so, he understood his duty clearly and knew that he had no right to marry before committing the murder. Once it was done, he could stand at the altar beside Sybil Merton and place his life in her hands without any fear of wrongdoing. Once it was done, he could hold her in his arms, certain that she would never need to blush for him or lower her head in shame. But it had to be done first, and the sooner it happened, the better for them both.

Many men in his situation would have chosen the flower-strewn path of pleasure over the steep ascent of duty. Lord Arthur, however, was too conscientious to put enjoyment before principle. His love contained more than simple passion: to him, Sybil represented everything good and noble. For a moment he felt a natural revulsion at what he was required to do, but it soon disappeared. His heart assured him that this was a sacrifice, not a sin, while his reason reminded him that there was no alternative. He had to choose between living for himself and living for others. However terrible the task undoubtedly was, he knew that he must not allow selfishness to overcome love. Sooner or later, all of us face the same decision; the same question is put to everyone. Lord Arthur faced it early, before the calculating cynicism of middle age could spoil his character or the shallow, fashionable egotism of our time corrode his heart. He therefore felt no hesitation about fulfilling his duty. Fortunately, he was also no mere dreamer or idle amateur. If he had been, he would have hesitated like Hamlet and allowed indecision to undermine his purpose. Instead he was fundamentally practical. For him, life meant action rather than reflection. He possessed that rarest of qualities: common sense.

By now, the wild, confused emotions of the previous night had entirely subsided. He looked back almost with embarrassment on his frantic wanderings through the streets and his fierce emotional suffering. The very sincerity of that suffering made it seem unreal to him now. He wondered how he could have been foolish enough to rage and complain about something inevitable."}

{"request_id":"59f27d56785b6bf4b36d5686","rewritten_text":"My father had been the life within my life, and now his memory held that place. I could feel gratitude toward someone else, but I could never again love or hope as I once had. Everything was suffering; even pleasure was something I endured rather than enjoyed. I resembled a lonely hollow in the mountains, enclosed on every side by steep black cliffs, beyond the reach of warmth and without a path to sunnier fields. That was why the spirit of friendship could soothe me briefly but could not restore me. It arrived like a gentle visitation, and when it left I scarcely felt its absence. The spirit of life was dead inside me. Do not be surprised, then, that I welcomed heaven's finest gift—a friend—with so little joy, or mourned its departure with so little bitterness.

My friend's name was Woodville. I will briefly tell his story, so that you may understand how cold my heart must have grown to resist the warmth of his eloquence and tender sympathy. Since he too was profoundly unhappy, we might have consoled each other well, had the Medusa head of Misery not turned me to stone. Woodville's misfortunes had not struck at the heart's very core as mine had. His grief was natural, capable of purifying rather than destroying the heart. Once its shadow lifted, he might emerge brighter and happier than before.

Woodville was the son of a poor clergyman and had received a classical education. He belonged to that rare handful whom fortune favours from birth, showering them without restraint with gifts of mind and body, and guarding them so closely that no imperfection, however slight, or disappointment, however brief, is allowed to touch them. She seemed to have made his mind from a substance so excellent that no impurity could stain it, and his understanding so sound that no error could distort it. His genius was extraordinary. When it rose like a brilliant star in the east, every eye turned toward it in admiration. He was a poet, though that name has been so often degraded that it cannot express everything he was. He resembled one of the ancient poets, crowned by the Muses in his cradle, with bees feeding upon his lips. Among other men he seemed surrounded by a heavenly radiance that set him apart and raised him above them. His exceptional beauty, the dazzling fire in his eyes, and the rich tones of his speech, which held listeners silent in ecstatic wonder, made him surpass everyone else. Before him, other people seemed created only to serve his superior excellence."}

{"request_id":"c4b26a1f6e47891cd77eb2a3","rewritten_text":"I thought this peculiar lie had been told with remarkable simplicity and beauty. But I had heard it only once, which makes a difference. No doubt the others had enjoyed it when it was new.

Sir Dinadan the Humorist woke first and soon stirred everyone else with a thoroughly poor practical joke. He tied metal mugs to a dog’s tail and let him loose. The terrified animal raced round and round, with every other dog barking in pursuit. They crashed into whatever stood in their way, producing utter confusion and a deafening racket. Every man and woman present laughed until tears ran down their faces; some tumbled from their chairs and rolled on the floor in delight. They were like a crowd of children. Sir Dinadan was so pleased with himself that he kept explaining, to the point of exhaustion, how this immortal idea had come to him. Like others of his brand of comedian, he was still laughing long after everyone else had finished. His success inflated him enough to attempt a speech—a funny one, naturally. I don't think I have ever heard so many worn-out jokes strung together. He was worse than the minstrels, worse than a circus clown. There was something especially depressing about sitting thirteen hundred years before my birth and hearing those same feeble, flat, moth-eaten jokes that had made me sick as a boy thirteen hundred years later. I nearly became convinced that a genuinely new joke was impossible. Everyone laughed at these ancient relics—but people always do. I had noticed that centuries afterward. The scoffer, of course, did not laugh; I mean the boy. He scoffed instead. Nothing was safe from his scorn. He said most of Sir Dinadan’s jokes were rotten and the rest fossilized. I told him “fossilized” was good, since I too believed that only geological periods could do justice to the impressive age of some of them. But this neat idea found no foothold in his mind: geology had yet to be invented. Still, I made a note of the remark and planned to educate the nation enough to appreciate it, assuming I survived. No point throwing away a good thing just because the market is not ready.

Then Sir Kay rose and started up his storytelling machine, using me as fuel."}

{"request_id":"07ee9fbdbb0023cf504cecff","rewritten_text":"“By eight o’clock I had actually risen seventeen miles above the earth. It therefore seemed clear that my speed was increasing and that some acceleration would have been noticeable even without the ballast I had thrown out. The pain in my head and ears returned in violent bursts, and my nose still bled occasionally. Overall, though, I suffered much less than one might have expected. Breathing, on the other hand, grew harder every moment, and each inhalation brought an uncomfortable spasm in my chest. I unpacked the equipment for condensing air and prepared it for immediate use.

“At this stage of the ascent, the earth looked truly beautiful. To the west, north, and south, as far as I could see, stretched an endless sheet of apparently calm ocean. Its blue deepened moment by moment, and its surface was already beginning to look slightly curved. Far to the east, yet clearly visible, lay the islands of Great Britain, the entire Atlantic coastlines of France and Spain, and a small part of northern Africa. No individual building could be made out. Humanity’s proudest cities had vanished completely from the face of the earth. From Gibraltar, now reduced to a faint speck, the dark Mediterranean extended eastward to the limit of my sight, its bright islands scattered like stars across the sky. Eventually its whole expanse seemed to plunge over the abyss at the horizon, and I found myself standing on tiptoe, listening for echoes of that enormous waterfall. Above me, the sky was jet black and the stars shone brilliantly.

“The pigeons now appeared to be suffering badly, so I decided to release them. First I untied a beautiful bird mottled with gray and set him on the edge of the wicker basket. He seemed extremely distressed, looking anxiously around, fluttering his wings, and cooing loudly, but nothing could persuade him to leave the basket. Finally, I picked him up and tossed him about six yards away from the balloon. Instead of descending as I expected, he struggled fiercely to return, uttering shrill, piercing cries. At last he regained his former perch on the rim. Almost immediately, however, his head fell against his breast, and he dropped dead into the basket."}

{"request_id":"426de81b9f4ab5526a782613","rewritten_text":"“Each compares it to a speaker of a language he does not know, rather than one he understands. The Frenchman thinks the voice was Spanish and says he ‘might have picked out words if he knew Spanish.’ The Dutchman insists it was French, but the report tells us that ‘the witness does not understand French and was questioned through an interpreter.’ The Englishman believes it was German, though he ‘does not understand German.’ The Spaniard is ‘certain’ it was an Englishman, but bases this entirely on the sound, ‘since he knows no English.’ The Italian thinks it was a Russian, yet ‘has never spoken with anyone from Russia.’ A second Frenchman disagrees with the first and is sure the speaker was Italian; but he too does not know that language and, like the Spaniard, relies on intonation. Consider how extraordinarily unfamiliar that voice must have been to produce testimony like this! Even its tones sounded recognizable to none of these people from Europe’s five great divisions. You may suggest it belonged to someone from Asia or Africa. Neither Asians nor Africans are numerous in Paris, but without rejecting your suggestion, let me draw attention to three points. One witness describes the voice as ‘harsh rather than high-pitched.’ Two others call it ‘rapid and irregular.’ Not one witness reports distinguishing any words, or even sounds that resembled words.

“I do not know,” Dupin continued, “what impression these observations have made on you so far. But I can confidently say that sound deductions from this testimony alone—the evidence about the gruff and high-pitched voices—are enough to raise a suspicion that should guide every further step of the investigation. ‘Sound deductions’ does not quite convey my meaning. I mean they are the only correct conclusions, and that this suspicion follows inevitably from them as the sole possible result. I will not yet tell you what that suspicion is. For now, simply remember that it was strong enough to give my examination of the room a definite shape and direction.

“Let us imagine ourselves back in that room. What should we look for first? The route by which the murderers escaped. I think I can safely say that neither of us believes in supernatural events."}

{"request_id":"89dfa1944a79bd336fd1ca68","rewritten_text":"All the young people were eager to visit Lyme. Captain Wentworth spoke of going back there himself. It was only seventeen miles from Uppercross, and although it was November, the weather was quite good. Louisa, the most eager of them all, had made up her mind to go. Besides enjoying getting her own way, she now believed there was virtue in sticking to a decision. She overcame all her parents' wishes to delay the visit until summer. So Lyme it would be, with Charles, Mary, Anne, Henrietta, Louisa, and Captain Wentworth making the trip.

Their first careless plan was to leave in the morning and return that night. But Mr. Musgrove would not agree, out of concern for his horses. Once they thought it through sensibly, they realized that a mid-November day would leave little time to explore a new place. Given the countryside they had to cross, the journey there and back would take seven hours. They therefore decided to stay overnight and return in time for dinner the next day. Everyone felt this was a considerable improvement. They met for an unusually early breakfast at the Great House and set off exactly on time. Even so, it was well past noon when the two carriages descended the long hill into Lyme and entered the town's even steeper main street. The four ladies traveled in Mr. Musgrove's coach, while Charles drove Captain Wentworth in his two-wheeled carriage. Clearly, they would have only enough time to look around before the day's light and warmth disappeared.

Once they had arranged rooms and ordered dinner at an inn, there could be no doubt about what to do next: walk straight down to the sea. They had arrived too late in the year to enjoy the entertainments and variety Lyme offered as a resort. The assembly rooms were closed, almost all the visitors had left their lodgings, and scarcely any families remained apart from permanent residents. The buildings themselves have little to admire. Instead, a visitor looks at the town's striking position, with its main street seeming almost to rush into the water; the walk to the Cobb around the pleasant little bay, crowded in the season with people and wheeled bathing huts; and the Cobb itself, with its old marvels and recent improvements. There is also the beautiful line of cliffs stretching eastward from the town. A visitor would be strange indeed who found nothing attractive in Lyme's immediate surroundings to make them want to become better acquainted with it."}

{"request_id":"9d02f74c8ccb30ddf362d1ae","rewritten_text":"Perdita went unnamed, yet I could scarcely doubt that his love for her explained his wavering intentions. And who merited love more than my generous-minded sister? Who better deserved the hand of this self-appointed king than she whose gaze might have belonged to the sovereign of nations? She loved him as he loved her, though disappointment had subdued her passion, while ambition fiercely contested his.

That evening we went together to the House. Although Raymond knew the approaching debate would examine and determine his projects and prospects, he remained cheerful and unconcerned. Entering the coffee-room, we were deafened by a murmur like ten thousand swarming beehives. Politicians stood in clusters, brows troubled, voices either loud or low. The aristocratic faction, comprising England's wealthiest and most influential men, seemed calmer than the rest, since the question would be considered without their intervention. Beside the fire stood Ryland with his followers. Born in obscurity, Ryland possessed an enormous fortune inherited from his manufacturer father. As a young man he had witnessed the king's abdication and the merging of Lords and Commons into one house. He had welcomed these advances of popular power and devoted his life to strengthening and extending them. Since that time the great landowners' influence had increased. At first, therefore, Ryland had viewed Lord Raymond's manoeuvres without displeasure, as they drew numerous adherents away from his opponents. Now, however, matters had gone too far. The impoverished nobility welcomed the prospect of restored monarchy as the means of recovering their vanished authority and privileges. The nearly extinguished spirit of royalty stirred again in men's minds. Willing slaves, making subjects of themselves, they stood ready to bow their necks beneath the yoke. A few upright and independent spirits still survived as supports of the state; but the common ear had tired of the word republic, and many—the outcome would show whether most—longed for the glitter and spectacle of kingship. Ryland rose in opposition. He declared that this faction had grown only through his tolerance; the hour for indulgence was now over, and a single sweep of his arm would brush aside the cobwebs obscuring his countrymen's vision.

Raymond's arrival in the coffee-room brought an almost shouted welcome from his friends. They crowded about him, reckoned their strength, and explained why they expected this or that member, still publicly uncommitted, to join their ranks."}

{"request_id":"e066df9f92d606f6fdb03bb5","rewritten_text":"I cannot understand why she desired my friendship; yet, were the circumstances repeated, I am certain I should be deceived once more. Caroline returned my call only yesterday, and in all the interval I received neither a note nor a line. When she came, she plainly took no pleasure in the visit. She offered a brief, formal apology for not coming sooner, expressed no wish to see me again, and seemed so entirely changed that, by the time she left, I had firmly determined to pursue the acquaintance no further. I feel sorry for her, though I cannot avoid blaming her. She acted very wrongly in distinguishing me as she did; I can honestly declare that every step towards intimacy originated with her. Still, I pity her, because she must know her conduct has been wrong, and because I am convinced that concern for her brother explains it. I need not say more. Though we know that concern to be wholly unnecessary, if she feels it, it readily accounts for her treatment of me. Her brother is so deservedly dear to her that anxiety on his behalf must be natural and affectionate. I do wonder, however, that she retains such fears now; for if he had cared at all for me, we must surely have met long ago. Something she herself said assures me that he knows I am in town. Yet her manner of speaking suggested she was trying to convince herself of his attachment to Miss Darcy. I do not comprehend it. Were I less fearful of judging severely, I might almost say that the whole affair strongly resembles duplicity. I shall try to dismiss every distressing thought and dwell only upon what can give me happiness: your love, and the unfailing kindness of our dear aunt and uncle. Write very soon. Miss Bingley mentioned that he might never return to Netherfield and might give up the house, but spoke with no certainty. We had best say nothing of it. I am delighted by the pleasant reports you receive from our Hunsford friends. Do visit them with Sir William and Maria; I am sure you will find yourself very comfortable there.

“Yours, etc.”

Elizabeth felt some pain on reading this letter, but recovered her spirits when she considered that Jane would no longer be imposed upon, at least by the sister. Every hope concerning the brother had now entirely vanished."}

{"request_id":"c5cff90b8a41f0bc74eb52ba","rewritten_text":"At breakfast the following morning, the Otis family discussed the ghost at some length. Naturally, the United States Minister was slightly irritated that his gift had been refused. “I have no desire to injure the ghost,” he said, “and considering how long he has lived in this house, I must say that throwing pillows at him seems extremely impolite.” This perfectly reasonable observation, I regret to report, made the twins howl with laughter. “On the other hand,” he continued, “if he really refuses to use the Rising Sun Lubricator, we will have to confiscate his chains. Nobody can possibly sleep with that noise outside the bedrooms.”

The rest of the week passed without disturbance. Only the bloodstain’s constant reappearance on the library floor attracted attention. This was certainly strange, since Mr. Otis locked the door every night and the windows remained securely barred. The stain’s chameleon-like colors also caused considerable discussion. Some mornings it was a dull, almost Indian red; then it turned vermilion, then deep purple. Once, when the family came downstairs for prayers according to the simple ceremonies of the Free American Reformed Episcopalian Church, they found it bright emerald green. Naturally, these shifting colors amused everyone greatly, and they placed bets on the subject every evening. Only little Virginia declined to join in. For some unexplained reason, seeing the bloodstain always troubled her deeply. The morning it turned emerald green, she nearly cried.

The ghost appeared again on Sunday night. Shortly after the family went to bed, a tremendous crash in the hall startled them. They rushed downstairs and discovered that a large old suit of armor had come off its stand and fallen onto the stone floor. The Canterville ghost sat in a high-backed chair, rubbing his knees, his face expressing intense pain. The twins had brought their pea shooters and immediately fired two pellets at him. Their aim showed the precision achievable only through long, careful practice on a handwriting teacher. Meanwhile the United States Minister pointed his revolver at the ghost and, observing Californian etiquette, ordered him to put his hands up. With a furious shriek, the ghost sprang to his feet and swept through them like mist. As he passed, he put out Washington Otis’s candle, leaving everyone in complete darkness."}

{"request_id":"e3b045f67b049d2ca7f842ec","rewritten_text":"I passed several funerals, each with few mourners. Onlookers regarded them as signs of the gravest danger. Some watched the processions with feverish eagerness; others hurried away in fear; some wept aloud.

After the immediate relief of the sick, Adrian's principal concern had been to conceal the plague's symptoms and spread from London's inhabitants. He knew that fear and gloomy anticipation strengthened disease, and that despair and anxious brooding made the human body especially vulnerable to infection. Consequently, no distressing spectacles were visible. Most shops remained open, and the movement of pedestrians continued to some extent. Yet although the appearance of a stricken city had been avoided, London looked greatly changed to me, since I had not seen it since the outbreak began. There were no carriages, and tall grass grew in the streets. The houses looked abandoned, with most shutters closed. The people I met wore a ghastly, frightened stare, very different from Londoners' usual businesslike manner. My solitary carriage drew attention as it rattled towards the Protectoral Palace. The fashionable streets approaching it looked more desolate and deserted still. Adrian's antechamber was crowded; this was the hour when he received visitors. Unwilling to interrupt his work, I waited, watching the petitioners arrive and depart. They belonged to the middle and lower classes, whose livelihoods had disappeared with the collapse of trade and the busy pursuit of profit that pervaded every branch of life in our country. New arrivals looked anxious, sometimes terrified, in marked contrast to the resigned, even satisfied expressions of those who had been heard. I recognized my friend's influence in their livelier movements and cheerful faces. When the clock struck two, no more were admitted. Those who had missed their chance left sullenly or sorrowfully, while I entered the audience chamber.

The improvement in Adrian's health struck me immediately. He no longer drooped towards the ground like an over-tended spring flower that has grown beyond its strength and bends beneath the weight of its own blossoms. His eyes shone, his expression was composed, and his entire bearing conveyed a concentrated energy very unlike his former weariness. He sat at a table with several secretaries, who sorted petitions or recorded the notes made during that day's audience. Two or three petitioners were still waiting. I admired his fairness and patience. Those who could support themselves outside London he advised to leave immediately, providing them with the means to do so."}

{"request_id":"0769f3c3a3a3c6b459fc58ce","rewritten_text":"At last we removed two-thirds of the contents to lighten the chest, making it possible, with some difficulty, to lift it out of the hole. We placed the removed items among the brambles and left the dog guarding them. Jupiter gave him strict orders not to leave the spot or make a sound under any circumstances until we returned. Then we hurried home with the chest. After exhausting effort, we reached the hut safely at one in the morning. We were too worn out to attempt anything else immediately. We rested until two, ate supper, and set off for the hills again, carrying three sturdy sacks we were fortunate enough to find on the premises. Shortly before four we reached the pit and divided the remaining treasure among us as evenly as possible. Leaving the holes open, we returned once more to the hut. We put down our golden loads for the second time just as the earliest pale streaks of dawn appeared above the eastern treetops.

We were now completely exhausted, but the excitement would not let us rest. After three or four hours of disturbed sleep, we got up, as though by prior agreement, to examine the treasure.

The chest had been filled to the brim. We spent that entire day and most of the following night inspecting its contents. Nothing had been organized or arranged; everything had simply been thrown together. Once we had carefully sorted the collection, we discovered that our wealth exceeded even our first expectations. The coins alone amounted to slightly more than four hundred and fifty thousand dollars, valuing them as accurately as possible according to the tables then available. There was no silver whatsoever. Every coin was gold, old and varied: French, Spanish, and German money, a few English guineas, and some tokens unlike anything we had ever seen. Several coins were exceptionally large and heavy, but so worn that their inscriptions were unreadable. None was American. The jewels were harder to value. We counted a hundred and ten diamonds, none small and some exceptionally large and fine; eighteen brilliantly luminous rubies; three hundred and ten beautiful emeralds; twenty-one sapphires; and one opal. All these stones had been broken out of their settings and tossed loose into the chest. We separated the settings from the rest of the gold. They appeared to have been battered with hammers, apparently to make identification impossible."}

{"request_id":"854738262b258f28496298e1","rewritten_text":"It is only four o'clock, but this is winter, and the sun has already set. There are no clouds in the clear, freezing sky to reflect its slanting rays. Instead, the air itself has a faint pink glow, reflected again in the snow covering the ground. I live in an isolated cottage on a wide, lonely heath. No sound of life reaches me. The empty plain is white except for a few dark patches on the tops of the steep, pointed mounds. Snow slid from them as it fell, leaving a thinner layer than on the flat ground, and the midday sun has melted it away. A few birds peck at the hard ice over the pools, for the frost has lasted a long time.

My state of mind is strange. I am alone—utterly alone—in the world. Misfortune has swept over me and left me withered. I know I am going to die, and I feel happy, even joyful. I feel my pulse: it beats quickly. I put my thin hand to my cheek: it burns. A frail, restless spark of life inside me is giving off its last flashes. I will never see another winter's snow. I believe I will never again feel the life-giving warmth of a summer sun. With that certainty, I begin to write my tragic story. Perhaps a story like mine should die with me, but an unnamed feeling urges me on. My body and mind are too weak to resist even the slightest impulse. When I was strong, I thought my story contained a sacred horror that made it impossible to speak of. Now, as I approach death, I reveal and profane its mysterious terrors. It is like the grove of the Eumenides, which only the dying may enter—and Oedipus is about to die.

What am I writing? I must gather my thoughts. Perhaps nobody will read these pages except you, my friend, who will receive them after my death. Yet I will not write only to you. It will please me to describe our friendship in a way that would be unnecessary if you alone were my reader. So I will tell my story as though writing for strangers. You have often asked why I live alone, why I weep, and above all why I keep such an unkind, unbreakable silence. While living, I dared not answer. In death, I reveal the mystery. Others may glance carelessly through these pages. But to you, Woodville, my kind and loving friend, they will be precious reminders of a heartbroken girl who still feels gratitude towards you as she dies. Your tears will fall on the words describing my sorrows. I know they will, and while I still live, I thank you for your sympathy."}

{"request_id":"9c3b920c85aba07ab0188f7d","rewritten_text":""Some time passed before I recovered enough to attend to the balloon's routine needs. When I finally examined it carefully, I was immensely relieved to find no damage. All my equipment was safe, and fortunately I had lost neither supplies nor ballast. I had secured everything so firmly that such a loss was, in fact, impossible. My watch showed six o'clock. I was still rising quickly, and the barometer indicated an altitude of three and three-quarter miles. Directly beneath me on the ocean lay a small, dark, slightly elongated object, apparently about the size and shape of a child's domino. Through the telescope I clearly identified it as a British ninety-four-gun ship, sailing close to the wind and pitching heavily, with its bow pointed west-southwest. Apart from this ship, I could see only the ocean, the sky, and the sun, which had risen some time before.

"It is time I explained to Your Excellencies the purpose of this dangerous voyage. You will remember that my desperate circumstances in Rotterdam had finally led me to decide on suicide. I did not actually hate life itself; rather, the misfortunes surrounding my situation had tormented me beyond endurance. In this state, still wanting to live but weary of life, I found a new possibility in the treatise at the bookseller's stall. Then I made my final decision. I would depart yet remain alive, leave the world yet continue to exist. To abandon riddles and speak plainly, I resolved to attempt a passage to the moon, whatever the consequences. So that you will not consider me madder than I truly am, I shall explain as well as I can the reasoning that persuaded me such an undertaking, though unquestionably difficult and dangerous, was not entirely beyond the reach of a courageous spirit.

"The first consideration was the moon's actual distance from the earth. The mean, or average, distance between the centres of the two planets is 59.9643 times the earth's equatorial radius, which is only about 237,000 miles. I emphasize that this is the average distance. Remember that the moon follows an elliptical orbit, whose eccentricity amounts to no less than 0.05484 of the ellipse's semimajor axis, with the earth's centre at one focus. If I could somehow arrange to meet the moon at its perigee, the distance I have given would therefore be considerably reduced."}

{"request_id":"f741567a245554b4895caa4a","rewritten_text":"“But the thought that sustained me at the outset of my course now merely presses me deeper into the dust. My theories and expectations have come to nothing, and, like the archangel who coveted omnipotence, I am bound within an everlasting hell. My fancy was lively, but my powers of reasoning and sustained labour were equally strong; together they enabled me to conceive and accomplish the creation of a man. Even now I cannot remember without emotion the dreams that accompanied my unfinished work. In imagination I walked through heaven, rejoicing now in my abilities, now burning at the prospect of their consequences. From childhood great hopes and exalted ambition possessed me; yet how low I have fallen! My friend, had you known me in those days, you would scarcely recognize this degraded being. Despair seldom entered my heart. A mighty destiny seemed to carry me onward until I fell, never, never to rise again.”

Must I lose this admirable creature, then? I have yearned for a friend, sought someone who would understand and love me. Here, upon these deserted seas, I have found him; yet I fear I have gained him only long enough to learn his worth before losing him. I would restore his attachment to life, but he rejects the thought.

“I thank you, Walton,” he said, “for your kindness towards one so wretched. But when you speak of fresh attachments and new affections, do you believe anyone can replace the dead? Can any man become another Clerval to me, or any woman another Elizabeth? Even without exceptional virtues to engage our love, the friends of childhood hold a certain influence over our minds that later companions can scarcely achieve. They understand our earliest dispositions, which may change but are never wholly removed, and can therefore judge our conduct with greater confidence in the honesty of our intentions. Unless signs appeared in youth, a brother or sister can hardly suspect the other of deceit or treachery, while a later friend, however affectionate, may despite himself be regarded with mistrust. But my friends were precious not only through familiarity and shared experience, but through their own excellence. Wherever I go, Elizabeth’s comforting voice and Clerval’s conversation will whisper in my ears. They are dead, and in this loneliness only one feeling can induce me to keep living. Were I occupied with some noble enterprise or purpose promising great benefit to my fellow beings, I could live to complete it."}

{"request_id":"2c0ecbf522ac34005476540d","rewritten_text":"I cannot understand why she wanted to become close to me, but if the same circumstances arose again, I am sure I would be fooled again. Caroline did not return my visit until yesterday. In the meantime, I received no note, not a single line. When she finally came, she clearly took no pleasure in it. She gave a brief, formal apology for not visiting sooner, said nothing about wanting to see me again, and behaved so differently in every respect that by the time she left I had firmly decided to let the acquaintance end. I pity her, although I cannot help blaming her. She was wrong to single me out as she did. I can honestly say that every step toward friendship came from her. Yet I feel sorry for her, because she must know she has behaved badly, and because I am certain her concern for her brother explains it. I need not explain further. We know there is no reason for that concern, but if she feels it, it readily accounts for her behavior toward me. Since she loves her brother so much, as he deserves, any anxiety on his behalf is natural and kind. Still, I cannot understand why she has those fears now. If he cared about me at all, we would have met long ago. Something she said herself convinced me that he knows I am in London. Yet from the way she talked, she seemed to be trying to persuade herself that he truly prefers Miss Darcy. It makes no sense to me. If I were not afraid of judging too harshly, I would almost say that all this looks distinctly dishonest. I will try to put painful thoughts aside and think only of what makes me happy: your affection and our dear aunt and uncle’s constant kindness. Please write very soon. Miss Bingley mentioned that he might never return to Netherfield and might give up the house, though she spoke without certainty. We had better keep that to ourselves. I am so pleased you have good news from our friends at Hunsford. Do visit them with Sir William and Maria. I am sure you will be very comfortable there.

“Yours, etc.”

The letter caused Elizabeth some pain. But her spirits recovered when she reflected that Jane would no longer be deceived, at least by the sister. As for the brother, all hope was now completely gone."}

{"request_id":"360614125dc3f6913d977351","rewritten_text":"I then bored a hole in the end of the keg above the pitcher and inserted a plug of soft wood carved into a tapering cone. I pushed it in or drew it out as necessary, experimenting until it fitted with precisely the tightness required. Water seeping through the hole would then fall into the pitcher below and fill it to the brim in exactly sixty minutes. This was naturally quick and easy to establish by measuring what proportion of the pitcher filled within a given time. Once that was arranged, the rest of the plan was straightforward. I positioned my bed on the basket floor so that, when I lay down, my head would rest directly beneath the pitcher's spout. Clearly, after an hour, the full pitcher would have to overflow, and the water would escape through the spout, which sat slightly below the rim. Just as clearly, falling more than four feet, that water could only land on my face. The inevitable result would be to wake me instantly, even from the soundest possible sleep.

“It was already eleven when I finished these arrangements, and I went straight to bed, entirely confident that my invention would work. That confidence was justified. Exactly every sixty minutes, my dependable timekeeper woke me. I poured the pitcher's contents back into the keg through its bung-hole, performed the necessary work on the condenser, and lay down again. These regular interruptions disturbed my sleep even less than I had expected. When I finally rose for the day, it was seven o'clock, and the sun stood many degrees above the line of my horizon.

“April 3. I found the balloon at an enormous altitude, with the earth's curvature noticeably more pronounced. Beneath me, a group of black specks lay in the ocean, undoubtedly islands. Far north, at the edge of the horizon, I noticed a narrow white streak of extraordinary brilliance. I had no hesitation in identifying it as the southern edge of the Polar Sea's ice. This greatly excited my curiosity, since I hoped to travel much farther north and might eventually find myself directly above the Pole itself. I regretted that, if this happened, my immense height would prevent me from making as precise a survey as I would have liked."}

{"request_id":"425071b1d7f5ff4f0396f5fa","rewritten_text":"In a certain town, whose name I shall prudently withhold for several reasons, without supplying an invented one, there stands a public building once common to towns both large and small: a workhouse. In that institution for the poor, on a day and date I need not bother to repeat, since they cannot possibly matter to the reader at this point, the little mortal named at the start of this chapter was born.

For a long time after the parish doctor brought him into this world of sorrow and trouble, nobody could be sure the child would survive long enough to receive any name at all. Had he failed to do so, these memoirs would almost certainly never have appeared. If they had, they would have filled only a couple of pages, earning the priceless distinction of being the shortest and most accurate biography in the literature of any age or country.

I do not intend to argue that being born in a workhouse is, in itself, the luckiest or most enviable experience a human being can have. Yet in this particular case, it was the best thing that could possibly have happened to Oliver Twist. The difficulty was persuading Oliver to begin breathing—a tiresome activity, but one that habit has made necessary for a comfortable life. For some time he lay gasping on a small mattress stuffed with loose fibers, caught between this world and the next, with the balance leaning decidedly towards the next. If, during that short interval, Oliver had been surrounded by attentive grandmothers, worried aunts, experienced nurses, and deeply learned doctors, he would undoubtedly have been killed almost at once. Fortunately, only two people were present: a poor old woman made somewhat confused by an unusually generous helping of beer, and a parish doctor paid under contract to attend such cases. Oliver and Nature were therefore left to settle the question between themselves. After a few struggles, Oliver breathed, sneezed, and began announcing to everyone in the workhouse that the parish had gained another mouth to support. He did this by raising as loud a cry as anyone could reasonably expect from a baby boy who had possessed that useful instrument, a voice, for little more than three minutes and a quarter."}

{"request_id":"f306113ce241c35b2fa8effd","rewritten_text":"“Keep in mind the points I have emphasized: the strange voice, the extraordinary agility, and the complete lack of motive for such a horrifying murder. Now consider the killing itself. A woman was strangled by hand and pushed headfirst up a chimney. Ordinary killers do not murder in this way, and certainly do not dispose of bodies like this. You must agree that forcing the corpse into the chimney was extremely strange, wholly unlike what we expect of human behavior, even from the most depraved people. Think, too, of the strength needed to push her body up that narrow opening so firmly that several people working together could barely pull it down.

“Now consider the other evidence of astonishing strength. Thick locks of gray human hair lay on the hearth—very thick locks, torn out by the roots. You know how much force it takes to pull even twenty or thirty hairs from someone’s head at once. You saw those locks just as I did. Pieces of scalp still clung to their roots, a hideous sight. This clearly showed the enormous power used to tear out perhaps half a million hairs at a time. The old woman’s throat was not simply cut: her head was completely separated from her body, using only a razor. Notice, too, the animal savagery of these acts. I will not dwell on the bruises on Madame L’Espanaye’s body. Monsieur Dumas and his worthy colleague Monsieur Etienne declared that some blunt object had caused them. On that point, they are quite correct. The blunt object was plainly the stone paving in the yard. The victim fell onto it from the window overlooking the bed. Simple as this explanation now seems, the police missed it for the same reason they missed the width of the shutters. The matter of the nails had closed their minds completely to the possibility that the windows had ever been opened.

“If, in addition, you have properly considered the room’s peculiar disorder, we can now bring all these observations together. We have astonishing agility, strength beyond human ability, animal ferocity, and slaughter without a motive. We have a form of horror so grotesque that it seems entirely foreign to human nature. Finally, we have a voice whose tones sounded unfamiliar to listeners from many countries, a voice without any clear or understandable syllables."}

{"request_id":"94f6908f1a254a7902113f2e","rewritten_text":"It is scarcely four o’clock, yet winter’s sun has already gone down. No clouds hang in the clear, freezing sky to catch its sloping rays, but a faint rosy tint colours the air itself and is reflected once more upon the snow below. My dwelling is a lonely cottage on a broad, deserted heath; no living voice comes to me. The waste stretches white before my eyes, broken only by a few dark patches where the midday sun has bared the tops of pointed hillocks. There the snow, slipping as it fell, had lain less deeply than on level ground. A handful of birds peck at the solid ice upon the pools, for the frost has endured a long while.

My mind is strangely disposed. I am alone—utterly alone in the world. Misfortune’s blight has swept across me and left me withered. I know death approaches, and I am happy, even joyful. I feel my pulse: it races. I press my wasted hand against my cheek: it burns. A frail, quick spirit remains within me, giving out its final sparks. I shall never behold another winter’s snow; I believe I shall never again experience the renewing warmth of a summer sun. With this conviction I begin to set down my sorrowful history. Perhaps a tale like mine ought to perish with me; yet an impulse I cannot explain urges me forward, and I am too feeble in body and mind to withstand even the lightest prompting. When life was vigorous in me, I believed my story held a sacred horror that forbade its telling. Now, as I prepare to die, I profane those mysterious terrors. It is like the grove of the Eumenides, into which only the dying may pass; and Oedipus approaches death.

What am I setting down? I must gather my thoughts. I cannot know whether anyone except you, my friend, will read these pages, which you will receive when I am dead. I do not write to you alone, because I wish to linger over our friendship in a manner unnecessary if you were my only reader. I shall therefore tell my history as though to strangers. Often you have asked why I live apart, why I weep, and, above all, why my silence is so unyielding and unkind. Living, I dared not answer; dying, I disclose the secret. Others may turn these pages carelessly. To you, Woodville, my gentle, loving friend, they will be precious relics of a broken-hearted girl whose gratitude to you still warms her as she dies. Your tears will wet the words recounting my sorrows; I know they will, and while life remains I thank you for your compassion."}

{"request_id":"a5f331a24bec036c19e31896","rewritten_text":"At last we removed two-thirds of the contents to make the box lighter. Even then, lifting it out of the hole took some effort. We placed the removed items among the brambles and left the dog to guard them. Jupiter firmly ordered him not to move or make a sound, whatever happened, until we returned. Then we hurried home with the chest. After an exhausting struggle, we reached the hut safely at one in the morning. We were too worn out to do anything more at once. We rested until two and ate supper, then immediately set off for the hills with three strong sacks that happened to be available. Shortly before four, we reached the pit and divided the remaining treasure among us as equally as possible. Leaving the holes open, we started back. We deposited our second golden loads at the hut just as the first faint light of dawn appeared above the eastern treetops.

We were now completely exhausted, but our intense excitement kept us from resting properly. After sleeping uneasily for three or four hours, we all rose as though we had arranged it beforehand and began examining the treasure.

The chest had been filled to the top. We spent the entire day and most of the following night studying its contents. Nothing had been organized; everything was piled together at random. After carefully sorting it all, we discovered that we were even richer than we had first believed. The coins alone were worth a little over four hundred and fifty thousand dollars, according to our best calculations using the available tables of coin values. There was no silver at all. Every coin was gold, and they were old and varied: French, Spanish, and German money, a few English guineas, and some tokens unlike anything we had seen. Several very large, heavy coins were so worn that we could not read their inscriptions. None was American. The jewels were harder to value. There were a hundred and ten diamonds, some exceptionally large and fine, and none small; eighteen brilliantly sparkling rubies; three hundred and ten beautiful emeralds; twenty-one sapphires; and one opal. All the stones had been broken out of their settings and tossed loose into the chest. We found the settings among the other gold. They seemed to have been hammered out of shape, as though to stop anyone from recognizing them."}

{"request_id":"cbe79a7f9fbcda1d7832c61a","rewritten_text":"The sole difficulty that appeared to occupy him was the choice of whom to dispatch. He understood perfectly that murder, like the faiths of the Pagan world, demands a victim no less than a priest. Since he was no genius, he possessed no enemies; moreover, he considered this an unsuitable occasion for indulging private resentment or aversion, his undertaking being of the utmost gravity and solemnity. He therefore wrote the names of his friends and relations upon a sheet of notepaper and, after deliberate reflection, selected Lady Clementina Beauchamp. This dear elderly lady lived in Curzon Street and was his second cousin on his mother's side. He had always cherished Lady Clem, as she was universally known. Being himself extremely rich, having inherited the whole of Lord Rugby's estate upon attaining his majority, he could gain no sordid financial benefit from her death. Indeed, the longer he considered it, the more perfectly she seemed to meet the requirements. Any postponement would be unjust to Sybil, he felt, and he resolved to arrange matters immediately.

Naturally, his first business was to pay the cheiromantist. Seating himself at a little Sheraton desk near the window, he wrote a cheque for £105 to Mr. Septimus Podgers, sealed it in an envelope, and instructed his valet to deliver it to West Moon Street. Next he telephoned the stables for his hansom and dressed for an outing. At the door he turned to look at Sybil Merton's photograph, vowing that whatever happened, she would never learn what he was undertaking for her. The secret of his self-sacrifice would remain forever within his own heart.

On the road to the Buckingham he called at a florist's and ordered for Sybil a lovely basket of narcissus, their white petals surrounding bright pheasant's eyes. Reaching the club, he proceeded directly to the library, rang, and requested that the waiter bring a lemon-and-soda and a volume on Toxicology. He had settled firmly upon poison as the most suitable means of conducting this disagreeable business. Any personal violence was profoundly repugnant to him. Besides, he particularly wished to avoid murdering Lady Clementina in a fashion likely to draw public notice. The prospect of becoming a celebrity at Lady Windermere's, or finding his name displayed in the paragraphs of vulgar society newspapers, was hateful to him."}

{"request_id":"aa2479fd6f918966d1f725c7","rewritten_text":"I uncovered it, and a light breeze quickly stirred it into flame. Noticing this, I made a fan from branches and used it to revive the embers whenever they nearly died out. When darkness returned, I was pleased to discover that the fire provided light as well as warmth. It also proved useful for food: some scraps left by the travellers had been roasted and tasted much better than the berries I gathered from trees. I therefore tried cooking my own food by laying it directly on the glowing embers. This ruined the berries, but greatly improved the nuts and roots.

"Food soon grew scarce, however. Often I spent an entire day searching unsuccessfully for a few acorns to ease my hunger. I decided to leave the place where I had been living and look for somewhere that could satisfy my few needs more easily. The prospect of losing my fire distressed me greatly. I had found it by chance and did not know how to create another. For several hours I considered this problem seriously, but at last I had to give up trying to solve it. Wrapping myself in my cloak, I set off through the woods toward the setting sun. I wandered for three days before finally reaching open countryside. Heavy snow had fallen the previous night, covering the fields in an unbroken white sheet. The view was desolate, and the cold, wet substance on the ground chilled my feet.

"It was about seven in the morning, and I desperately wanted food and shelter. At last I noticed a small hut on a rise, evidently built for a shepherd's use. I had never seen anything like it and studied its construction with great curiosity. The door stood open, so I went inside. An old man sat beside a fire, preparing his breakfast over it. Hearing a sound, he turned and saw me. He screamed, fled the hut, and raced across the fields faster than his frail body seemed capable of moving. His appearance, unlike any I had seen before, and his sudden flight puzzled me somewhat. But the hut itself enchanted me. Neither rain nor snow could enter; the ground was dry. At that moment it seemed as beautiful and heavenly a refuge to me as Pandemonium must have seemed to the demons of hell after their suffering in the lake of fire."}

{"request_id":"b7c156e3578c53744afa6e27","rewritten_text":"Among the public buildings of a certain town, which for several good reasons I had better leave unnamed, without even giving it an invented name, stands an institution once familiar in towns of every size: a workhouse. In that workhouse, on a date I need not bother to specify, since it cannot possibly matter to the reader at this point in the proceedings, the small mortal named at the head of this chapter was born.

For some time after the parish surgeon introduced him into this world of grief and difficulty, it remained seriously doubtful whether the baby would live long enough to receive any name. Had he failed to do so, these memoirs would almost certainly never have appeared. Or, if they had, their entire contents would have occupied a couple of pages, giving them the priceless distinction of being the shortest and most accurate biography in the literature of any country or age.

I do not intend to argue that birth in a workhouse is, in itself, the luckiest and most enviable event that could befall a human being. I do maintain, however, that in this particular case it was the best thing that could possibly have happened to Oliver Twist. The difficulty was persuading Oliver to undertake the business of breathing, an inconvenient practice that habit has made essential to comfortable existence. For a while he lay gasping on a small mattress stuffed with scraps of wool, suspended rather unevenly between this world and the next, with the balance decidedly favouring the next. Had attentive grandmothers, anxious aunts, experienced nurses, and profoundly learned doctors surrounded him during those moments, they would unquestionably have killed him in very short order. As it happened, the only people present were an elderly pauper woman made somewhat hazy by an unusually generous supply of beer, and a parish surgeon who handled such matters under contract. Oliver and Nature were therefore left to settle the question between themselves. After a few struggles, Oliver drew breath, sneezed, and announced to the workhouse residents that the parish had acquired a fresh burden. His announcement took the form of a cry as loud as could reasonably be expected from a male infant who had possessed that useful accessory, a voice, for little more than three minutes and fifteen seconds."}

{"request_id":"85fea6f43a7b5ce2a03ea1cc","rewritten_text":"At the top of the stairs he regained his composure and decided to unleash his famous burst of demonic laughter. It had proved extremely useful on several occasions. Rumor credited it with turning Lord Raker’s wig gray overnight, and it had certainly persuaded three of Lady Canterville’s French governesses to resign before completing a month. So he gave his most horrifying laugh, making the old vaulted roof echo repeatedly. The last dreadful echoes had barely faded when a door opened and Mrs. Otis appeared in a pale blue dressing gown. “I’m afraid you’re quite unwell,” she said, “so I’ve brought you some of Dr. Dobell’s tincture. If it’s indigestion, you’ll find this an excellent remedy.” The ghost glared furiously and immediately prepared to transform himself into a large black dog. He was justly celebrated for this accomplishment, which the family doctor always blamed for the permanent mental incapacity of Lord Canterville’s uncle, the Honorable Thomas Horton. Approaching footsteps made him reconsider his terrible intention. Instead, he glowed faintly and vanished with a deep, graveyard groan just as the twins reached him.

Back in his room, he broke down completely, overcome by violent distress. The twins’ vulgarity and Mrs. Otis’s crude materialism were naturally infuriating, but what upset him most was his failure to wear the armor. He had hoped that even modern Americans would be moved by the sight of a Spectre in Armour, if only out of respect for their national poet Longfellow. He himself had passed many weary hours reading Longfellow’s graceful, appealing poetry while the Cantervilles were in London. Besides, the armor was his own. He had worn it with great success at the Kenilworth tournament and received lavish compliments from no less a person than the Virgin Queen. Yet when he put it on now, the enormous breastplate and steel helmet had overwhelmed him with their weight. He fell heavily onto the stone floor, badly scraping both knees and bruising the knuckles of his right hand.

For several days afterward he was very ill and hardly left his room, except to maintain the bloodstain properly. With careful attention to his health, however, he recovered and resolved to make a third attempt at frightening the United States Minister and his family."}

{"request_id":"1d51e9b95b0268ecf8b58ee6","rewritten_text":"He wrote that he was wasting his time where he was. The friends he had made in London had sent letters asking him to return and finish the negotiations for his venture in India. He could no longer postpone leaving. But his journey to London might lead to his longer voyage even sooner than he expected, and he begged me to spend as much time with him as I could. He therefore asked me to leave my lonely island and meet him at Perth, so we could travel south together. His letter brought me back to life a little, and I decided to leave the island in two days.

Before going, however, I had a task to do that made me shudder. I had to pack my chemical instruments. That meant entering the room where I had carried out my hateful work and touching the tools whose very appearance made me feel sick. At daybreak the next morning, I gathered enough courage to unlock the laboratory. Pieces of the unfinished creature I had destroyed lay scattered across the floor. I almost felt as though I had torn apart the living flesh of a human being. I stopped to steady myself, then entered. My hands shook as I carried the instruments out. But I realized I must not leave the remains of my work behind to frighten the local people and arouse their suspicions. I put the remains in a basket with a large number of stones, set it aside, and resolved to throw it into the sea that night. Meanwhile, I sat on the beach cleaning and arranging my chemical equipment.

My feelings had changed completely since the night the demon appeared. Before then, I had regarded my promise with dark despair as something I must carry out, whatever the consequences. Now I felt as if a covering had been lifted from my eyes and I could see clearly for the first time. The thought of returning to my work never entered my mind, even for an instant. His threat weighed heavily on me, but I did not think of preventing it by an action of my own. I had firmly decided that creating another being like the fiend I had already made would be an act of the most vile and terrible selfishness. I drove away every thought that might lead me to any other conclusion."}

{"request_id":"847aa5fa19f98891075ce767","rewritten_text":"We grew up side by side, with somewhat less than a year between our ages. It scarcely needs saying that no quarrel or estrangement ever divided us. Concord animated our companionship, while the differences and oppositions in our dispositions served only to bring us closer. Elizabeth's nature was quieter and more collected; yet, ardent as I was, I could devote myself more intensely to a subject and felt a deeper thirst for learning. She occupied herself with the airy inventions of poets. In the grand and marvellous landscape about our Swiss home—the sublime mountain forms, the seasons' changes, storms and stillness, winter's silence, and the stir and vitality of Alpine summers—she discovered inexhaustible sources of wonder and pleasure. While she regarded the splendid outward aspect of things with grave contentment, I took delight in tracing their causes. To me the world was a mystery waiting to be deciphered. Among my first remembered feelings are curiosity, eager efforts to discover nature's secret laws, and a joy approaching ecstasy whenever those laws became clear.

When a second son was born, seven years younger than I, my parents entirely relinquished their wandering existence and settled in their own country. We owned a house in Geneva and a country residence at Belrive, on the eastern bank of the lake, rather more than a league outside the city. We lived chiefly at the latter, where my parents passed their days in considerable retirement. My disposition inclined me to shun crowds and devote myself warmly to a few. Thus I felt little attachment to my schoolmates in general, but formed the closest friendship with one of them. Henry Clerval was a Geneva merchant's son, a boy of uncommon gifts and imagination. He delighted in bold undertakings, hardship, and danger itself. He had read deeply in romances and tales of chivalry, composed heroic verses, and begun many stories of enchantment and knightly exploits. He sought to persuade us to perform plays and take part in masquerades, choosing our characters from the heroes of Roncesvalles, King Arthur's Round Table, and the gallant company who had shed their blood to recover the holy sepulchre from the infidels.

No one could have enjoyed a happier childhood than mine. Kindness and indulgence seemed the very essence of my parents. We never regarded them as tyrants who would dispose of our lives according to their whims, but as the authors and providers of all the pleasures that surrounded us."}

{"request_id":"759e777366450bedd74a4cc0","rewritten_text":"Mrs Musgrove seized the first chance to speak with Anne privately. 'Oh, Miss Anne, I do wish Mrs Charles possessed something of your way with the children. With you they are entirely different creatures! Generally, of course, they are sadly spoilt. What a pity you cannot teach your sister to manage them. Such fine, healthy little children, poor dears—no partiality in saying so! Yet Mrs Charles has no notion how to treat them. Good heavens, what a nuisance they can be! I assure you, Miss Anne, it makes me less eager to have them at our house than I would otherwise be. Mrs Charles, I think, is rather displeased that I do not invite them more frequently. But children are a trial when one must constantly correct them: don't touch this, don't do that; or purchase tolerable behaviour with more cake than is wholesome.'

Mary also confided in her. 'Mrs Musgrove believes her servants so dependable that questioning it would amount to treason. Yet, without any exaggeration, I am certain that her senior housemaid and laundry maid spend all day roaming the village instead of attending to their work. Wherever I go, there they are. I declare I cannot visit the nursery twice without finding some sign of them. If Jemima were not the most reliable, steady creature alive, they would corrupt her completely; she tells me they are forever urging her to walk out with them.' Mrs Musgrove's account, meanwhile, ran thus: 'I never interfere with my daughter-in-law's arrangements; I know too well that it would not answer. But I shall mention this to you, Miss Anne, since you may be able to remedy matters. I have little confidence in Mrs Charles's nursery maid. Strange tales reach me about her, and she is always off gadding. I can say from personal knowledge that she dresses so extravagantly as to ruin any servant who associates with her. Mrs Charles puts absolute faith in her, I know. Still, I offer this hint so you may keep an eye open. Should you notice anything wrong, you need not hesitate to speak.'

Then Mary complained that, when other families dined with them at the Great House, Mrs Musgrove frequently neglected to accord her the precedence she deserved. Why, she asked, should being regarded as one of the household deprive her of her proper place?"}

{"request_id":"29ec39da8adfb38a3d3e0cd4","rewritten_text":"You will be pleased to learn that the undertaking which inspired such gloomy forebodings in you has begun without mishap. I reached this place yesterday, and my first employment is to reassure my dear sister that I am well and increasingly confident of success.

Already I am far to the north of London. Walking through the streets of Petersburgh, I feel a chill northern wind against my face, strengthening my nerves and filling me with pleasure. Can you comprehend the sensation? The breeze comes from the very regions towards which I travel, offering an advance taste of their icy climate. Encouraged by this promising wind, my dreams grow more ardent and distinct. In vain do I attempt to convince myself that the pole is a realm of cold and desolation; my imagination continually pictures a country of loveliness and joy. There, Margaret, the sun never disappears: its wide disk grazes the horizon and sheds unending radiance. There—for, with your permission, sister, I shall place some faith in earlier voyagers—snow and frost are excluded. A tranquil sea may bear us towards a land whose wonders and beauty exceed those of every inhabited region yet discovered. Its natural productions and landscape may have no parallel, just as the appearances of the heavenly bodies in those unexplored solitudes assuredly have none. What cannot be hoped for in a land of everlasting light? Perhaps I shall find the extraordinary force that draws the compass needle, and bring order to a thousand astronomical observations whose apparent irregularities need only this voyage to become permanently intelligible. I shall satisfy my burning curiosity by beholding a part of the globe no traveller has seen, and may walk on ground never marked by a human foot. Such attractions suffice to overcome every fear of peril or death and make me begin this difficult journey with the delight of a child setting off in a small boat with holiday companions to explore his native river. Yet even if every conjecture proves mistaken, you cannot deny the immeasurable service I may render humanity, down to its final generation, by finding a passage near the pole to countries now requiring months to reach; or by discovering the magnet's secret, a discovery possible, if at all, only through an expedition like mine.

These thoughts have quieted the agitation with which I opened my letter. My heart glows with an enthusiasm that lifts me heavenward, for nothing so calms the mind as a settled purpose, an object upon which the soul can steadily direct its intellectual gaze."}

{"request_id":"9622276c7fd5757eae46a0e0","rewritten_text":"He also had to consider Sybil’s parents. They were rather old-fashioned and might oppose the marriage if any scandal arose. Still, he felt sure that if he explained the whole situation, they would be the first to appreciate his motives. Everything therefore favored poison. It was safe, reliable, and quiet, and avoided unpleasant scenes, which, like most Englishmen, he strongly disliked.

“He knew absolutely nothing about poisons, however. The waiter seemed unable to find any books in the library except Ruff’s Guide and Bailey’s Magazine, so he searched the shelves himself. Eventually he found a beautifully bound Pharmacopoeia and Erskine’s Toxicology, edited by Sir Mathew Reid. Sir Mathew was President of the Royal College of Physicians and one of the Buckingham’s oldest members, having been elected by mistake for someone else. The error had so annoyed the Committee that when the intended candidate appeared, they unanimously rejected him. The technical language in both books confused Lord Arthur. He was beginning to regret neglecting his classical studies at Oxford when he discovered, in Erskine’s second volume, a detailed and interesting description of aconitine written in reasonably clear English. It appeared to be exactly what he needed. According to the account, it worked quickly, almost instantly, caused no pain, and tasted quite acceptable when enclosed in a gelatin capsule, as Sir Mathew recommended. He noted the stated fatal quantity on his shirt cuff, returned the books to the shelves, and walked up St. James’s Street to Pestle and Humbey’s, the distinguished pharmacy. Mr. Pestle, who always served aristocratic customers himself, was surprised by the request and respectfully murmured that a doctor’s certificate was required. Lord Arthur explained that he needed it for a large Norwegian mastiff showing early signs of rabies. The dog had already bitten the coachman twice on the calf and had to be destroyed. This explanation completely satisfied Mr. Pestle. He praised Lord Arthur’s remarkable knowledge of poisons and had the prescription prepared immediately.

“Lord Arthur placed the capsule in a pretty little silver sweets box he spotted in a Bond Street shop window. He threw away Pestle and Hambey’s unattractive pillbox and drove straight to Lady Clementina’s."}

{"request_id":"24ea4cd907f24553671e89a7","rewritten_text":"But the thought that sustained me at the beginning of my career now only drives me farther into the dust. All my theories and hopes have come to nothing. Like the archangel who aspired to absolute power, I am chained in an everlasting hell. My imagination was vivid, and my abilities to analyze and apply myself were equally strong. Together, those qualities enabled me to conceive and accomplish the creation of a man. Even now, I cannot calmly recall the dreams I entertained while the work remained unfinished. In thought I walked through heaven, sometimes celebrating my own powers, sometimes burning with anticipation of their consequences. Since childhood I had been filled with high hopes and great ambition. How far I have fallen! My friend, if you had known the man I once was, you would not recognize me in this degraded state. Despair rarely entered my heart. A great destiny seemed to carry me forward, until I fell, never, never to rise again.”

Must I lose this remarkable man? I have yearned for a friend, for someone who would understand and love me. Here on these deserted seas I have found him, but I fear I have gained him only to discover his worth and then lose him. I want to reconcile him to life, but he rejects the thought.

“Thank you, Walton,” he said, “for your kindness towards such a miserable wretch. But when you speak of new attachments and fresh affections, do you imagine anyone could replace those I have lost? Could any man be what Clerval was to me, or any woman become another Elizabeth? Even when exceptional qualities have not inspired a special love, our childhood companions retain a hold over us that hardly any later friend can acquire. They know the dispositions we had as children, which may change but are never entirely erased. They can therefore judge our behavior with greater certainty about the honesty of our motives. A brother or sister will never suspect the other of deceit or dishonesty unless those tendencies appeared early. Yet another friend, however devoted, may sometimes be viewed with suspicion despite himself. My friends were dear to me not merely through familiarity and shared experience, but through their own excellence. Wherever I go, I will always hear Elizabeth's comforting voice and Clerval's conversation whispering in my ear. They are dead. In such loneliness, only one feeling can persuade me to remain alive. If I were engaged in some great enterprise or plan that promised widespread benefit to humanity, I could live to complete it."}

{"request_id":"46d1bba7f9c54d5e2d2a88d9","rewritten_text":"Summer advanced, and plague, crowned with the sun's fierce rays, sent her unfailing arrows across the earth. Nations bowed beneath them and died. Crops that had grown abundantly lay rotting on the ground by autumn, while the miserable man who had gone out to gather food for his children lay rigid in the furrow, struck down by plague. The green woods moved their branches majestically over the dying scattered in their shade, whose discordant cries answered that solemn music. Brightly colored birds darted among the shadows, and untroubled deer rested safely on the ferns. Oxen and horses wandered from unattended stables to graze in the wheat, for death fell only on humankind.

As summer and mortality increased, so did our fear. My poor beloved and I looked at each other and at our children. “We will save them, Idris,” I said. “I will save them. Years from now we will tell them about these fears, when both the fears and their cause have disappeared. Even if they alone remain on earth, they will live. Their cheeks will not pale, nor will their sweet voices weaken.” Our eldest understood something of what was happening around him. At times he solemnly asked me why there was so much devastation. But he was only ten, and the joy of childhood soon drove these untimely worries from his face. Evelyn, a laughing cherub, a playful little child without any notion of pain or grief, would shake his fair curls away from his eyes and fill the halls with laughter. In a thousand innocent ways, he drew our attention to his games. Clara, our beautiful, gentle Clara, supported, comforted, and delighted us. She devoted herself to nursing the sick, consoling the grieving, helping the elderly, and joining the young in their games to awaken their happiness. She moved through the rooms like a good spirit sent from heaven to brighten our dark hour with an otherworldly light. Gratitude and praise followed wherever she went. Yet when she stood before us in simple modesty, playing with our children or attentively doing small kindnesses for Idris, we wondered where in her pure, lovely features or the soft notes of her moving voice so much courage, wisdom, and energetic goodness could reside.

The summer dragged on, for we trusted that winter would at least slow the disease. The hope that it might disappear entirely was too precious and deeply felt to speak aloud. Whenever someone carelessly voiced it, the listeners burst into tears and passionate sobs, revealing how great their fears were and how slight their hopes."}

{"request_id":"309284c64c0f336ddef760ef","rewritten_text":"Four days after these strange events, a funeral procession left Canterville Chase at about eleven at night. Eight black horses pulled the hearse, each wearing an enormous tuft of swaying ostrich feathers on its head. A sumptuous purple cloth covered the lead coffin, with the Canterville coat of arms embroidered on it in gold. Servants carrying flaming torches walked beside the hearse and carriages, making the entire procession remarkably impressive. Lord Canterville, who had travelled from Wales especially for the funeral, was the principal mourner and rode in the first carriage with little Virginia. The United States Minister and his wife followed, then Washington and the three boys, while Mrs. Umney occupied the last carriage. Everyone generally agreed that, after being frightened by the ghost for over fifty years, she deserved to see the last of him. A deep grave had been prepared in a corner of the churchyard, directly beneath the old yew tree, and the Reverend Augustus Dampier conducted the service with great solemnity. After the ceremony, following an ancient Canterville family custom, the servants put out their torches. As the coffin was lowered, Virginia stepped forward and placed on it a large cross of white and pink almond blossoms. At that moment the moon emerged from behind a cloud, bathing the little churchyard in silent silver light, and a nightingale began singing in a distant grove. Remembering the ghost’s account of the Garden of Death, she felt tears blur her eyes and spoke scarcely a word on the journey home.

The following morning, before Lord Canterville left for London, Mr. Otis spoke with him about the jewels the ghost had given Virginia. They were truly magnificent, particularly a ruby necklace in an old Venetian setting, an exquisite example of sixteenth-century craftsmanship. Their immense value made Mr. Otis seriously question whether he could let his daughter accept them.

‘My lord,’ he said, ‘I understand that in this country the rules of mortmain, governing permanently held property, apply to ornaments as well as land. It seems perfectly clear that these jewels are, or ought to be, family heirlooms of yours. I must therefore ask you to take them to London and consider them simply part of your property, restored to you in unusual circumstances. As for my daughter, she is still only a child, and I am pleased to say she has so far shown little interest in accessories of this sort of idle luxury."}

{"request_id":"ae8a2f0c1a25cc50ed129647","rewritten_text":"Few people had such a pure heart or combined such true humility with so firm a trust in their own honesty and the honesty of others. She had led a secluded life from birth. Her mother died when she was very young, but her father devoted himself to her education. He held many unusual ideas, and these shaped the way he taught her. She knew the heroes of Greece and Rome well, along with English heroes who had lived centuries earlier, yet she knew almost nothing about the events of her own day. She had read few writers from the past fifty years or more, though apart from this gap she had read very widely. So, although she seemed to understand less about life and society than he did, her knowledge went deeper and rested on stronger foundations. Even if her beauty and kindness had failed to fascinate him, her intelligence would always have held his mind captive. He looked to her for guidance. He adored her so much that he took pleasure in strengthening his own sense of being inferior to her, a feeling she sometimes inspired.

His mother died when he was nineteen. He left college after her death and, withdrawing for a time from his old friends, went to live near his Diana. Her gentle voice and still more precious caresses gave him all his comfort. This brief separation from his companions gave him the courage to claim his independence. He felt that, however much they might ridicule his planned marriage, they would not dare to do so once he was married. He therefore asked his guardian’s permission, which he obtained with some difficulty, and the permission of his beloved’s father, who agreed more readily. Without telling anyone else what he intended, he became Diana’s husband by the time he reached his twentieth birthday.

He loved her passionately, and her tenderness had such power over him that he could think of nothing but her. He invited some college friends to visit, but their shallow behaviour disgusted him. Diana had lifted the veil that had kept him a boy. Now he was a man, and he wondered how he could ever have shared his fellow students’ empty phrases and ideas, or feared their disapproval even for a moment. He gave up his old friendships because those friends were truly unworthy of him, rather than from any tendency to change his affections."}

{"request_id":"bdbb6c1fc6121c9c922f03b7","rewritten_text":"“By eight o’clock I had in fact reached a height of seventeen miles above the earth’s surface. It appeared plain to me, therefore, that my speed of ascent was increasing, and that some slight acceleration would have been perceptible even without my having thrown out the ballast. The pain in my head and ears came back violently from time to time, and my nose still occasionally bled; yet, taken altogether, my sufferings were considerably less than one might have anticipated. Breathing, however, grew more difficult with every moment, and each breath was accompanied by an annoying convulsive movement of the chest. I now took out the condensing apparatus and prepared it for instant use.

“The prospect of the earth at this stage of my ascent was truly lovely. To the west, north, and south, stretching as far as sight could reach, lay an unlimited expanse of seemingly placid ocean, whose blue grew deeper with each moment and which was already beginning to show a faint convexity. Far away to the east, though quite clearly distinguishable, stretched the islands of Great Britain, all the Atlantic shores of France and Spain, and a little of the northern African continent. No vestige of a single building was visible, and mankind’s most magnificent cities had vanished entirely from the earth’s face. From the rock of Gibraltar, diminished now to an indistinct speck, the dark Mediterranean extended eastward to the limit of my sight, sprinkled with bright islands as the heavens are sprinkled with stars. At last its whole volume of water appeared to plunge headlong into the gulf beyond the horizon, and I caught myself listening on tiptoe for the reverberations of that tremendous waterfall. Above me the sky was jet-black, and the stars shone with extraordinary brilliance.

“About this time the pigeons appeared to be suffering greatly, and I resolved to set them free. I first released one from its bonds, a handsome pigeon mottled with gray, and set him on the edge of the wicker basket. He seemed exceedingly distressed, glancing anxiously about, fluttering his wings, and cooing loudly, but nothing would induce him to venture away from the car. Finally I picked him up and tossed him some six yards clear of the balloon. Instead of trying to descend, as I had supposed he would, he struggled most violently to return, uttering cries of great shrillness and intensity as he did so. In the end he regained his previous perch on the rim; but scarcely had he reached it before his head sank against his breast and he dropped dead inside the car.”"}

{"request_id":"6c1d01f17aedde27acd845e5","rewritten_text":"I thought this curious lie had been told with wonderful simplicity and grace. But I had only heard it once, and that makes a difference. No doubt the others had enjoyed it when it was new.

Sir Dinadan the Humorist woke first and soon roused everyone else with a rather feeble practical joke. He tied several metal mugs to a dog’s tail and let him loose. The terrified animal raced around the room, with all the other dogs barking after him. They slammed into everything in their path, creating complete confusion and a deafening racket. Every man and woman in the crowd laughed until tears ran down their faces, and some fell out of their chairs and rolled on the floor in delight. They were just like a crowd of children. Sir Dinadan was so proud of what he had done that he kept explaining, over and over until it became tiresome, how this immortal idea had come to him. Like other humorists of his kind, he was still laughing after everyone else had finished. His success pleased him so much that he decided to give a speech—a funny speech, naturally. I don’t think I had ever heard so many exhausted old jokes strung together. He was worse than the minstrels, worse than a circus clown. It seemed especially sad to be sitting here, thirteen hundred years before my birth, hearing the same dull, decaying jokes that had made my stomach ache when I was a boy thirteen hundred years later. It almost persuaded me that a new joke was impossible. Everyone laughed at these ancient things—but people always do; I had noticed that centuries afterward. Of course, the mocker didn’t laugh—I mean the boy. No, he sneered; there was nothing he wouldn’t sneer at. He said most of Sir Dinadan’s jokes were rotten, and the rest had turned to stone. I liked that idea, since I thought myself that geological periods were the only proper way to measure the majestic age of some of those jokes. But this clever observation meant nothing to the boy, because geology hadn’t been invented yet. Still, I wrote the remark down and planned, if I survived, to educate the country until it could appreciate it. There is no sense in throwing away something good simply because the public isn’t ready for it.

Now Sir Kay got up and began stoking the machinery of his storytelling, using me as fuel."}

{"request_id":"b0b0c8c08fc30003bc8f5941","rewritten_text":"We spent several miserable hours waiting until eleven, when the trial was due to begin. My father and the rest of the family had to appear as witnesses, and I went with them to court. Throughout that wretched imitation of justice, I endured a living torture. The question was whether my curiosity and reckless experiments would cause two deaths: one victim a smiling child, innocent and joyful, the other murdered still more terribly, under a burden of disgrace that would make the killing unforgettable in its horror. Justine was a worthy young woman, with qualities that should have ensured a happy life. Now everything would be buried in a shameful grave, and I would be responsible. A thousand times I would rather have confessed to the crime myself. But I had been absent when it occurred; such a confession would have been dismissed as a madman’s ravings and would not have cleared the woman suffering because of me.

Justine appeared calm. She wore mourning clothes, and the seriousness of her feelings made her always attractive face exquisitely beautiful. She seemed to draw confidence from her innocence and did not tremble, although thousands stared at her and cursed her. The spectators’ belief in the terrible crime she supposedly committed erased any sympathy her beauty might otherwise have inspired. Yet her composure was visibly forced. Her earlier confusion had been presented as evidence of guilt, so she made herself show courage. On entering the courtroom, she looked around and quickly found where we sat. A tear seemed to cloud her eye at the sight of us, but she soon regained control. Her expression of sorrowful affection seemed to declare her complete innocence.

The trial opened. After the prosecutor stated the charge, several witnesses were called. A number of strange circumstances worked against her, enough to shake anyone who lacked my certainty of her innocence. She had been out all night when the murder occurred. Near morning, a market woman had seen her close to the place where the child’s body was later discovered. Asked what she was doing there, she had looked very strange and given only a confused, incomprehensible reply. She reached the house at about eight. When someone asked where she had spent the night, she said she had been searching for the child and urgently asked whether there was any news of him."}

{"request_id":"79efd7c58c584d2c0f2ad512","rewritten_text":"The following day he stayed indoors, spending most of it in his room, sick with an overwhelming fear of death and yet indifferent to living. The sense that he was being hunted, trapped, and pursued had begun to take possession of him. A mere flutter of the tapestry in the wind made him tremble. Dead leaves striking the leaded windows seemed like his own squandered resolutions and desperate regrets. Whenever he shut his eyes, he saw the sailor’s face again, peering through the misted glass, and felt horror once more grip his heart.

Perhaps, though, his imagination alone had summoned vengeance from the darkness and placed those dreadful forms of punishment before him. Real life was chaotic, but imagination had a terrifying logic. Imagination made remorse follow at the heels of sin. Imagination gave every crime its deformed offspring. In the ordinary world of facts, the wicked went unpunished and the good unrewarded. The strong received success; failure was forced upon the weak. That was all. Besides, if a stranger had been prowling around the house, the servants or gamekeepers would have noticed him. If there had been footprints in the flower beds, the gardeners would have reported them. Yes, it had only been imagination. Sibyl Vane’s brother had not returned to murder him. He had sailed off in his ship to sink in some winter sea. From that man, at least, he was safe. After all, the man did not know who he was, could not possibly know. His youthful appearance had been a mask that saved him.

Yet if it had only been an illusion, how dreadful to think that conscience could summon such terrifying apparitions, give them a visible shape, and make them move before his eyes! What would his life become if, night and day, shadows of his crime peered out at him from quiet corners, mocked him from hidden places, whispered into his ear while he sat at a feast, and woke him with freezing fingers while he slept? As the thought moved through his mind, his face grew white with terror, and the air seemed suddenly colder. Oh, what a frenzied hour of madness had driven him to kill his friend! How appalling even the memory of that scene was! He saw the whole thing again. Every hideous detail returned with fresh horror. From the black cavern of time, terrible and wrapped in scarlet, the image of his sin arose."}

{"request_id":"df5908a2c624c544f6e170a3","rewritten_text":"What sort of existence could they share while Fate continued to weigh this dreadful destiny in her balance? At whatever cost, the wedding must be deferred. On that point his determination was absolute. Though he loved the girl fervently, though the lightest contact with her fingers as they sat side by side sent delicious happiness thrilling through every nerve, he saw his duty no less plainly. He understood entirely that he possessed no right to marry before accomplishing the murder. Once it was done, he might stand at the altar beside Sybil Merton and entrust his life to her without fear of misconduct. Once it was done, he could fold her in his arms, certain that she would never blush on his account or bow her head for shame. But first it had to be accomplished, and the sooner it was, the better for them both.

Many men similarly situated would have chosen the flower-strewn road of pleasure over duty’s arduous heights. Lord Arthur, however, was too scrupulous to place enjoyment before principle. His love contained something beyond simple passion; to him Sybil represented everything noble and good. For an instant he felt a natural revulsion at the deed required of him, but the feeling quickly vanished. His heart assured him that this was sacrifice rather than sin, while reason pointed out that no alternative remained. The choice lay between an existence devoted to himself and one devoted to others. Undeniably appalling though his appointed task might be, he knew he must never let selfishness conquer love. All of us must eventually confront this same decision; the same question comes to everyone. For Lord Arthur it arrived early, before the calculating cynicism of middle age had corrupted his character, or the shallow fashionable selfishness of the present day had eaten into his heart. He therefore felt no reluctance to fulfil his duty. Happily for him, too, he was neither a mere visionary nor a lounging dilettante. Such a man would have faltered like Hamlet, allowing indecision to spoil his intention. Lord Arthur was, at bottom, practical. To him life signified action more than reflection. He possessed the rarest quality of all, common sense.

By now the previous night’s turbulent, disordered emotions had wholly subsided. He remembered his frantic roaming from one street to another and his violent anguish with something approaching embarrassment. Precisely because his suffering had been so sincere, it now seemed scarcely real. He marvelled that he could have behaved so foolishly as to rage and declaim against what could not be avoided."}

{"request_id":"0fbdc7cf2398e3052343133c","rewritten_text":"It now placed my altitude at 132,000 feet, or twenty-five miles, which meant that I could survey no less than one three-hundred-and-twentieth of the earth’s total surface. By nine o’clock the land to the east had vanished again, though before losing it I noticed that the balloon was drifting quickly north-northwest. The curvature of the ocean beneath me was unmistakable, despite the clouds moving back and forth across my view. I now observed that even the faintest wisps of vapour never rose higher than ten miles above sea level.

“At half past nine I experimented by releasing a handful of feathers through the valve. Instead of floating as I expected, they fell straight down together, like a bullet, at tremendous speed. Within a few seconds they had disappeared. At first I could make no sense of this extraordinary sight: I could hardly believe that my upward speed had suddenly increased so enormously. Then I realised that the air had become too thin to support even feathers. They really were falling as rapidly as they appeared, and what had startled me was the combined effect of their downward movement and my own ascent.

“By ten o’clock I had very little that required immediate attention. Everything was proceeding beautifully, and I believed the balloon was climbing faster with every moment, although I no longer had a way to measure the rate of acceleration. I felt neither pain nor discomfort of any kind. My spirits were better than at any time since leaving Rotterdam. I occupied myself by checking my various pieces of equipment and renewing the air inside the chamber. I decided to refresh it regularly every forty minutes, chiefly to safeguard my health, rather than because such frequent renewal was strictly necessary. Meanwhile, I could not resist imagining what lay ahead. My fancy delighted in the wild and dreamlike landscapes of the moon. For once free of restraint, my imagination wandered wherever it pleased through the endlessly changing marvels of a shadowy, shifting country. At one moment I saw ancient, venerable forests, jagged cliffs, and waterfalls crashing thunderously into bottomless chasms. Then, abruptly, I found myself in the stillness of deserted places at noon, where no breath of wind ever entered. Immense meadows of poppies and slender flowers resembling lilies stretched into an exhausting distance, everything forever silent and motionless."}

{"request_id":"e6e19348aab9bb0923e66837","rewritten_text":"Madame and Mademoiselle L’Espanaye were not killed by ghosts. Whoever committed the murders had a physical body and escaped by physical means. But how? Fortunately, there is only one way to reason through this question, and it must bring us to a definite answer. Let us consider every possible exit in turn. Clearly, when the group came upstairs, the killers were in the room where Mademoiselle L’Espanaye was found, or at least in the next room. We therefore need to look for exits only from those two rooms. The police have thoroughly exposed the floors, ceilings, and walls. No secret passage could have escaped their attention. Still, I did not rely on their eyes; I examined everything myself. There were no secret exits. Both doors into the hallway were firmly locked, with the keys on the inside. Now consider the chimneys. Though they are of normal width for eight or ten feet above the fireplaces, farther up they narrow so much that even a large cat could not pass through. Since escape by any of these routes was absolutely impossible, only the windows remain. No one could have escaped through the front-room windows without being seen by the crowd in the street. The murderers must therefore have used the back-room windows. Having reached this conclusion so clearly, we cannot reject it simply because it appears impossible. Our task is to show that these apparent ‘impossibilities’ are actually possible.

“There are two windows in the bedroom. No furniture blocks the first, which is completely visible. The lower part of the other is hidden by the head of the large, heavy bed, pushed right against it. The first window was found securely fastened inside. Those who tried to raise it could not move it, however hard they pulled. A large hole had been drilled into the left side of its frame, and a very thick nail had been driven into it almost to the head. When the other window was examined, a similar nail was found in the same position. A forceful attempt to raise that window also failed. The police were then entirely convinced that no one had escaped through either window. They consequently thought it unnecessary to remove the nails and open them.

“My own examination was more thorough, for exactly the reason I have just explained. I knew that here, at these windows, everything that appeared impossible had to be shown possible in fact."}

{"request_id":"c7ba708943c5cf108dea5026","rewritten_text":"Elizabeth had grown sorrowful and dispirited. Her customary pursuits no longer pleased her; enjoyment of any kind seemed an offense against the dead. She believed endless grief and weeping the fitting homage to innocence so cruelly ruined. Gone was the happy girl who once wandered beside the lake with me and spoke rapturously of our future. The first of those afflictions sent to loosen our hold upon the world had reached her, and its darkening influence put out her loveliest smiles.

“When I consider, dear cousin,” she said, “the wretched death of Justine Moritz, the world and all its doings look quite different to me. Formerly, the wickedness and injustice I encountered in books or others’ accounts seemed stories of bygone times or imagined evils. At least they were distant, things understood by reason rather than felt by imagination. Now suffering has entered our own home, and people seem monsters thirsting for one another’s blood. Yet surely I judge them unfairly. Everyone thought that poor girl guilty. Had she committed the crime for which she died, she would indeed have been among the most depraved creatures alive. To kill her benefactor and friend’s son for a few jewels—a child she had tended since birth and apparently loved as her own! I could never approve the death of any person, but I should certainly have judged such a being unworthy of human society. Yet she was innocent. I know it, I feel it; and your agreement strengthens my conviction. Alas, Victor, if falsehood can resemble truth so closely, who can count upon secure happiness? I feel myself upon the brink of a cliff, with thousands pressing towards me to cast me into the gulf. William and Justine have been murdered, and their murderer escapes. He wanders freely through the world, perhaps held in esteem. But even were I sentenced to the scaffold for those same crimes, I would never exchange my place for his.”

Her words caused me the most dreadful anguish. Though not by my own hand, I was in effect the real murderer. Elizabeth saw my torment in my face. Taking my hand affectionately, she said, “My dearest friend, you must grow calm. God knows how profoundly these events have wounded me, but I am not so miserable as you."}

{"request_id":"8460328abca9a30d600f76e0","rewritten_text":"“Some time elapsed before I recovered enough composure to attend to the balloon’s ordinary needs. I then inspected it closely and, to my immense relief, discovered no damage. All my instruments were secure, and neither provisions nor ballast had been lost. Indeed, I had fastened them so firmly in place that such a mishap was impossible. My watch showed six o’clock. I continued to rise swiftly, and the barometer indicated a height of three and three-quarter miles. Directly below, upon the ocean, appeared a little black object, somewhat oblong, seemingly the size and very image of the childish plaything called a domino. Through my telescope I clearly recognized a British ship mounting ninety-four guns, sailing close to the wind and pitching heavily, her head pointed west-southwest. Apart from this vessel, nothing met my eyes but sea, sky, and the sun, which had risen long before.

“It is time, your Excellencies, that I explained the purpose of my dangerous journey. You will recall that my desperate circumstances in Rotterdam had finally determined me upon suicide. I felt no actual loathing for life itself; rather, the incidental sufferings of my situation had harassed me past endurance. Wishing to live, yet exhausted by living, I found in the treatise at the bookseller’s stall an opening for my imagination. My decision was at last complete. I would depart and nevertheless remain alive; abandon the world and still exist. To speak without riddles, I resolved, whatever the consequences, to make my way to the moon if I could. Lest you suppose me madder than I really am, I shall explain as fully as possible the arguments that persuaded me such an undertaking, though certainly difficult and undeniably hazardous, did not lie wholly beyond the reach of a courageous spirit.

“The first consideration was the moon’s true distance from the earth. The average separation between the centres of these two planets is 59.9643 terrestrial equatorial radii, or merely some 237,000 miles. I say the average separation, for it must be remembered that the moon follows an ellipse whose eccentricity amounts to no less than 0.05484 of its major semi-axis, with the earth’s centre placed at one focus. Thus, if I could somehow arrange to encounter the moon at perigee, as it were, the distance just stated would be considerably reduced."}

{"request_id":"98bb5ae41ccb61d395865cad","rewritten_text":"Among the public edifices of a certain town, which for numerous reasons prudence advises me not to name, and for which I shall invent no name either, stands a building once usual in towns both large and small: namely, a workhouse. In that workhouse there was born, upon a day and date I need not weary myself by specifying, since they can matter nothing to the reader, at this point in our affairs at least, the mortal individual whose name appears at the beginning of this chapter.

For a considerable while after the parish surgeon introduced him to this world of grief and vexation, it remained seriously uncertain whether the infant would live to possess any name whatever. Had he not, these memoirs would most probably never have been published; or, if published, their compass of a page or two would have bestowed upon them the priceless distinction of being the briefest and most accurate biography in the literature of any nation or period.

I do not intend to argue that entering the world in a workhouse is, of itself, the luckiest and most enviable event that can happen to a human creature. I do assert, however, that in this particular case nothing could possibly have served Oliver Twist better. The truth is that Oliver proved exceedingly reluctant to undertake the business of breathing, a tiresome exercise which habit has made indispensable to comfortable existence. For some time he gasped upon a little flock mattress, suspended rather unevenly between this world and the next, with the balance distinctly inclining towards the latter. Had Oliver, during this short interval, been encircled by solicitous grandmothers, nervous aunts, practised nurses, and physicians of immense wisdom, they would certainly and unquestionably have killed him almost immediately. As it happened, no one attended him except an aged pauper woman, somewhat befuddled by an unusual ration of beer, and a parish surgeon who handled such affairs under contract. Oliver and Nature were therefore left to settle the dispute themselves. Consequently, after struggling a few times, Oliver drew breath, sneezed, and announced to the workhouse inhabitants that the parish had acquired a fresh burden. He did so with as powerful a wail as could reasonably be demanded of a male baby whose possession of that highly serviceable accessory, a voice, had scarcely exceeded three minutes and a quarter."}

{"request_id":"77327c07ea61e259e45b4269","rewritten_text":"The only difficulty that seemed to concern him was choosing whom to kill. He understood that murder, like pagan religion, required a victim as well as a priest. Since he was no genius, he had no enemies. Besides, he felt this was no occasion to indulge personal resentment or dislike; his mission was too deeply serious and solemn. He therefore listed his friends and relatives on a sheet of writing paper. After careful thought, he chose Lady Clementina Beauchamp, a dear old woman living in Curzon Street, and his second cousin on his mother’s side. He had always loved Lady Clem, as everyone called her. Since he was wealthy himself, having inherited all Lord Rugby’s property when he came of age, her death could offer him no vulgar financial advantage. Indeed, the more he considered it, the more perfectly suitable she seemed. Feeling that any delay would be unfair to Sybil, he decided to begin making arrangements immediately.

First, of course, he had to pay the palm reader. He sat at a small Sheraton writing desk by the window and wrote a check for £105 to Mr. Septimus Podgers. After placing it in an envelope, he instructed his valet to deliver it to West Moon Street. Then he telephoned the stables for his hansom cab and dressed to go out. As he left the room, he looked back at Sybil Merton’s photograph and vowed that, whatever happened, he would never reveal what he was doing for her. He would keep the secret of his self-sacrifice forever hidden in his heart.

On his way to the Buckingham, he stopped at a florist and sent Sybil a beautiful basket of narcissus flowers, their lovely white petals surrounding bright pheasant’s-eye centers. At the club he went directly to the library, rang the bell, and asked the waiter for a lemon-and-soda and a book on poisons. He had decided that poison was the best way to handle this troublesome business. Any sort of physical violence disgusted him. He was also anxious to avoid killing Lady Clementina in a way that attracted public attention, since he hated the thought of becoming the center of interest at Lady Windermere’s or seeing his name in the columns of vulgar society newspapers."}

{"request_id":"f5ae0e2fed7ce52617066805","rewritten_text":"They constituted her life, dearer than any place hallowed by love, dearer than everything else upon the earth. The boys greeted the news of our departure with childish delight; Clara inquired whether we were bound for Athens. “Perhaps,” I answered, and pleasure illuminated her face. There she would see her parents’ tomb and the land rich in memories of her father’s glory. Silently and ceaselessly she had dwelt upon those scenes. Remembering them had transformed her childhood gaiety into gravity and filled her with lofty, restless thoughts.

Many beloved companions must accompany us, however humble they might be. There was the spirited yet obedient horse Lord Raymond had presented to his daughter; there were Alfred’s dog and a tame eagle whose vision age had clouded. Yet we could not draw up this list of favourites without grieving for our great losses and sighing deeply over all that must remain. Idris’s eyes filled with tears as Alfred and Evelyn brought first a treasured rose tree, then an exquisitely carved marble vase, declaring that each must come. They lamented our inability to carry away the castle and forest, the deer and birds, and every familiar, cherished thing. “Dear, foolish children,” I said, “we have already lost for ever treasures infinitely more valuable than these, and we abandon these things to save treasures beside which they count as nothing. Let us never forget our purpose and our hope; they will raise an unassailable barrier against this flood of sorrow over trifles.”

The children were readily diverted and went back to anticipating their future pleasures. Idris was gone. She had withdrawn to conceal her weakness; leaving the castle, she had descended into the little park and sought a lonely place where she could surrender to her tears. I discovered her embracing an ancient oak, her rosy lips pressed against its rugged trunk, while tears streamed freely and sobs and broken cries escaped her control. With overwhelming grief I saw the beloved of my heart so entirely overcome by sorrow! I drew her to me; feeling my kisses upon her eyelids and my arms enclosing her, she awoke again to the knowledge of what she still possessed. “You are so kind not to rebuke me,” she said. “I weep, and a sharp pang of unbearable grief rends my heart. Yet I am happy: mothers mourn their children, wives are bereaved of husbands, while you and my children remain to me.”"}

{"request_id":"d2b64884eb7e345ab1bc679e","rewritten_text":"I next pierced the end of the keg above the pitcher and inserted a soft wooden stopper, shaped to a taper or cone. By pushing it farther in or drawing it out, I soon discovered the precise tightness at which the water leaking through would fill the vessel below in sixty minutes. This was readily and quickly established by observing what fraction of the pitcher filled in a measured interval. With these arrangements complete, the remainder of my design is plain. I laid out my bed upon the car’s floor so that, when reclining, my head rested directly beneath the pitcher’s spout. At the end of an hour, the vessel would plainly overflow; and it would do so through the spout, which sat a little below the rim. Equally plainly, water falling more than four feet from that position must strike my face, waking me instantly even from the deepest possible sleep.

“It was already eleven when I finished these preparations. I went immediately to bed, entirely assured of my contrivance’s success, and in this I suffered no disappointment. Every sixty minutes precisely, my faithful timekeeper awakened me. I emptied the pitcher back through the keg’s bung-hole, attended to the condenser, and returned to bed. These repeated disturbances troubled me even less than I had expected. When I rose at last for the day, the hour was seven, and the sun stood many degrees above my horizon.

“April 3d. The balloon had reached a truly enormous height, and the earth’s apparent curvature was notably greater. A group of black dots upon the ocean beneath me were undoubtedly islands. Far northward, along the horizon’s edge, I could distinguish a narrow white streak of extraordinary brilliance. I readily took it for the southern edge of the ice covering the Polar Sea. My curiosity was powerfully aroused, since I hoped to travel much farther north and might eventually stand directly above the Pole. I now regretted that, should this occur, my immense altitude would prevent the close examination I desired."}

{"request_id":"c9254f1f6bb1430db4ff3ce4","rewritten_text":"As I reached through the valve with a cup of water for the old cat, my shirtsleeves caught in the loop supporting the basket. In an instant I had pulled it loose from the bottom. It vanished so suddenly that it might as well have dissolved into thin air. I am certain that less than a tenth of a second passed between the basket coming free and its complete disappearance, together with everything inside it. I sent my good wishes after it towards the earth, but of course I had no hope that the cat or her kittens could survive to tell the story of their bad luck.

“At six o’clock I noticed that a large part of the earth visible to the east lay in deep shadow. The darkness spread quickly, and by five minutes to seven it covered the entire surface I could see. Yet the setting sun continued to shine on the balloon for a long time afterwards. Though I had fully expected this, it gave me enormous pleasure. Clearly, in the morning I would see the sun rise at least several hours before the people of Rotterdam, despite their being much farther east. Day after day, as I climbed higher, I would enjoy longer and longer stretches of sunlight. I decided to begin a journal of the journey, counting each day continuously from one hour to twenty-four, without allowing the periods of darkness to affect the count.

“At ten o’clock I felt sleepy and decided to lie down for the rest of the night. Only then did a problem occur to me which, obvious as it may seem, I had completely overlooked until that very moment. If I went to sleep, how would the air inside the chamber be renewed while I slept? I could not possibly keep breathing it for longer than an hour at most. Even if I could stretch that time to an hour and a quarter, the consequences might be disastrous. This problem troubled me greatly. It may be hard to believe that, after all the dangers I had already survived, I took this difficulty so seriously that I abandoned hope of reaching my final goal and decided I would have to descend. But my uncertainty lasted only a moment."}

{"request_id":"b8c3be40488285e8a73c482c","rewritten_text":"I was born in a small corner of the world surrounded by sea, a land beneath cloud shadows. When I picture the globe, with its boundless oceans and pathless continents, my native country seems an insignificant speck within that immense whole. Yet measured by intellectual power, it far outweighed nations with greater territories and much larger populations. Such was the truth that the human mind alone created everything people considered good or great, while Nature herself merely served as its chief assistant. England, far north in the restless sea, now appears in my dreams as an enormous, well-crewed ship, commanding the winds and riding proudly over the waves. As a boy, I knew her as my entire universe. Standing on the hills where I was born, I saw plains and mountains stretching to the limits of sight, dotted with my countrymen’s homes and made fertile by their work. For me, the earth’s centre lay on that spot. The rest of the globe seemed a story I could have forgotten without the slightest strain on imagination or understanding.

From the beginning, my fortunes have shown how powerfully change can shape the varied course of a human life. In my case this condition was almost an inheritance. Nature had lavished upon my father the enviable gifts of wit and imagination, then left the vessel of his life to be driven by those winds without providing reason for a rudder or judgment for a pilot. His family was obscure, but circumstances brought him to public attention early. He soon spent his modest inheritance in the brilliant world of fashionable luxury in which he played his part. During the brief years of his careless youth, the elegant idlers of the day adored him. Among his greatest admirers was the young sovereign, who escaped political intrigue and the demanding responsibilities of kingship to find unfailing amusement and renewed spirits in my father’s company. His impulses, which he never controlled, constantly drew him into trouble from which only his inventiveness could rescue him. The growing burden of gambling debts and tradesmen’s bills would have crushed anyone else, but he bore it with lighthearted, irrepressible gaiety. His presence was so essential at the dinners and gatherings of the wealthy that his failings were treated as minor faults, and he was welcomed with intoxicating praise.

Popularity of this kind, like every kind, soon fades. Meanwhile, the difficulties surrounding him multiplied at a frightening rate compared with the meagre resources he had for escaping them."}

{"request_id":"1a342786ea7cae02daed8c57","rewritten_text":"Do not misunderstand me; I was never truly mad. Even when my tumultuous thoughts seemed to hurry me towards insanity, I remained conscious of my condition, and disclosed them to nothing save solitude and silence. Those about me perceived none of this. They beheld only a poor girl whose spirit was broken, speaking softly and gently, while tears would occasionally escape beneath her lowered eyelids, which she endeavoured to conceal. A girl who sought to be alone and recoiled from notice; who never smiled; ah, no! I did not smile—and that was all.

At last I escaped. I departed from my guardian’s house, and no tidings of me ever followed; the letters I left, together with other circumstances I had contrived, persuaded them that I had taken my own life. Thus the search for me was less diligent than it would otherwise have been, and before long every remembrance and trace of me disappeared. I sailed from London in a little vessel bound for a northern English port. Now that my endeavour had succeeded, and I found myself entirely alone, peace came back to me. The sea lay still, and the ship glided gently onward; sitting on deck beneath heaven’s open canopy, I fancied myself another being. No longer the frantic, raving, most unhappy Mathilda, but a young Hermitess vowed to retirement, whose heart she must labour to preserve from every disturbance and unhallowed despair. The fanciful habit, resembling a nun’s, which I had assumed; the thought that my existence itself was a secret belonging to me alone; the solitude appointed for all my future days, fostered mild thoughts within my injured heart. The air stirring through my hair renewed my strength, and with peaceful eyes I observed the sunlight sparkling upon the waves, and the birds pursuing one another above the water, barely touching it with their feathers. My sleep, too, was free of troubled dreams; I woke restored, to taste once more the quiet of my liberty.

Four days brought us to the harbour for which we had sailed. Unwilling to settle upon the coast, I travelled inland at once. I had already determined the kind of place in which I should dwell. It must be a lonely house upon an extensive plain, remote from any other dwelling: a place where the entire horizon lay before me, and where I might roam a long way untroubled by the appearance of another human being. I bore mankind no hatred, but felt that the peaceful course of my emotions required solitude. I established myself amid a broad waste, upon a desolate heath scattered with stones, between which grew short grass, with a few rushes here and there beside some little pool."}

{"request_id":"52b33628056fd0d0aafc97aa","rewritten_text":"Words cannot express the transport with which I turned from the political quarrels of my country, and the bodily sufferings of far-off lands, towards my dear home, that chosen dwelling of kindness and affection; towards peace and the exchange of every hallowed sympathy. If I had never left Windsor, I could not have felt these things so keenly; but in Greece I had fallen a prey to dread and lamentable change; there, following a season of sorrow and anxiety, I had witnessed the departure of two whose very names stood for virtue and greatness. Yet griefs like these could never penetrate the family circle still remaining to me, whilst we lived quietly, withdrawn within our cherished forest. The advance of years did indeed work some slight alteration here; and time, according to its custom, impressed the signs of mortality upon our hopes and enjoyments. Idris, that most loving wife, sister, and friend, was also a fond and gentle mother. Motherhood was to her no diversion, as it is to many, but a passion. Three children had been born to us; the second had died during my stay in Greece. That loss had mingled sorrow and apprehension with the victorious ecstasies of maternal love. Until then, these small creatures born of her, the youthful inheritors of her fleeting existence, had seemed assured of life; now she feared that the merciless destroyer might carry off her surviving treasures as it had taken their brother. Their slightest sickness wrung her with terror; any separation from them made her wretched. She had stored all her happiness in their frail lives, and maintained an endless vigil lest the stealthy thief should again rob her of these precious jewels. Happily, there was little to justify her alarm. Alfred, who was now nine, was an honest, manly little boy, with a bright forehead, tender eyes, and a disposition at once gentle and independent. Our youngest remained an infant; yet the bloom of health coloured his soft cheek, and his tireless liveliness made our rooms ring with harmless laughter.

Clara was past that age whose speechless ignorance awakened Idris’s fears. She was beloved by Idris and by us all. Intelligence blended in her so happily with innocence, feeling with patience, gravity with unclouded good temper; such surpassing beauty accompanied such winning simplicity, that among our possessions she rested like a pearl within a shrine, a treasure of excellence and wonder.

As winter began, our Alfred, now nine years old, went for the first time to school at Eton. To him this seemed the earliest stride towards manhood, and his pleasure answered to that belief. Sharing studies and diversions brought out his finest qualities: his constant perseverance, his generosity, and his firmness under good command."}

{"request_id":"ea9243dfb58151948085bc83","rewritten_text":"Having done this, I detached a few additional loops from the rim, slipped in another section of cloth, and joined the loosened loops to their corresponding buttons. By proceeding thus, I could introduce the entire upper portion of the bag between the netting and the hoop. Clearly, the hoop must now fall inside the car, while the car's full weight, together with everything it contained, would be sustained solely by the buttons. At first glance this might appear a precarious support; it was, however, nothing of the sort, for the buttons were exceedingly stout in themselves and stood so near together that each bore only a trifling share of the total burden. Indeed, even had the car and its contents weighed three times as much, I should have felt no apprehension. I next lifted the hoop once more inside the gum-elastic covering, supporting it at almost its original elevation with three light poles made ready for this purpose. Naturally, this served to hold the upper part of the bag open and maintain the lower portion of the netting in its proper position. Nothing remained except to secure the mouth of the enclosure, which I easily effected by collecting the folds of the fabric and twisting them tightly together on the inner side with a sort of fixed tourniquet.

“Three round panes of thick yet transparent glass had been set into the sides of the covering now arranged about the car. Through these I could readily survey my surroundings in every horizontal direction. The section of fabric that constituted the bottom contained a fourth window of the same description, aligned with a small opening in the floor of the car itself. Through it I could look directly downwards. But the particular method of fastening the opening above, and the folds it necessarily produced in the fabric, had made it impossible to fit any corresponding arrangement overhead; I could therefore expect no view of objects immediately above me. This was, of course, of slight importance: even if I had contrived to insert a window at the top, the balloon itself would have rendered it useless.

“Some twelve inches beneath one of the side windows there was a round aperture, eight inches across, furnished with a brass rim whose inner edge was cut to receive the turns of a screw. Into this rim I screwed the condenser's large tube, the body of the apparatus remaining, as a matter of course, inside the gum-elastic chamber."}

{"request_id":"d78b4834f16e83b45a2ad3ec","rewritten_text":"‘Oh, it’s nothing! I enjoy it. Money isn’t why I work; my art is everything to me.’

Lord Arthur placed four pounds, two shillings and sixpence on the table, thanked the little German for his kindness, and, after managing to turn down an invitation to meet some Anarchists over a substantial tea the next Saturday, left the house and headed for the Park.

He spent the following two days intensely excited, and at noon on Friday drove to the Buckingham to wait for news. Throughout the afternoon the impassive hall porter continued to put up telegrams from different parts of the country reporting race results, divorce verdicts, weather conditions, and similar matters. Meanwhile, the ticker delivered tedious particulars of an overnight session in the House of Commons and a minor panic on the Stock Exchange. The evening papers arrived at four, and Lord Arthur retreated into the library carrying the Pall Mall, the St. James’s, the Globe, and the Echo. This deeply offended Colonel Goodchild, who was eager to read accounts of the speech he had given that morning at the Mansion House about South African Missions and the desirability of appointing black Bishops in every province, and who, for some reason, strongly disliked the Evening News. Yet none of these papers made even a passing reference to Chichester, and Lord Arthur concluded that the attempt had failed. The disappointment was devastating, and for a while he lost his composure entirely. He visited Herr Winckelkopf the following day and received profuse apologies, together with an offer of another clock at no charge, or a case of nitroglycerine bombs at cost. But Lord Arthur’s faith in explosives had vanished, and Herr Winckelkopf himself admitted that so many products were adulterated these days that even pure dynamite was almost impossible to obtain. Although the little German conceded that something must have malfunctioned in the mechanism, he still hoped the clock might explode. He cited a barometer he had once sent the military Governor of Odessa: set to go off after ten days, it had waited something like three months before exploding. Admittedly, when it finally did explode, its only achievement was to blow a housemaid to pieces, since the Governor had left town six weeks earlier. Nevertheless, the incident at least demonstrated that dynamite, when its destructive power was governed by machinery, remained a potent force, even if it could not always be relied on for punctuality."}

{"request_id":"b485c71ca040aabe796a55ad","rewritten_text":"‘Oh, it is nothing! I enjoy it. I do not work for money; my art is my whole life.’

Lord Arthur placed four pounds, two shillings and sixpence on the table and thanked the little German for his kindness. After managing to refuse an invitation to meet some Anarchists over a meal of meat and tea the following Saturday, he left the house and headed for the Park.

For the next two days he was intensely excited. At noon on Friday he drove to the Buckingham to await news. Throughout the afternoon the unruffled hall-porter posted telegrams from around the country, reporting horse-race results, decisions in divorce cases, the weather, and similar matters. Meanwhile, the news machine ticked out tedious accounts of an overnight session in the House of Commons and a minor panic on the Stock Exchange. The evening newspapers arrived at four, and Lord Arthur carried the Pall Mall, the St. James's, the Globe and the Echo into the library. This greatly annoyed Colonel Goodchild, who wanted to read reports of the speech he had given that morning at the Mansion House. He had spoken about missions in South Africa and the benefits of appointing black bishops in every province, and for some reason he strongly disliked the Evening News. But none of the papers made even a passing mention of Chichester, and Lord Arthur concluded that his attempt had failed. This was a dreadful blow, and for a while he felt completely shaken. The next day he visited Herr Winckelkopf, who apologized at length and offered him a replacement clock for nothing, or a case of nitroglycerine bombs at cost. Lord Arthur, however, had lost all confidence in explosives. Herr Winckelkopf himself admitted that nowadays so many things were mixed with inferior ingredients that even pure dynamite was hard to obtain. Though the little German agreed that something must have failed in the mechanism, he still hoped the clock might explode. He gave the example of a barometer he had once sent to the military Governor of Odessa. It was set to explode after ten days, but had waited something like three months. Admittedly, when it finally went off, it had only blown a housemaid to pieces, since the Governor had left town six weeks earlier. Still, the incident at least proved that dynamite controlled by machinery was a powerful instrument of destruction, even if its punctuality left something to be desired."}

{"request_id":"dbc88ce492944dcda7a9a4be","rewritten_text":"When the Otis family assembled for breakfast the following morning, they talked of the ghost at considerable length. The United States Minister was naturally somewhat vexed that his gift had been refused. ‘I certainly have no desire,’ he remarked, ‘to inflict any personal harm upon the ghost; and, considering how many years he has occupied the house, I must say I regard throwing pillows at him as extremely discourteous’—an entirely reasonable observation which, I regret to relate, made the twins roar with laughter. ‘On the other hand,’ he went on, ‘should he really refuse to employ the Rising Sun Lubricator, we shall be obliged to confiscate his chains. Nobody could possibly sleep with that sort of din outside the bedrooms.’

During the remainder of the week, however, nothing disturbed them. Only the blood-stain, perpetually reappearing on the library floor, attracted notice. This was decidedly singular, since Mr. Otis invariably locked the door at night and the windows remained securely barred. The stain’s chameleon-like colouring also provoked much discussion. On certain mornings it showed a dull red, almost Indian red; then it became vermilion, then a deep purple. Once, when they descended for family prayers, performed according to the plain observances of the Free American Reformed Episcopalian Church, it confronted them in vivid emerald-green. Such kaleidoscopic alterations naturally afforded the household immense amusement, and wagers concerning them were freely laid each evening. Little Virginia alone declined to share the joke. For some reason nobody could explain, the blood-stain always made her very unhappy, and on the morning of its emerald-green appearance she all but wept.

The ghost made his second appearance on Sunday night. Soon after the family had retired, a tremendous crash in the hall startled them. They hurried downstairs and discovered that a great suit of ancient armour had slipped from its support and tumbled onto the stone floor. Meanwhile the Canterville ghost sat in a chair with a high back, rubbing his knees, his face expressive of the keenest suffering. The twins, who had fetched their pea-shooters, instantly fired two pellets at him with that precision obtainable only through prolonged and diligent practice upon a writing-master. At the same moment the United States Minister pointed his revolver at him and, in obedience to Californian manners, commanded him to put up his hands. The ghost sprang to his feet with a furious shriek and streamed through their midst like vapour, putting out Washington Otis’s candle in passing and leaving the entire company in utter darkness."}

{"request_id":"532679c952ffeacaf3f3c682","rewritten_text":"Lay down your weapons, fellow human beings! Brothers! Repent, and you will find forgiveness, help, and brotherly love waiting for you. We cherish you because you share our fragile human form; every one of you will find a friend and a place to stay among these troops. Must we turn against one another while plague, our common enemy, hangs over us even now, exulting in a slaughter more cruel than its own?”

Both armies stopped. On our side, the soldiers kept a firm grip on their weapons and watched the enemy with hard eyes. The others still held their weapons too, more from fear than any desire to fight. They glanced at one another, each hoping someone would give him an example to follow, but they had no leader. Adrian leaped from his horse and approached a man who had just been killed. “He was a man,” he cried, “and now he is dead. Hurry, bind the wounds of the fallen—let no one die. Let no more souls slip through the merciless wounds you have made to stand before God’s throne and tell of brothers killing brothers. Dress their wounds and return them to their friends. Drive from your hearts the savage fury of tigers; throw down those instruments of hatred and cruelty. In this brief pause in the destruction that awaits us, let each man be a brother, protector, and support to another. Put away those bloodstained weapons, and some of you come quickly to tend these wounds.”

While speaking, he knelt and gathered into his arms a man whose life was pouring in warm blood from his side. The wounded man gasped. Both armies had grown so quiet that everyone could hear his moans; every heart, which moments earlier had been fiercely committed to wholesale killing, now trembled between hope and fear for this single man. Adrian pulled off his military sash and wound it around the injured man, but he was too late. The man gave a deep sigh, his head dropped back, and his limbs went limp. “He is dead!” Adrian said as the body slipped from his arms to the earth. He lowered his head in grief and awe. In the death of this one man, the fate of the whole world seemed to hang. Troops on both sides dropped their weapons. Even the veterans cried, and our men extended their hands to their enemies as love and profound friendship flooded every heart. The opposing forces came together, unarmed and hand in hand, speaking only of how they could help one another. Each side repented: one of its earlier cruelties, the other of its recent violence. United now, they followed the General’s orders to make their way towards London."}

{"request_id":"d6df365aefee4e116f25c335","rewritten_text":"The stars were still shining brightly when I woke, and Taurus, high in the southern sky, told me it was midnight. My sleep had been troubled by dreams. I dreamed I had been invited to Timon’s last feast. I arrived hungry, but when the dishes were uncovered, only hot water rose in clouds of steam that offered no satisfaction. I ran from the angry host, who took Raymond’s form. In my fevered imagination, the vessels he threw after me were filled with foul vapour, and my friend’s body twisted into a thousand shapes before growing into an enormous ghost, its forehead marked by plague. The spreading shadow climbed higher and higher, filling the unbreakable dome that arched over the world, holding it together and enclosing it, and then seeming to struggle to burst through. The nightmare became unbearable. With a great effort I shook off sleep and brought my mind back to reason. My first thought was of Perdita. I must return to her and support her, finding in despair whatever comfort might sustain her wounded heart. I must draw her back from the wild extremes of grief through the strict demands of duty and the gentle tenderness of sorrow.

The stars were my only guide. I turned away from the terrible ruins of the Golden City and, after much effort, managed to make my way outside its walls. There I met a company of soldiers. I borrowed a horse from one of them and hurried to my sister. Even in that short time, the plain had changed. The camp had been taken down, and what remained of the scattered army gathered here and there in small groups. Every face was troubled; every movement showed shock and fear.

With a heavy heart I entered the palace and stood there, afraid to move forward, to speak, or even to look. Perdita sat in the middle of the hall on the marble floor, her head bent against her chest, her hair in disorder, her fingers twisting constantly together. She was as pale as the marble, and pain tightened every feature. She noticed me and looked up in question. The faint hope in her glance was agony. The words died before I could speak them, and I felt a dreadful smile twist my lips. She understood my expression. Her head dropped again, and her fingers resumed their restless movement. At last I found my voice, but its sound frightened her. The unhappy girl had understood my look, and nothing could make her want that terrible news put into firm, unchangeable words. Indeed, she seemed to want to turn my thoughts away from it. Rising from the floor, she whispered, ‘Hush! After so much crying, Clara is asleep. We must not wake her.’"}

{"request_id":"4fa6effec4984f295315f25a","rewritten_text":"When I stopped weeping, reason and memory returned, and I began to consider more calmly what had happened and how I ought to act. Only a few hours had passed, yet my circumstances had been utterly transformed. Since morning I had lived through changes that should have taken years. My father was dead to me, and for a moment I felt as though he lay white-haired in his coffin while I, my youth lost to advancing age, mourned a death that had come in its proper season. But that was not the truth. I was still young—oh, far too young—and he was not dead to everyone else. Yet I, in my misery, must never see him or speak to him again. I must flee him more urgently than I would my greatest enemy. Whether alone or in a city, I must never again look upon him. The thought left me breathless with anguish, and seized my imagination so completely that for a time I could pursue no other train of thought. From this day forward, I thought, I would live in the bleakest seclusion. I would go to the Continent and become a nun, not out of religious devotion, since I was not a Catholic, but to shut myself away from the world forever. There I would find solitude in which to weep, where the voices of the living could never reach me.

But my father—my beloved, deeply unhappy father? Would he die? Would he never master the fierce passion that now ruled him without pity? Many, many years from now, when age had extinguished the burning feelings that tormented him, might he become my father again? This thought eased the tension in my brow, and I felt—and wept to feel—a faint, sorrowful smile soften the suffering on my lips. I dared to imagine a better future. Years must pass, but hope would give them wings; and even if they passed slowly, they would still pass, and I had not lost my father forever. Let him endure another sixteen years of lonely wandering. Let him once again pour out his wild complaints to vast forests and thunderous waterfalls in a distant land. Let him again face terrifying dangers and hardships that crush the soul. Let the hot southern sun once more burn his passion-worn cheeks, and the cold night rain fall upon him and chill his blood.

To this life, unhappy father, I condemn you! Go! Spend your days among savage peoples and your nights beneath the open sky! Let your limbs grow weary and your heart grow cold, until every trace of youth within you is dead!"}

{"request_id":"8115a653c329085542b708aa","rewritten_text":"The flickering gas lamps followed a curve far into the distance. Outside a small house enclosed by walls stood a lone hansom cab, its driver sleeping inside. He hurried towards Portland Place, glancing behind him now and then as if afraid someone was following. At the corner of Rich Street, two men were reading a little notice pasted on a billboard. A peculiar curiosity drew him across the road. As he approached, he caught sight of the word ‘Murder’ in black print. He flinched, and his face flushed deeply. The notice offered a reward for information leading to the capture of a man of average height, aged between thirty and forty, wearing a bowler hat, a black coat, and checked trousers, and bearing a scar on his right cheek. He read it repeatedly, wondering whether the miserable man would be caught and how he had acquired the scar. Perhaps one day his own name would be posted on the walls of London. Perhaps there would be a price on his head too.

The thought filled him with a nauseating horror. He spun around and hurried away into the night.

He barely knew where he went. He vaguely remembered wandering through a maze of filthy houses, losing himself in an enormous web of gloomy streets. Dawn was already bright when he finally found himself in Piccadilly Circus. As he walked home towards Belgrave Square, he passed the great wagons heading for Covent Garden. Their drivers wore white smocks and had pleasant, sunburned faces and coarse, curly hair. They strode strongly along, cracking their whips and occasionally calling to one another. A plump boy sat on the back of a huge grey horse at the head of a jingling team, primroses tucked into his battered hat, his little hands gripping the mane as he laughed. Against the morning sky, the heaps of vegetables resembled masses of jade—green jade set against the pink petals of some extraordinary rose. Lord Arthur was strangely moved, though he could not say why. The delicate beauty of dawn seemed to him unbearably poignant. He thought of all the days that begin beautifully and end in storms. And these country people, with their rough, cheerful voices and easygoing manner—what an unusual London they witnessed! A city without the sins of night or the smoke of daytime, pale and ghostly, a deserted town of tombs. He wondered what they made of it, and whether they knew anything of its magnificence and disgrace, its fierce, flame-coloured pleasures and its terrible hunger, or all that it created and ruined between morning and evening."}

{"request_id":"ef234069588405899bc11691","rewritten_text":"All the young people were frantic to visit Lyme. Captain Wentworth spoke of returning there himself; the distance from Uppercross was only seventeen miles, and the weather, though it was November, was tolerably good. In brief, Louisa, the keenest of this eager party, had determined on the excursion. Besides enjoying the satisfaction of her own wishes, she now possessed the notion that perseverance in her chosen course was meritorious, and overcame every inclination of her parents to postpone the visit until summer. To Lyme, therefore, they would go: Charles, Mary, Anne, Henrietta, Louisa, and Captain Wentworth.

Their first unthinking proposal was to set out in the morning and come home that night; but Mr. Musgrove refused this, out of consideration for his horses. On sensible reflection, moreover, a day in mid-November would afford little opportunity to inspect an unfamiliar place once the seven hours demanded by the roads for the journey there and back were subtracted. They were accordingly to remain overnight and return only in time for dinner the following day. Everyone acknowledged this as a substantial improvement. Although they gathered at the Great House for a rather early breakfast and departed precisely on time, it was well after noon when their two carriages began the long descent into Lyme and entered the town’s even steeper street. The four ladies occupied Mr. Musgrove’s coach, while Charles drove Captain Wentworth in his curricle. Clearly there would be scarcely time to look about before the daylight and its warmth disappeared.

Once rooms had been obtained and dinner ordered at an inn, their next business was undoubtedly to walk straight to the sea. They had arrived too late in the season to enjoy whatever entertainments and diversions Lyme offered as a resort. The assembly rooms were closed, nearly every lodger had departed, and scarcely any households remained beyond those permanently resident. Since the buildings themselves offer little to admire, a visitor’s attention must seek the town’s striking position, its chief street seeming almost to rush into the waves; the path to the Cobb, following the curve of the agreeable little bay, which in season is lively with bathing machines and company; the Cobb itself, with its ancient marvels and recent improvements; and the lovely succession of cliffs extending eastward from the town. A most peculiar visitor it would be who found no attractions in Lyme’s immediate surroundings sufficient to inspire the wish for a closer acquaintance."}

{"request_id":"6d1b07c921d06733af7bc625","rewritten_text":"I come from a little corner ringed by sea, a country beneath the shadow of clouds, which, when I picture the globe with its boundless ocean and uncharted continents, seems but a trifling dot in that enormous whole; yet measured by intellectual strength, it greatly surpassed lands of wider territory and more abundant inhabitants. Thus truly did the mind of man alone create whatever was good or great for him, while Nature served only as his chief minister. England, lying far to the north amid the troubled waters, appears in my dreams now as a mighty ship, amply manned, commanding the winds and riding the waves in pride. In childhood she constituted my universe. Standing upon the hills of my birthplace, watching mountains and plains extend to the furthest edge of sight, dotted with my countrymen's homes and rendered fruitful by their toil, I placed the very centre of the earth upon that spot. The remainder of its sphere seemed a mere story, which neither my understanding nor my fancy would have found any difficulty in forgetting.

From their outset my fortunes have illustrated the sway of change over the diverse course of human existence. In my case this was almost an inheritance. My father belonged to those men upon whom nature lavishes the coveted powers of wit and imagination, then abandons their vessel of life to those driving winds, supplying neither reason for a rudder nor judgment to pilot the voyage. He came of obscure parentage, but circumstances soon brought him before the public, and the modest estate inherited from his father quickly vanished in the brilliant world of luxury and fashion where he played his part. Throughout the brief years of careless youth, fashionable idlers worshipped him, not least the young sovereign, who fled party intrigue and the burdens of royal duty to find unfailing entertainment and refreshment in his company. My father's impulses, which he never governed, constantly involved him in embarrassments from which only his ingenuity could rescue him. The rising heap of debts, both to tradesmen and of honour, would have crushed another man; he bore it with buoyant spirits and irrepressible merriment. Meanwhile his presence was so indispensable at the dinners and gatherings of the wealthy that his faults were treated as slight offences, and he was welcomed with flattery that intoxicated him.

Popularity of this sort, like every sort, soon passes. The difficulties of all kinds that confronted him multiplied at an alarming rate beside the scant resources he possessed for escaping them."}

{"request_id":"ad7e7fa736fe83d1327663d9","rewritten_text":"Once this was done, I released a few more loops from the rim, pushed another section of cloth through, and attached those loops to their matching buttons. Working this way, I could fit the bag's entire upper section between the netting and the hoop. The hoop would now clearly fall inside the basket, leaving the buttons alone to support the full weight of the basket and everything in it. At first this might seem unsafe, but it was not. The buttons themselves were very strong, and they sat so close together that each carried only a tiny share of the total weight. In fact, I would not have worried even if the basket and its contents had weighed three times as much. I then lifted the hoop back up inside the rubber covering. Using three lightweight poles made for the purpose, I supported it almost as high as before. This kept the top of the bag spread open and held the lower part of the netting in its proper place. All I still needed to do was seal the opening at the top. That was easily managed: I gathered the folds of material together and twisted them tightly from inside with a fixed device resembling a tourniquet.

“Three round panes of thick, clear glass had been fitted into the sides of the covering that now enclosed the basket. Through them I could easily look around me in every horizontal direction. A fourth window of the same kind was set into the cloth underneath, aligned with a small hole in the basket's floor. This allowed me to look straight down. I had found it impossible, however, to fit a similar window above me. The unusual way the opening was sealed left wrinkles in the cloth that prevented it. I therefore could not expect to see anything directly overhead. This hardly mattered, of course, since even if I had managed to install a window at the top, the balloon itself would have blocked the view and made it useless.

“About a foot below one of the side windows was a round opening eight inches across. It had a brass rim with a screw thread cut into its inner edge. The condenser's large tube screwed into this rim, while the body of the machine remained, of course, inside the rubber chamber."}

{"request_id":"25be68f1598c9a6d19b08571","rewritten_text":"We spent several sorrowful hours waiting for eleven o’clock, when the trial was to begin. My father and the other members of the family were required to appear as witnesses, and I went with them to the court. Throughout that miserable travesty of justice I endured a living torment. The question to be settled was whether my curiosity and unlawful contrivances would bring about the deaths of two fellow creatures: the one a smiling infant, all innocence and happiness; the other murdered in a manner far more dreadful, burdened with every circumstance of disgrace that could stamp the murder with unforgettable horror. Justine, too, was a worthy girl, endowed with qualities that promised her a happy life; now everything was to vanish into a shameful grave, and through my doing! A thousand times would I sooner have declared myself guilty of the crime laid to Justine’s charge; but I had been away when it occurred. Such a confession would have been taken for a madman’s ravings and would not have cleared the girl who suffered on my account.

Justine’s bearing was composed. She wore mourning, and the solemnity of her emotions lent her face, always pleasing, an exquisite beauty. She seemed nevertheless assured of her innocence and did not shake, though thousands stared at her and cursed her. Whatever kindness her beauty might otherwise have awakened had been erased from the spectators’ minds by their conception of the monstrous deed she was believed to have done. She remained calm, but the effort behind her calmness was plain; since her earlier bewilderment had been cited as evidence of guilt, she compelled herself to present an air of courage. On coming into court she glanced about and soon found the place where we sat. At the sight of us a tear appeared to cloud her eye; she mastered herself at once, however, and her expression of mournful tenderness seemed to bear witness to her complete innocence.

The proceedings opened, and when the advocate prosecuting her had set out the accusation, several witnesses were summoned. A number of curious circumstances joined against her, sufficient to unsettle anyone who lacked the proof of her innocence that I possessed. She had stayed out all night when the murder was committed. Near morning a market-woman had seen her close to the place where the murdered child’s body was subsequently discovered. Asked by the woman what she was doing there, she had looked exceedingly strange and given only a bewildered, incomprehensible reply. She reached the house at about eight o’clock; when someone asked where she had spent the night, she answered that she had been searching for the child and anxiously demanded whether there was any news of him."}

{"request_id":"fe2c036344806b41fa9c7039","rewritten_text":"You see, I had stowed my handkerchief in my helmet, along with a few other things. But this was the kind of helmet you couldn't remove without help. That hadn't crossed my mind when I put it there; in fact, I hadn't known. I'd imagined it would be an especially convenient place. Now the thought of it sitting there, so close and apparently handy, yet completely beyond my reach, made everything worse. Yes, what you chiefly want is whatever you can't have; everyone has noticed that. Well, it drove every other thought from my mind and concentrated all my attention inside that helmet. Mile after mile, my thoughts stayed there, imagining the handkerchief, seeing it before me. All the while salty sweat kept running into my eyes, and the cloth I needed was out of reach. It was bitterly frustrating. Written down, this sounds trivial, but it was nothing of the sort. It was misery in its most substantial form. I wouldn't say so if it weren't true. I decided that next time I would bring a handbag, however it looked and whatever anyone said. Naturally those metal-clad dandies of the Round Table would consider it outrageous, and might raise hell over it. But I wanted comfort first; fashion could wait. We jogged along, occasionally crossing a dusty patch that rose in clouds, filled my nose, and made me sneeze and weep. Of course I said things I shouldn't have. I won't deny it. I'm no better than anyone else.

We seemed unable to meet a soul in this deserted Britain, not even an ogre. Considering my mood, that was fortunate for the ogre—particularly an ogre carrying a handkerchief. Most knights would have concentrated on taking his armor. As far as I was concerned, provided I got his bandanna, he could keep every piece of his hardware.

Meanwhile it was growing steadily hotter inside my suit. The sun kept beating down, heating the iron more and more. When you're hot like that, every small thing gets on your nerves. At a trot I rattled like a crate full of plates, which irritated me. Worse still, I could hardly bear the shield flapping and banging, first against my chest and then around my back. If I slowed to a walk, the joints of my armor creaked and shrieked with that maddening wheelbarrow sound. At that speed we made no breeze, so I was in danger of frying inside the stove. Besides, the more gently you moved, the more heavily the iron pressed down, until you seemed to gain several tons every minute."}

{"request_id":"adb92c6ed094b2b6b4982e40","rewritten_text":"Many American women, on leaving their own country, acquire an air of permanent poor health, believing it to be a sign of European sophistication. Mrs. Otis had never made that mistake. She possessed a superb constitution and an astonishing supply of natural vitality. In many ways, indeed, she was thoroughly English: an excellent illustration of how much we now share with America—everything, of course, except the language. Her eldest son was Washington, so named during a burst of parental patriotism that he never stopped regretting. He was a fair-haired, reasonably handsome young man whose qualifications for American diplomacy consisted of leading the German dance at the Newport Casino for three consecutive seasons. Even in London, he enjoyed a reputation as an excellent dancer. His only weaknesses were gardenias and the aristocracy; in every other respect he was extremely sensible. Miss Virginia E. Otis was a small girl of fifteen, graceful and lovely as a fawn, with a splendid independence in her large blue eyes. She was a remarkable horsewoman. Once, on her pony, she had raced old Lord Bilton twice around the park, beating him by a length and a half just before the statue of Achilles. The young Duke of Cheshire was so delighted that he proposed to her immediately. His guardians sent him back to Eton that very evening, weeping bitterly. Next came the twins, generally known as “The Stars and Stripes” because they were forever being whipped. They were charming boys and, apart from the worthy Minister, the family's only genuine republicans.

Since Canterville Chase lay seven miles from Ascot, the closest railway station, Mr. Otis had wired ahead for an open carriage to collect them. They began their drive in excellent spirits. It was a beautiful July evening, and a delicate fragrance from the pine woods filled the air. Occasionally they heard a wood pigeon dwelling lovingly on its own sweet song, or glimpsed a pheasant's gleaming breast deep among the rustling ferns. Small squirrels watched from the beech trees as they passed. Rabbits darted through the undergrowth and over moss-covered rises, their white tails lifted. But as they turned into the avenue leading to Canterville Chase, clouds suddenly covered the sky. An odd stillness seemed to grip the air. A great flock of rooks flew silently overhead, and before they reached the house, several large raindrops had begun to fall.

An elderly woman waited on the steps to welcome them, tidily dressed in black silk with a white apron and cap."}

{"request_id":"0d0bc08df7283f901ee37393","rewritten_text":"It was after two o’clock when I suddenly turned and found myself beside the lake, near a cove where a small boat was moored. Our house was close by, and I could see my father and aunt walking on the lawn. I jumped into the boat and, well used to such things, pushed away from the shore and rowed across as fast as I could. My father has often told me how I looked as I approached: dressed in white beneath my tartan wrap, my hair flowing over my shoulders, and the boat moving faster than anyone would have expected me to make it go. He said I seemed more like a spirit than a human girl. As I reached the shore, he held the boat steady. I sprang lightly out and was in his arms a moment later.

Now my life truly began. Everything around me changed from dull sameness into a bright scene of joy and delight. The happiness I found with my father went far beyond even my most hopeful expectations. We were always together and never ran out of things to discuss. He had spent his sixteen years away among peoples almost unknown in Europe. He had travelled through Persia, Arabia, and northern India, entering the homes and settlements of local people with a freedom few Europeans enjoyed. His accounts of their customs, his stories, and his descriptions of the landscape filled hours with pleasure whenever we tired of discussing our own plans for the future.

Affectionate words were so new to me that I listened with delight as he described his feelings for me through those long years when he had seemed to forget me. “At first,” he said, “I could not bear to think about my poor little girl. Later, as grief faded and hope returned, she was all I could turn to. In cities and deserts alike, her small, fairy-like figure, as I imagined it, was always moving before my eyes. The cool breeze from the north seemed sweeter and gentler because I felt it carried something of your spirit. I often thought I would return at once and take you with me to some fertile island where we could live in peace for ever. On the journey home, so many fears struck down my eager hopes; the pain of waiting became almost unbearable. I dared not imagine that the sun might shine and the moon rise over your grave instead of over you alive. But no, that has not happened. I have my Mathilda, my comfort and my hope.”"}

{"request_id":"18a502610e817ecac9f4e2b7","rewritten_text":"As summer wore on, plague, crowned with the sun’s powerful rays, sent its arrows across the earth, never missing its targets. Nations bowed their heads beneath them and died. The grain that had grown so abundantly lay rotting on the ground in autumn. The miserable man who had gone out to gather food for his children lay cold in a furrow, killed by plague. Green woods swayed majestically, while dying people lay beneath their shade, answering the solemn music of the branches with harsh cries. Brightly colored birds darted among the shadows. Untroubled deer rested safely on the ferns. Oxen and horses wandered from their unattended stables and grazed in the wheat, for death struck human beings alone.

Our fears grew with the summer and the rising deaths. My poor beloved and I looked at each other and at our children. “We will save them, Idris,” I said. “I will save them. Years from now we will tell them about these fears, when both the fears and their cause have passed away. Even if they alone remain on earth, they will live. Their cheeks will not grow pale, nor will their sweet voices weaken.” Our eldest understood something of what was happening around him. Sometimes he would look at me gravely and ask why there was so much destruction. But he was only ten, and the natural happiness of youth soon cleared his face of worries beyond his years. Evelyn was a laughing little angel, a playful child who knew nothing of pain or sorrow. Tossing his fair curls out of his eyes, he made the halls ring with laughter and drew our attention to his games in a thousand innocent ways. Clara, our lovely, gentle Clara, was our support, our comfort, and our joy. She took it upon herself to nurse the sick, comfort the grieving, help the old, and join the games and encourage the happiness of the young. She moved through the rooms like a kindly spirit sent from heaven to brighten our dark hour with an unearthly light. Gratitude and praise followed wherever she went. Yet when she stood modestly before us, playing with our children or busily performing small kindnesses for Idris, we wondered how so much courage, wisdom, and energetic goodness could dwell in her lovely, innocent features and the soft notes of her moving voice.

Summer dragged by, for we believed that winter would at least slow the disease. The hope that it might disappear completely was too precious, too deeply felt to speak aloud. When anyone carelessly voiced that thought, the listeners burst into tears and passionate sobs, showing how great their fears were and how little hope they had."}

{"request_id":"3db3d77171046bcf5ecd4e30","rewritten_text":"In truth, I felt no apprehension for her. She was what is called a capable woman, well able to conduct her affairs without my help. Indeed, I suspect she had always considered me an idle lad, a mere encumbrance, fit only for raising castles in the air, and was somewhat pleased to see me go. The night was dark when I took leave of her. With the three creditors who had caused me such vexation serving as my aides-de-camp, I conveyed the balloon, its car, and its equipment by an indirect route to the place where the remaining articles had been stored. Finding everything there untouched, I at once set about my business.

“It was April the first. As I have mentioned, the night was dark, without a single visible star, and occasional showers of fine rain made us exceedingly uncomfortable. My principal concern, however, was the balloon. Despite its protective coat of varnish, it was becoming somewhat weighed down with damp; the powder, too, might suffer damage. I accordingly kept my three importunate creditors busily employed, packing ice about the central cask and stirring the acid in the others. Nevertheless, they continually plagued me with questions about my purposes for this apparatus, and complained bitterly of the dreadful exertions I demanded. They could not imagine, they said, what benefit there could be in soaking themselves to the skin simply to assist at such frightful incantations. I grew anxious and labored with all my strength, for I honestly believe the fools thought I had made a bargain with the devil, and that my present undertaking was, in short, no honest business. I greatly feared, therefore, that they might desert me altogether. Yet I managed to soothe them by promising to settle every account in full as soon as this undertaking was concluded. Naturally, they interpreted these assurances in their own fashion, doubtless supposing that, whatever happened, a vast store of ready money would soon fall into my hands. So long as they received all I owed them, with a little extra for their assistance, I dare say they cared scarcely at all what might become of my soul or my body.

“After some four and a half hours, I judged the balloon to be inflated enough. I therefore secured the car to it and placed all my instruments inside, taking care to include the condensing apparatus, a plentiful supply of water, and a considerable stock of provisions, among them pemmican, which packs a great deal of nourishment into a relatively small space."}

{"request_id":"a00eb4d329800d1a36fd10e4","rewritten_text":"He wrote that he was wasting his time where he was, and that friends he had made in London were asking him to return and finish the negotiations concerning his Indian venture. He could put off leaving no longer. Since the journey to London might lead to his longer voyage even sooner than he expected, he begged me to spend as much time with him as I could. He therefore urged me to leave my lonely island and meet him in Perth, so that we could travel south together. His letter restored some of my interest in life, and I decided to leave the island in two days.

Before leaving, however, I had a task to complete that made me shudder even to consider. I had to pack my chemical instruments. This meant entering the room where I had carried out my hateful work and handling tools whose very appearance sickened me. At dawn the following morning, I gathered enough courage to unlock the laboratory door. The remains of the unfinished creature I had destroyed lay strewn across the floor, and I almost felt that I had torn apart the living flesh of a human being. I stopped to steady myself, then went inside. My hands shook as I carried the instruments out. But I realized that I must not leave the remnants of my work behind to arouse the villagers' horror and suspicion. I placed them in a basket with a large quantity of stones and set it aside, intending to throw it into the sea that same night. Meanwhile, I sat on the beach cleaning and organizing my chemical equipment.

The change in my feelings since the night of the demon's appearance could not have been more complete. Previously I had regarded my promise with bleak despair as something I was bound to fulfill, whatever the consequences. Now it seemed that a veil had been lifted from my eyes and I could see clearly for the first time. Resuming my work never entered my mind, even for an instant. His threat oppressed my thoughts, but I did not consider that I might avert it through a voluntary act of my own. I had firmly concluded that making another being like the fiend I had already created would be an act of the vilest, most appalling selfishness. I drove away every thought that might have brought me to any other conclusion."}

{"request_id":"699a7c74b1dce65c8ab3f502","rewritten_text":"Put down your weapons, fellow men, my brothers! Forgiveness, aid, and brotherly affection await your repentance. You are precious to us because you share humanity's fragile form. Every one of you will find a host and friend among these soldiers. Must man make war upon man while plague, the enemy of everyone, hangs over us already, exulting in a slaughter more savage than her own?”

Both armies hesitated. Our soldiers held their weapons tightly and regarded the enemy with stern eyes. The others still retained their arms, more through fear than any wish to fight. They glanced from one another, each longing for an example to follow, yet none would lead. Adrian sprang from his horse and went toward one of the newly slain. “He was a man,” he cried, “and now he is dead. Oh, hurry to dress the wounds of those who have fallen! Let none die. Let no more souls pass through your pitiless cuts to tell before God's throne the story of brothers murdering brothers. Bind their wounds; give them back to their friends. Banish the tiger's hearts that rage within your breasts. Cast aside these instruments of hatred and cruelty. In this respite from our destroying fate, let every man become his neighbor's brother, protector, and support. Away with those bloodied weapons! Let some of you hasten to tend these wounds.”

While speaking he knelt and lifted a man whose warm life flowed from a wound in his side. The unhappy creature gasped. Such silence had fallen over both hosts that his moaning could be plainly heard. Every heart, so recently fierce for general slaughter, now throbbed with anxious hope and fear for this one man's survival. Adrian pulled off his military scarf and wound it about the wounded man, but he was too late. The sufferer gave a long sigh; his head dropped backward, and his limbs could no longer support him. “He is dead!” Adrian said as the body slipped from his arms to the earth. He lowered his head in grief and awe. The destiny of the whole world seemed entangled in the death of that single man. The troops on either side cast down their arms. Even hardened veterans shed tears. Our men reached out their hands to their opponents, and a surge of love and profound friendship filled every breast. Unarmed, hand in hand, the two forces mingled, speaking only of ways to help each other. The enemies became one company, each side repentant: one for its earlier cruelties, the other for its recent violence. Together they followed the General's orders to march toward London."}

{"request_id":"4abe84a0c7c904760733b5b8","rewritten_text":"We grew up together, with less than a year between our ages. I hardly need to say that disagreements and quarrels were unknown to us. Our companionship was perfectly harmonious, and the differences in our characters only brought us closer. Elizabeth was calmer and more inwardly settled. Yet despite my excitable nature, I could work with greater intensity, and my hunger for knowledge was much stronger. She loved to follow the poets' flights of imagination. The grand and wonderful landscape around our Swiss home gave her endless things to admire and enjoy: the magnificent mountains, the changing seasons, storms and stillness, winter's silence, and the busy, overflowing life of our Alpine summers. While she gazed at these splendid sights with thoughtful satisfaction, I took pleasure in discovering what caused them. To me, the world was a secret waiting to be understood. Some of my earliest memories are of curiosity, determined efforts to uncover nature's hidden laws, and an almost ecstatic joy whenever they became clear to me.

When my parents had a second son, seven years younger than I was, they gave up traveling altogether and settled in their native country. We owned a house in Geneva and a country home at Belrive, on the lake's eastern shore, a little over three miles from the city. We lived mostly at the country house, where my parents led a rather secluded life. By nature I preferred to avoid crowds and form deep attachments to a few people. Most of my schoolmates therefore meant little to me, but one became my closest friend. Henry Clerval was the son of a Geneva merchant, a boy with unusual talent and imagination. He loved daring projects, hardship, and even danger for its own sake. He had read widely in stories of knights and romance. He wrote heroic songs and began many tales of magic and knightly adventure. He tried to persuade us to perform plays and dress up as characters drawn from the heroes of Roncesvalles, King Arthur's Round Table, and the knights who shed their blood to recover the holy tomb from those they regarded as unbelievers.

No one could have enjoyed a happier childhood than mine. My parents were kindness and generosity itself. We never thought of them as tyrants who would govern our lives according to a passing whim. Instead, we saw them as the people who created and provided all the many pleasures that filled our days."}

{"request_id":"87e911675629765e6ba9d497","rewritten_text":"The only question that seemed to trouble him was whom to kill. He understood that murder, like the religions of the ancient pagan world, needs a victim as well as a priest. Since he was no genius, he had no enemies. Besides, he felt this was no occasion for satisfying a personal grudge or dislike: his mission was far too solemn and serious for that. He therefore wrote a list of his friends and relatives on a sheet of notepaper. After careful thought, he chose Lady Clementina Beauchamp, a dear old lady who lived in Curzon Street and was his second cousin on his mother’s side. He had always been very fond of Lady Clem, as everyone called her. And because he was extremely wealthy himself, having inherited all Lord Rugby’s property when he came of age, he could gain no vulgar financial advantage from her death. Indeed, the more he considered it, the more she seemed exactly the right person. Feeling that any delay would be unfair to Sybil, he decided to make the arrangements immediately.

First, of course, he had to pay the palm reader. He sat down at a small Sheraton writing table near the window and wrote a check for £105 payable to Mr. Septimus Podgers. He put it in an envelope and instructed his valet to deliver it to West Moon Street. Then he telephoned the stables for his hansom cab and dressed to go out. As he left the room, he looked back at Sybil Merton’s photograph. Whatever happened, he vowed, he would never let her discover what he was doing for her. He would always keep the secret of his self-sacrifice buried in his heart.

On his way to the Buckingham, he stopped at a florist’s and sent Sybil a beautiful basket of narcissus flowers, with lovely white petals and bright centers like pheasants’ eyes. When he reached the club, he went directly to the library, rang the bell, and asked the waiter for a lemon and soda and a book about poisons. He had firmly decided that poison was the best way to handle this troublesome business. Any kind of physical violence was deeply unpleasant to him. He was also particularly anxious to avoid killing Lady Clementina in a way that might draw public attention. He hated the thought of being treated as a celebrity at Lady Windermere’s parties, or of finding his name in the gossip columns of vulgar society newspapers."}

{"request_id":"922ecf121ffdd5d51b677fd9","rewritten_text":"All the young people were desperate to see Lyme. Captain Wentworth spoke of returning there himself; it was only seventeen miles from Uppercross, and although it was November, the weather was quite good. Louisa, in short, was the most enthusiastic of them all. Once she had decided to go, she had both the pleasure of getting her own way and her new conviction that standing by her decisions was a virtue to support her. She overcame every wish her father and mother expressed to postpone the visit until summer. So they were going to Lyme: Charles, Mary, Anne, Henrietta, Louisa, and Captain Wentworth.

Their first, thoughtless plan was to leave in the morning and return that night. Mr Musgrove, however, refused to agree for his horses’ sake; and once they considered it sensibly, they realized that a day in mid-November allowed little time to explore a new place after subtracting the seven hours the countryside made necessary for the journey there and back. They would therefore spend the night and were not expected home until dinner the following day. Everyone regarded this as a considerable improvement. Yet although they assembled at the Great House for a rather early breakfast and left precisely on time, it was well past noon when the two carriages began descending the long hill into Lyme and turned onto the even steeper street in the town itself. Mr Musgrove’s coach carried the four ladies, while Charles drove Captain Wentworth in his curricle. Clearly they would have barely enough time to look around before the daylight and warmth disappeared.

Once they had arranged their accommodation and ordered dinner at one of the inns, their next step was unquestionably to walk straight down to the sea. They had arrived too late in the year for any of the entertainment or variety Lyme could provide as a resort. The assembly rooms were closed, almost all the visitors had left their lodgings, and scarcely any families remained apart from those who lived there. Since the buildings themselves offer nothing to admire, a visitor’s attention turns to the town’s remarkable setting: its principal street seems almost to rush into the water; the walk to the Cobb follows the edge of the pleasant little bay, lively in the season with bathing machines and holidaymakers; and the Cobb itself has its old marvels and recent improvements. Beyond them lies the beautiful stretch of cliffs running eastward from the town. It would take a very unusual visitor to find no charm in Lyme’s immediate surroundings, nothing that made them want to become better acquainted with the place."}

{"request_id":"f515c1db0ca66e24c51c7fea","rewritten_text":"We grew up together, less than a year apart in age. I hardly need say that we knew no quarrels or estrangement. Harmony lay at the heart of our companionship, and the differences and contrasts between our personalities brought us closer. Elizabeth was calmer and more self-contained, but despite my passionate nature, I could concentrate more intensely and felt a deeper thirst for knowledge. She occupied herself with the poets’ flights of imagination. In the majestic, extraordinary landscapes surrounding our Swiss home—the sublime mountain forms, the changing seasons, storms and stillness, winter’s silence, and the life and tumult of our Alpine summers—she found endless reasons for wonder and delight. While my companion contemplated the magnificent appearance of things with solemn contentment, I took pleasure in investigating their causes. To me, the world was a secret I wanted to uncover. Curiosity, an earnest search for nature’s hidden laws, and joy approaching ecstasy as those laws revealed themselves are among the earliest feelings I remember.

When their second son was born, seven years younger than me, my parents abandoned their traveling life altogether and settled in their native country. We owned a house in Geneva and a country home at Belrive, on the lake’s eastern shore, a little more than three miles from the city. We lived mainly at the latter, where my parents led fairly secluded lives. I naturally avoided crowds and formed passionate attachments to a few people. Consequently, most of my schoolmates meant little to me, but with one I developed the closest possible friendship. Henry Clerval was the son of a Geneva merchant, a boy of remarkable talent and imagination. He loved bold undertakings, hardship, and even danger for its own sake. He read widely in tales of chivalry and romance. He composed heroic songs and began writing numerous stories of enchantment and knightly adventure. He tried to persuade us to perform plays and take part in masquerades, taking our characters from the heroes of Roncesvalles, King Arthur’s Round Table, and the companies of knights who shed their blood to reclaim the Holy Sepulchre from those they called unbelievers.

No one could have had a happier childhood than I did. My parents embodied kindness and indulgence. We never saw them as tyrants directing our lives according to their whims. Instead, we understood that they were the people who created and provided all the many pleasures we enjoyed."}

{"request_id":"0cc52115b6aa95b51485dc4a","rewritten_text":"The following day he stayed inside the house, spending most of it in his own room. He was sick with a frantic fear of death, yet felt no interest in living. The feeling that he was being hunted, trapped, and followed had begun to take over his mind. If a tapestry merely stirred in the breeze, he trembled. The dead leaves blowing against the leaded windows seemed like his own broken promises and desperate regrets. Whenever he shut his eyes, he saw the sailor’s face again, peering through the misty glass, and felt horror grip his heart once more.

But perhaps his imagination alone had summoned revenge from the darkness and placed those dreadful visions of punishment before him. Real life was a confusion of events, while the imagination followed a terrifying logic. It was imagination that made regret follow closely behind wrongdoing. Imagination made every crime give birth to its own twisted offspring. In the everyday world of facts, the wicked went unpunished and the good went unrewarded. The strong were granted success, and the weak were forced to fail. That was all. Besides, if a stranger had been lurking around the house, the servants or gamekeepers would have noticed him. If there had been footprints in the flower beds, the gardeners would have reported them. Yes, it had only been his imagination. Sibyl Vane’s brother had not returned to murder him. He had sailed away in his ship to sink in some winter sea. He was safe from that man, at least. Why, the man did not know who he was, could not possibly know. His youthful appearance had protected him.

And yet, if it had all been an illusion, how dreadful to think that his conscience could create such terrifying ghosts, give them visible bodies, and make them move in front of him! What would his life become if, night and day, the shadows of his crime stared from quiet corners, mocked him from hiding places, whispered in his ear while he sat at dinner, and woke him with freezing fingers while he slept? As this thought passed through his mind, his face turned pale with fear, and the air seemed suddenly colder. Oh, what a frenzy of madness had driven him to kill his friend! Even the memory of that scene was unbearable. He saw the whole thing again. Every horrible detail returned, more dreadful than before. From the dark cavern of the past rose the image of his sin, terrible and wrapped in scarlet."}

{"request_id":"d2925416a6c522a2d4174fb0","rewritten_text":"As I put my hand through the valve, carrying a cup of water for the old cat, my shirtsleeves caught in the loop supporting the basket and instantly dislodged it from the bottom. Had the whole thing actually dissolved into thin air, it could not have disappeared from view more suddenly or completely. I am certain that less than a tenth of a second passed between the basket coming loose and its utter disappearance, together with everything inside it. My good wishes accompanied it down to earth, but naturally I had no hope that either the cat or her kittens would survive to tell the story of their misfortune.

“At six o’clock, I noticed that a large part of the earth visible to the east was covered in deep shadow. This continued spreading very rapidly until, at five minutes to seven, the entire surface within my view lay wrapped in the darkness of night. Yet a considerable time passed before the setting sun’s rays stopped lighting the balloon. Although I had, of course, fully expected this, it still gave me tremendous pleasure. Clearly, in the morning I would see the sun rise at least several hours before the citizens of Rotterdam, despite their position so much farther east. Thus, with each passing day, and in proportion to the height I reached, I would enjoy sunlight for longer and longer periods. I now decided to keep a journal of my journey, counting each day continuously from one to twenty-four hours, without regard to the intervals of darkness.

“At ten o’clock, feeling sleepy, I decided to lie down for the remainder of the night. But a difficulty now arose which, obvious though it may seem, had escaped my notice until that very moment. If I went to sleep as intended, how would the air in the chamber be refreshed while I slept? It would be impossible to go on breathing it for more than an hour at the very most. Even if that period could be stretched to an hour and a quarter, the consequences might be disastrous. Considering this predicament caused me considerable anxiety. You may find it hard to believe that, after all the dangers I had already endured, I should regard this problem so seriously that I abandoned hope of achieving my final purpose and eventually decided I would have to descend. But my hesitation lasted only a moment."}

{"request_id":"b723252cc039ac3c5e4bcd78","rewritten_text":"Do not misunderstand me: I was never truly mad. Even when my frantic thoughts seemed to be driving me into insanity, I remained aware of my condition, and I revealed those thoughts only to silence and solitude. The people around me knew nothing of it. They saw only a poor girl with a broken spirit, speaking in a soft, quiet voice, beneath whose lowered eyelids tears sometimes slipped out, which she tried to conceal. Someone who preferred to be alone and recoiled from attention; someone who never smiled—oh, no, I never smiled—and that was all.

Well, I escaped. I left my guardian’s house and was never heard from again. The letters I left behind, and other circumstances I deliberately arranged, led people to believe I had killed myself. They therefore searched less carefully than they otherwise would have, and before long every trace and memory of me disappeared. I left London aboard a small vessel heading for a port in northern England. Now that my attempt had succeeded and I was entirely alone, peace returned. The sea was calm and the vessel glided gently forward. Sitting on deck beneath the open sky, I felt as though I had become a different person. I was no longer the wild, raving, desperately unhappy Mathilda, but a young female hermit devoted to seclusion, who must strive to keep her heart free of turmoil and unholy despair. The fanciful, nunlike clothing I had adopted; the knowledge that my very existence was a secret known only to me; the solitude destined to fill the rest of my life—all nurtured gentle thoughts in my wounded heart. The breeze moving through my hair revived me. With peaceful eyes I watched sunlight glitter on the waves, and birds chase one another across the water, just brushing it with their feathers. I also slept without disturbing dreams and woke refreshed, ready to enjoy my quiet freedom once again.

Four days later we reached our destination harbour. I would not stay on the coast, but immediately travelled inland. I had already decided what sort of place I would live in: an isolated house on a broad plain, far from any other dwelling, where I could see the entire horizon and wander great distances without being troubled by the sight of other people. I did not hate humanity, but felt that the calm flow of my emotions depended on being alone. I settled in a wide, solitary place, on a bleak heath scattered with stones, short grass growing between them, and here and there a few rushes beside a small pool."}

{"request_id":"b01fbcdd2b6b5ff2d4f323cd","rewritten_text":"Four days after these strange events, a funeral procession left Canterville Chase at about eleven at night. Eight black horses pulled the hearse, each wearing a large tuft of swaying ostrich feathers on its head. A rich purple cloth covered the lead coffin, with the Canterville coat of arms embroidered on it in gold. Servants carrying burning torches walked beside the hearse and the carriages, making the whole procession wonderfully impressive. Lord Canterville, who had travelled from Wales especially for the funeral, was the chief mourner. He rode in the first carriage with little Virginia. The United States Minister and his wife followed, then Washington and the three boys, with Mrs. Umney in the last carriage. Everyone felt that, after being frightened by the ghost for more than fifty years, she deserved to see him finally laid to rest. A deep grave had been dug in a corner of the churchyard beneath the old yew tree, and the Reverend Augustus Dampier read the service with great solemnity. After the ceremony, the servants put out their torches, following an old Canterville family custom. As the coffin was lowered into the grave, Virginia stepped forward and placed a large cross of white and pink almond blossoms on it. At that moment the moon emerged from behind a cloud and silently filled the little churchyard with silver light. A nightingale began singing in a distant patch of woodland. Virginia remembered the ghost’s description of the Garden of Death. Tears blurred her eyes, and she barely spoke on the journey home.

The next morning, before Lord Canterville left for London, Mr. Otis spoke with him about the jewels the ghost had given Virginia. They were magnificent, particularly a ruby necklace in an old Venetian setting, a superb piece of sixteenth-century craftsmanship. They were so valuable that Mr. Otis had serious doubts about allowing his daughter to accept them.

‘My lord,’ he said, ‘I understand that in this country the law of mortmain, concerning property held permanently, applies to jewellery as well as land. It seems perfectly clear that these jewels are, or ought to be, your family heirlooms. I therefore ask you to take them to London with you. Please consider them simply a part of your property that has returned to you in rather unusual circumstances. As for my daughter, she is still only a child and, I am happy to say, so far takes very little interest in these ornaments of idle luxury.’"}

{"request_id":"854780257dc581dcabed5d77","rewritten_text":"“Keeping firmly in mind the points I have emphasized—the peculiar voice, the extraordinary agility, and the astonishing lack of motive for such an exceptionally atrocious murder—let us consider the slaughter itself. A woman has been strangled by hand and forced headfirst up a chimney. Ordinary murderers do not use methods like these, and certainly do not dispose of their victims this way. You must admit that thrusting the body up the chimney was something utterly bizarre, something that cannot be reconciled with our ordinary understanding of human behaviour, even if we imagine the most depraved men committing the crime. Think, too, of the strength needed to force a body upward into such an opening so firmly that several people together could barely pull it down!

“Now consider the other signs of phenomenal strength. Thick locks—extremely thick locks—of grey human hair lay on the hearth, torn out by the roots. You know how much force it takes to tear even twenty or thirty hairs from a person’s head at once. You saw these clumps just as I did. Their roots—what a dreadful sight!—were clogged with pieces of scalp. They provide clear evidence of the tremendous power used to pull out perhaps half a million hairs at a time. The old woman’s throat was not simply cut: her head was completely severed from her body, using nothing more than a razor. I also want you to consider the animal ferocity of these acts. I say nothing here of the bruises on Madame L’Espanaye’s body. Monsieur Dumas and his respected colleague, Monsieur Etienne, declared that these were caused by a blunt object. So far, those gentlemen are entirely correct. That object was obviously the stone paving of the courtyard, onto which the victim fell from the window overlooking the bed. Simple as this explanation may now appear, the police missed it for the same reason they missed the shutters’ width. Their discovery of the nails had completely closed their minds to the possibility that the windows could ever have been opened.

“If, along with all this, you have properly considered the strange disorder of the room, we have now brought together several ideas: astounding agility, superhuman strength, animal ferocity, slaughter without a motive, horror so grotesque that it is utterly foreign to humanity, and a voice whose sound was unfamiliar to listeners from many different nations and contained no clear or intelligible syllables.”"}

{"request_id":"727b83cb971b066fe321c63e","rewritten_text":"Summer drew onward, and plague, crowned by the sun’s mighty beams, sent her unfailing arrows across the earth. Beneath their power the nations bent their heads and perished. The grain that had risen abundantly lay rotting upon the soil in autumn, while the sorrowful creature who had ventured forth to find bread for his children lay rigid in the furrow, struck down by plague. The green forests swayed their branches in majesty, and beneath their shade the dying lay scattered, replying to that solemn music with discordant cries. Brightly coloured birds darted through the shadows; untroubled deer rested safely on the fern. Oxen and horses wandered out of unattended stables to graze among the wheat, for death descended upon mankind alone.

Our terror increased with the summer and its deaths. My poor beloved and I gazed at one another, and at our children. “We shall preserve them, Idris,” I said; “I shall save them. In years to come we shall tell them of these fears, vanished then with the cause that brought them. Even should they alone remain upon the earth, they shall yet live; their faces shall not pale, nor their dear voices weaken.” Our eldest understood something of the events surrounding him, and would sometimes question me gravely about the cause of such immense devastation. Yet he was only ten, and youth’s gladness soon drove untimely anxiety from his face. Evelyn, a merry cherub, a playful infant knowing neither suffering nor grief, shook his fair curls from his eyes and filled the halls with echoes of laughter, drawing our notice to his games in a thousand innocent ways. Clara, our sweet and gentle Clara, sustained, consoled, and delighted us. She undertook to nurse the sick, comfort the grieving, help the old, and share the amusements and stir the happiness of the young. Through the rooms she passed like a benevolent spirit sent from heaven to brighten our darkened hour with an unearthly radiance. Thanks and blessings followed her steps. Yet when she stood modestly before us, playing with our little ones or attentively performing small kindnesses for Idris, one wondered in which lovely feature of her innocent beauty, or which tender note of her affecting voice, such courage, wisdom, and untiring goodness could dwell.

Summer dragged slowly by, for we believed winter would at least restrain the illness. Complete deliverance was a hope too precious, too deeply felt, to speak aloud. If anyone carelessly voiced it, the listeners’ streaming tears and impassioned sobs revealed the depth of their dread and the faintness of their hope."}

{"request_id":"d90b4956292e842c1e70da2a","rewritten_text":"Spirits of the storm, take me in! Powers of destruction, open your arms and hold me forever—unless some kinder power has appointed a different end, allowing me, after long endurance, to earn my reward and feel my heart beat once again beside another heart like mine.

The Tiber lay at my feet, a road laid by nature herself through her continent, with many boats tied along its banks. I would embark in one with a few books, supplies, and my dog, and let the current carry me down to the sea. Then, staying close to land, I would follow the beautiful shores and sunlit headlands of the blue Mediterranean, passing Naples and travelling along Calabria. I would brave the twin dangers of Scylla and Charybdis, then steer fearlessly—for what did I have to lose?—across the water toward Malta and the more distant Cyclades. I would avoid Constantinople, whose familiar towers and inlets belonged to a life wholly different from the one I now lived. I would trace the coasts of Asia Minor and Syria, pass the seven mouths of the Nile, and turn north again until forgotten Carthage and deserted Libya fell out of sight and I reached the Pillars of Hercules. After that—it would not matter where. Before that long voyage ended, the ocean's muddy caves and silent depths might become my home; disease might strike its arrow through my heart as I drifted alone on the heaving Mediterranean. Or I might find what I sought—a companion—at some place where I landed. If not, then through endless time, frail and grey-haired, with my youth already buried beside those I loved, the solitary wanderer would still spread his sail and grip the tiller. Still following heaven's winds, he would round headland after headland forever, anchor in bay after bay, and keep ploughing the barren sea. Leaving the green land of my native Europe behind, I might follow Africa's tawny coast, weather the fierce waters of the Cape, and moor my battered little boat in a creek shaded by fragrant spice groves on the distant Indian Ocean islands.

These are extravagant dreams. Yet ever since they came to me a week ago, while I stood high on St. Peter's, they have possessed my imagination. I have selected my boat and gathered my meagre supplies. I have chosen a few books, chiefly Homer and Shakespeare. But every library in the world stands open to me, and I can replenish my collection at any port. I expect no change for the better, yet I cannot endure the monotony of the present."}

{"request_id":"2b0b8204f94d5ad7c7f77956","rewritten_text":"Few people could claim a heart so pure, or such genuine humility combined with a firm confidence in their own integrity and faith in the integrity of others. She had lived a secluded life from birth. Her mother died when she was very young, but her father devoted himself to her education. He held many unusual ideas, which shaped the system he chose for teaching her. She knew a great deal about the heroes of Greece and Rome, and those of England who had lived centuries earlier, yet knew almost nothing about current events. She had read few authors from at least the past fifty years, although apart from that exception her reading was extensive. Thus, while she appeared less familiar than he was with the mysteries of life and society, her knowledge ran deeper and rested on stronger foundations. Even if her beauty and gentleness had not captivated him, her intelligence would always have held his own in its power. He looked up to her as his guide, and adored her so much that he delighted in deepening, in his own mind, the feeling of inferiority she sometimes inspired.

His mother died when he was nineteen. He left college after her death and, distancing himself for a time from his old friends, withdrew to the neighbourhood where his Diana lived. All his comfort came from her gentle voice and her still more precious caresses. This brief separation from his companions gave him the courage to assert his independence. He felt that, however much they might ridicule his proposed marriage, they would not dare display their ridicule once it had taken place. He therefore sought his guardian’s consent, which he obtained with some difficulty, and that of his beloved’s father, which was granted more readily. Without telling anyone else of his plans, he became Diana’s husband by the time he reached his twentieth birthday.

He loved her passionately, and her tenderness had such a hold on him that he could think of nothing else. He invited some college friends to visit, but their frivolity disgusted him. Diana had torn away the veil that had kept him a boy: he had become a man. He wondered how he could ever have shared his fellow students’ empty phrases and ideas, or for a single moment have feared the criticism of such people. He abandoned his old friendships not because he was inconstant, but because they were truly unworthy of him."}

{"request_id":"5f240ed308bedab35a4f5e92","rewritten_text":"My handkerchief, you understand, was inside my helmet, along with a few other articles; but this happened to be a helmet a man couldn't remove without assistance. That hadn't entered my head when I stowed it there; indeed, I hadn't known it. I had imagined it would be wonderfully convenient. Now the knowledge that it lay so close, practically at hand and yet impossible to reach, made the whole business harder to endure. Yes, what you can't lay hands on is generally what you most want; everybody has observed that. Well, it drove everything else from my thoughts, swept them clean away and fixed them inside my helmet. Mile after mile they remained there, imagining that handkerchief, conjuring up that handkerchief. It was maddening to feel the salty perspiration trickle into my eyes while I couldn't reach it. In print this may seem a trifling inconvenience, but it was nothing of the sort; it was misery of the most substantial kind. I wouldn't claim that unless it were true. I resolved to bring a reticule next time, regardless of its appearance or anybody's remarks. Naturally, those iron-clad dandies of the Round Table would call it disgraceful and perhaps raise Sheol over it, but I wanted comfort first and fashion second. We jogged onward, occasionally coming upon a dusty stretch where clouds rose about us, filling my nose and making me sneeze and weep. Of course I said things better left unsaid; I don't pretend otherwise. I'm no better than other people.

In this solitary Britain we seemed unable to encounter a soul, not even an ogre. Considering my temper just then, that was fortunate for the ogre—an ogre carrying a handkerchief, I mean. Most knights would have cared only about taking his armor. As far as I was concerned, he might keep his ironmongery, provided I secured his bandanna.

All the while it grew steadily hotter inside. The sun, you see, kept beating upon the iron and heating it further. When you're hot in that fashion, every small thing gets on your nerves. At a trot I clattered like a box of crockery, which irritated me; besides, I could scarcely bear the shield swinging and banging first against my chest, then around my back. If I slowed to a walk, my joints squealed and creaked with the tiresome sound of a wheelbarrow. At that pace we stirred up no breeze, so I was in danger of being fried inside that stove. Worse still, the more quietly we moved, the heavier the iron pressed upon me, and with every passing minute I seemed to weigh several additional tons."}

{"request_id":"ca2d71fae45e9ac63618b08f","rewritten_text":"Receive me, spirits of the tempest! Powers of destruction, stretch wide your arms and embrace me for ever—unless a gentler power has ordained some other fate, permitting me, after enduring long, to win my recompense and once more feel my heart beating close to the heart of a fellow creature.

At my feet lay Tiber, the highway traced by nature's own hand across her continent, with many boats fastened along its banks. Into one of these I would step with a handful of books, provisions, and my dog, and drift upon the river's current to the sea. Then, remaining near the land, I would follow the lovely coast and sun-bright promontories of the blue Mediterranean, pass Naples and the shores of Calabria, and venture through the double danger of Scylla and Charybdis. Thereafter, with undaunted purpose—for what remained for me to lose?—I would glide over the ocean toward Malta and the farther Cyclades. Constantinople I would shun; the sight of its familiar towers and inlets belonged to an existence unlike my present one. I would skirt Asia Minor and Syria and, beyond the seven-mouthed Nile, turn north once more, until forgotten Carthage and forsaken Libya vanished behind me and I gained the pillars of Hercules. And afterward—where mattered nothing—the ocean's oozy caverns and silent depths might house me before that protracted journey ended. The shaft of disease might pierce my heart as I floated alone upon the tossing Mediterranean; or at some port of call I might discover the thing I sought—a companion. Failing this, throughout unending time, decrepit and grey, my youth already entombed with my loved ones, the solitary voyager would continue to spread his sail and grasp the tiller. Obedient still to the winds of heaven, he would round promontory after promontory for ever, anchor in one bay and then another, and still furrow the unfruitful ocean. Leaving my native Europe's green lands behind, descending Africa's tawny coastline and weathering the savage seas about the Cape, I might secure my battered skiff in a creek beneath spice-bearing groves, among the fragrant islands of the remote Indian Ocean.

Wild dreams indeed. Yet from the moment they visited me a week ago, as I stood upon the summit of St. Peter's, they have governed my imagination. My boat is chosen, my slender provisions gathered. I have picked a few books, chiefly Homer and Shakespeare; but all the world's libraries lie open to me, and at any harbour I may replenish my supply. I entertain no hope that things will improve; yet this unvarying present has become unbearable."}

{"request_id":"eccfa28466c89d32f11364cd","rewritten_text":"He wrote that he was wasting his days to no purpose where he remained, and that the friends he had made in London had sent letters urging his return to conclude their negotiations concerning his venture in India. He could postpone his departure no further. Since his journey to London might be followed by his longer voyage even sooner than he presently supposed, he implored me to give him as much of my company as I could. He therefore begged me to abandon my lonely island and join him at Perth, so that we might travel south together. His letter restored me in some measure to life, and I resolved to leave the island in two days' time.

Before leaving, however, I had a duty to fulfil, the mere thought of which made me shudder. My chemical instruments had to be packed, and to do so I must return to the room of my abominable labours and touch the implements whose very appearance sickened me. At dawn the following morning, I gathered enough courage to unlock the laboratory door. Across the floor lay the remnants of the unfinished creature I had destroyed. I felt almost as though I had torn apart the living body of a human being. After pausing to regain control of myself, I stepped inside. My hand shook as I carried the instruments out. It occurred to me, however, that the remains of my work must not be left behind to arouse the villagers' fear and suspicion. I placed them in a basket with a considerable weight of stones and set it aside, intending to cast it into the sea that same night. Meanwhile, I seated myself on the shore and occupied myself with cleaning and putting my chemical equipment in order.

The change in my feelings since the dæmon's appearance that night could not have been more absolute. Previously I had contemplated my promise in dark despair, as an obligation that must be honoured whatever its results. Now it seemed that a veil had been lifted from my sight, and I could see distinctly for the first time. Not for a single instant did I consider returning to my task. His threat burdened my mind, but I did not think of any willing action of my own as a means of escaping it. I had decided that making a second being like the fiend I had already created would constitute selfishness of the vilest and most dreadful kind. Every thought that might have led me to another judgment I drove from my mind."}

{"request_id":"45c2998c107195d04098c516","rewritten_text":"The wavering gas-lamps curved away in a long line into the distance, and a lone hansom waited outside a small house enclosed by walls, its driver sleeping within. He hurried towards Portland Place, glancing back from time to time as if afraid someone followed him. Two men stood at the corner of Rich Street, studying a little notice pasted on a hoarding. A peculiar curiosity moved him to cross the road. As he approached, his eye caught the word “Murder” in black print. He gave a start, and his cheek reddened deeply. The notice promised a reward for information that would secure the capture of a man of middling height, aged between thirty and forty, dressed in a billy-cock hat, black coat, and checked trousers, with a scar on his right cheek. Again and again he read it, wondering whether the unhappy fellow would be taken, and how he had received that scar. Perhaps one day his own name would appear on placards along the walls of London. Perhaps a reward would one day be offered for him as well.

He felt sick with terror at the thought. Wheeling round, he hastened onward through the night.

He scarcely knew where his steps led him. He remembered vaguely straying through a maze of shabby houses, losing himself in an enormous net of gloomy streets; daylight was already bright when at last he emerged in Piccadilly Circus. Walking home towards Belgrave Square, he encountered the heavy waggons bound for Covent Garden. Their carters in white smocks, with cheerful weather-browned faces and rough curling hair, marched stoutly beside them, snapping their whips and occasionally shouting to one another. A plump little boy rode a great grey horse at the head of a jingling team, primroses tucked in his battered hat, his small hands clinging firmly to its mane as he laughed. Against the morning sky the towering loads of vegetables resembled heaps of jade, green jade set against the pink petals of a wonderful rose. Lord Arthur found himself strangely moved, without understanding why. To him the tender beauty of dawn held something unspeakably sorrowful, and he considered all the days that open in loveliness and close in tempest. These country folk, too, with their gruff, merry voices and careless bearing—how curious a London lay before their eyes! A London without the night's wickedness or the day's smoke, a pale spectral city, a lonely city of graves! He wondered what they made of it, and whether they knew anything of its magnificence and disgrace, its fierce pleasures coloured like flame, its dreadful hunger, and everything it fashions and ruins between morning and evening."}

{"request_id":"7a4d89ad1de2b0033a919613","rewritten_text":"Even now I had not swallowed the last bitter drops of my suffering. I had not fully accepted my loss. I did not yet feel with every heartbeat, every nerve, every thought, that I alone remained of the human race—that I was the LAST MAN.

Clouds had gathered during the day, and at sunset a light rain began. Even the everlasting sky is weeping, I thought. Why should a mortal man be ashamed to exhaust himself in tears? I remembered the old legends in which people wept until they dissolved into endlessly flowing springs. If only that could happen to me! Then my fate would resemble, in some way, the deaths of Adrian and Clara in the water. Oh, grief has a strange imagination. From every shape and change around it, it spins a web on which to write the story of its suffering. It joins itself to all living nature and feeds on every object. Like light, it fills everything; like light, it gives everything its own colours.

My search had taken me some distance from the place where I had washed ashore. I came upon one of the watchtowers built at regular intervals along the Italian coast. I welcomed the shelter and the sight of something made by human hands, after looking so long at nature's bleak emptiness. I entered and climbed the rough spiral staircase to the guardroom. Fate was kind enough to spare me any distressing remains of the people who had once lived there. A few boards resting on two iron stands, covered in dry corn leaves, offered a bed. An open chest held some partly mouldy biscuits. Seeing them woke my appetite; perhaps I had been hungry already, but until then I had not noticed. I also suffered a fierce, burning thirst from exhaustion and the seawater I had swallowed. Nature kindly makes the relief of these needs pleasurable. So even I—even I!—felt refreshed and soothed as I ate that miserable food and drank a little sour wine from a half-full flask left in the deserted tower. Then I lay down on the bed, which no shipwreck survivor would have rejected. After the hateful smell of seaweed, the earthy scent of the dried leaves comforted me. I forgot that I was alone. I thought neither of the past nor of the future. My senses grew quiet, and I fell asleep. I dreamed of beloved places inland, of people making hay, and of a shepherd whistling for his dog to help bring the flock into its pen. I dreamed of sights and sounds from my mountain childhood that I had long forgotten."}

{"request_id":"ce048b4db87e31618e4c047a","rewritten_text":"I encountered several funerals, with few mourners following them, and those who watched regarded them as portents of the gravest kind. Some stared at the processions with frantic eagerness; others shrank away in fear; some cried openly.

After providing immediate relief to the sick, Adrian’s principal concern had been to conceal the plague’s symptoms and advance from the people of London. He understood that fear and gloomy anticipation lent powerful aid to disease, and that despondency and brooding anxiety made the human frame especially liable to infection. Accordingly, no distressing spectacles were visible: most shops remained open, and a certain flow of passers-by was maintained. Yet, though the outward aspect of a stricken city had been averted, London seemed greatly altered to me, who had not seen it since the visitation began. No carriages passed, and tall grass had grown in the streets; the houses looked forlorn, their shutters mostly shut; and the people I encountered wore a pale, frightened stare, wholly unlike the customary businesslike bearing of Londoners. My lone carriage drew attention as it rattled towards the Protectoral Palace, and the fashionable streets approaching it seemed even more dreary and deserted. Adrian’s antechamber was full: it was the appointed hour for audiences. Unwilling to interrupt his work, I waited and observed the petitioners coming and going. They were members of the middle and lower ranks, deprived of their livelihoods by the suspension of trade and of that vigorous pursuit of money in all its forms so characteristic of our country. The anxiety, sometimes terror, of the arrivals contrasted sharply with the resigned, even contented, bearing of those whose audience was over. In their livelier movements and cheerful expressions I could discern my friend’s influence. The clock struck two, and no more were admitted; the disappointed applicants departed sadly or sullenly, while I went into the audience-chamber.

The apparent improvement in Adrian’s health astonished me. He no longer drooped earthward like a spring flower too carefully tended, which grows beyond its strength and bends beneath the very crown of its blossoms. His eyes shone, his face was tranquil, and his entire figure displayed a collected energy far removed from its earlier languor. He was seated at a table among several secretaries, occupied in sorting petitions or recording notes taken in the course of that day’s audiences. Two or three petitioners remained before him. I admired the fairness and patience he showed. To those able to support themselves outside London, he counselled immediate departure, and supplied the means for them to leave."}

{"request_id":"a4143239f6a3046ab80727a5","rewritten_text":"The next morning, when the Otis family gathered for breakfast, they talked about the ghost at some length. The United States Minister was naturally rather annoyed that his gift had been refused. ‘I have no wish,’ he said, ‘to hurt the ghost personally. And considering how long he has lived in this house, I must say I think throwing pillows at him is very rude.’ This was a perfectly fair remark, though I regret to say that the twins burst into loud laughter. ‘On the other hand,’ he went on, ‘if he really refuses to use the Rising Sun Lubricator, we shall have to take away his chains. No one could possibly sleep with all that noise outside the bedroom doors.’

For the rest of the week, however, nothing disturbed them. The only thing that attracted attention was the bloodstain that kept reappearing on the library floor. This was certainly strange, since Mr. Otis locked the door every night and the windows stayed firmly barred. The stain’s changes of colour also caused plenty of discussion. Some mornings it was a dull, almost Indian red; at other times it was bright scarlet, and then a deep purple. Once, when they came downstairs for family prayers, held according to the simple practices of the Free American Reformed Episcopalian Church, they found it a brilliant emerald green. Naturally, these constantly changing colours greatly amused the family, and every evening they freely placed bets on the subject. Only little Virginia failed to enjoy the joke. For some unexplained reason, the bloodstain always upset her considerably, and on the morning when it was emerald green she almost cried.

The ghost appeared for the second time on Sunday night. Shortly after they had gone to bed, a terrible crash in the hall suddenly startled them. They rushed downstairs and found that a large suit of old armour had come loose from its stand and fallen onto the stone floor. The Canterville ghost sat in a high-backed chair, rubbing his knees, his face showing intense pain. The twins had brought their pea-shooters and immediately fired two pellets at him. Their aim was so accurate that they could only have achieved it through long and careful practice on a handwriting teacher. Meanwhile, the United States Minister pointed his revolver at the ghost and, following Californian manners, ordered him to put up his hands. The ghost jumped up with a wild scream of fury and swept through the family like mist. As he passed, he put out Washington Otis’s candle, leaving them all in complete darkness."}

{"request_id":"5e0f6065ccb35be2cfe382e6","rewritten_text":"What kind of life could they have while Fate still left this dreadful future undecided? The wedding had to be delayed, whatever the cost. He was absolutely certain of that. He loved the girl deeply; when they sat together, even the touch of her fingers sent a delicious thrill through every nerve. Still, he understood clearly what his duty was. He knew he had no right to marry before he had committed the murder. Once it was done, he could stand beside Sybil Merton at the altar and place his life in her hands without fearing that he was doing wrong. Once it was done, he could hold her close, certain that she would never have to blush on his account or lower her head in shame. But the deed had to come first, and the sooner it happened, the better for them both.

Many men in his place would have chosen the easy pleasures of romance over the difficult climb of duty. Lord Arthur, however, had too strong a conscience to put pleasure ahead of principle. His love involved more than passion; Sybil represented everything good and noble to him. For a moment he felt a natural disgust at what was required of him, but that soon faded. His heart assured him that this was a sacrifice, not a sin, while his reason told him no other choice existed. He must decide whether to live for himself or for others. However dreadful his task, he knew he could not allow selfishness to defeat love. Sooner or later, all of us must make that same decision; all of us face the same question. For Lord Arthur it arrived early, before middle age could spoil his character with its cold, calculating distrust, or the shallow, fashionable selfishness of the times could eat away his heart. He therefore felt no hesitation about fulfilling his duty. Luckily for him, he was neither a mere dreamer nor someone who dabbled idly in things. If he had been, he would have hesitated like Hamlet and let indecision undermine his purpose. Instead, he was a thoroughly practical man. To him, life meant acting rather than thinking. He possessed that rarest of qualities: common sense.

By now the wild, confused feelings of the night before had vanished completely. He looked back almost with embarrassment at his frantic wandering through the streets and the fierce emotional pain he had suffered. The very depth and sincerity of that suffering now made it seem unreal. How, he wondered, could he have been foolish enough to rage and complain about something that could not be avoided?"}

{"request_id":"588ffa34ef1b6d9cb7713cf1","rewritten_text":"My father had been the life within my life, and his memory remained so. I might be grateful to another, but never again could I love or hope as formerly; all was pain, and even my pleasures were borne rather than enjoyed. I resembled a lonely hollow among mountains, enclosed on every side by sheer black cliffs, into which no warmth could enter and from which no path led to sunnier meadows. Thus, although friendship’s spirit brought me brief comfort, it could not revive me. It arrived like a gentle visitation; it departed, and I scarcely noticed its loss. The animating spirit had died within me. Do not wonder, then, that I greeted heaven’s choicest gift—a friend—with no greater joy on its arrival, nor mourned more bitterly when it went.

My friend’s name was Woodville. I shall tell his history briefly, that you may judge the coldness of a heart unmoved by his eloquence and tender sympathy, and understand how, being himself deeply unhappy, he and I were suited to console one another, had Misery’s Medusa-head not turned me into stone. Woodville’s sorrows did not strike the heart’s centre as mine did; his grief was natural, destined to cleanse rather than destroy the heart. Once its shadow lifted from him, he might emerge with greater happiness and brightness than before.

Woodville was a poor clergyman’s son and had been educated in the classics. He belonged to that tiny number whom fortune blesses from birth, lavishing gifts of mind and person upon them without limit, and sheltering them so specially that no blemish, however slight, nor disappointment, however passing, is permitted to approach. She seemed to have fashioned his mind from an excellence no impurity could stain, and given him an understanding which no falsehood could lead astray. His genius surpassed all measure; when it appeared, like a brilliant star rising in the east, every eye turned towards it in admiration. He was a Poet. That title has been so frequently debased that it cannot express the fullness of what he was. He resembled some ancient poet whom the muses had garlanded in his cradle, and upon whose lips the bees had fed. Moving among other men, he appeared surrounded by a celestial halo that set him apart and raised him above them. His extraordinary beauty, the flashing ardour of his eyes, and the rich music of words that held his hearers in silent, ecstatic wonder, made him tower above all others, until beside him they seemed created only to attend upon his higher excellence."}

{"request_id":"519f9b9517ec8e3c1e80b72b","rewritten_text":"Many American women, on leaving their homeland, begin to look permanently unwell, thinking this a sign of European elegance. Mrs. Otis had never made that mistake. She enjoyed excellent health and a truly remarkable supply of energy and good spirits. In many ways, indeed, she was quite English, a fine example of how much we now share with America—everything, of course, except language. Her eldest son was named Washington in a patriotic moment that his parents had enjoyed and he never stopped regretting. He was a fair-haired, fairly handsome young man. He had prepared himself for American diplomacy by leading the German dance at the Newport Casino for three seasons running, and even in London he was known as an excellent dancer. His only weaknesses were gardenias and the aristocracy. In other matters he was extremely sensible. Miss Virginia E. Otis was a girl of fifteen, graceful and lovely as a young deer, with a fine, free look in her large blue eyes. She was a splendid rider. Once she had raced old Lord Bilton on her pony twice around the park, winning by a length and a half just before the Achilles statue. The young Duke of Cheshire was so delighted that he proposed to her immediately. His guardians sent him back to Eton that same night, crying bitterly. After Virginia came the twins, usually called ‘The Stars and Stripes’ because they were always being whipped. They were delightful boys and, apart from the worthy Minister, the family’s only true republicans.

Canterville Chase stood seven miles from Ascot, the nearest railway station, so Mr. Otis had sent a telegram requesting an open carriage to meet them. They began the drive in high spirits. It was a beautiful July evening, and the air carried the faint scent of pine woods. Sometimes they heard a wood pigeon lingering over its own sweet song, or saw a pheasant’s shining breast deep among the rustling ferns. Small squirrels peeped at them from the beech trees as they passed. Rabbits darted through the bushes and across mossy little hills, their white tails raised. But as they turned into the avenue leading to Canterville Chase, clouds suddenly covered the sky. A strange stillness seemed to settle over the air, and a large flock of rooks flew silently overhead. Before they reached the house, several heavy drops of rain had fallen.

An old woman stood on the steps to welcome them, neatly dressed in black silk, with a white cap and apron."}

{"request_id":"610164ec53329c4878b3ff85","rewritten_text":"Many years back I became closely acquainted with a Mr. William Legrand. Descended from an old Huguenot family, he had formerly possessed wealth, but repeated misfortunes had brought him to poverty. To escape the humiliation attendant upon these reverses, he departed from New Orleans, his ancestral city, and settled on Sullivan's Island, near Charleston in South Carolina.

It is a most peculiar island, composed almost entirely of sea sand and extending about three miles in length. Nowhere is it more than a quarter-mile broad. A barely discernible creek divides it from the mainland, seeping through a waste of mud and reeds much frequented by marsh hens. Its plant life, as one would expect, is sparse, or at any rate stunted. Trees of considerable size are nowhere visible. At the western end, near Fort Moultrie and a handful of wretched timber houses occupied in summer by those escaping Charleston's dust and fever, the spiky palmetto does indeed grow. Apart from that western point and a strip of firm white beach along the ocean, however, thick growths of sweet myrtle cover the entire island—the very shrub so highly valued by English gardeners. Here it frequently rises fifteen or twenty feet, forming an almost impassable thicket and filling the air with its perfume.

Deep within this thicket, near the eastern, more secluded extremity of the island, Legrand had erected a little hut. He was living there when chance first brought us together. Our acquaintance quickly became friendship, for the solitary man possessed much to awaken regard and interest. I discovered that he was well schooled and endowed with uncommon mental abilities, though afflicted by dislike of mankind and contrary shifts between exuberance and gloom. He owned numerous books but seldom consulted them. His principal diversions were shooting and fishing, or wandering the shore and the myrtle groves in search of shells and insect specimens; his collection of insects was one a Swammerdamm might have coveted. An elderly negro named Jupiter usually joined these expeditions. He had received his freedom before the family's misfortunes, yet neither threats nor inducements could persuade him to surrender what he regarded as his privilege of following his young “Massa Will” wherever he went. It is quite possible that Legrand's relations, believing his mind somewhat disordered, had deliberately encouraged this stubbornness in Jupiter so that the wanderer might remain under watch and protection."}

{"request_id":"0c04813469ff1574d06e8fd5","rewritten_text":"“I then reasoned in this fashion—_a posteriori_. The murderers had made their escape through one of these windows. Granted that, they could not afterward have secured the sashes from within, in the state in which they were discovered; this very obvious difficulty had brought the police examination here to a halt. Nevertheless, the sashes _were_ secured. They _must_, therefore, possess some means of fastening themselves. That inference was unavoidable. Approaching the unblocked window, I extracted the nail with some trouble and tried to lift the sash. Just as I expected, it resisted every effort. I now knew there must be a hidden spring, and this confirmation assured me that at least my starting assumptions were sound, however inexplicable the business of the nails remained. A thorough search soon disclosed the concealed spring. I pressed it and, content with this finding, refrained from lifting the sash.

“I next put back the nail and studied it closely. Anyone leaving by this window could have drawn it shut again, whereupon the spring would engage; but replacing the nail would have been impossible. The inference was clear, and restricted my inquiries still further. The murderers _must_ have departed by the second window. If, as seemed likely, the two sashes possessed identical springs, there _must_ be some difference in the nails, or at least in the manner of their fastening. I climbed onto the bedstead's sacking and examined the other window carefully over the head-board. Reaching down behind the board, I easily located and pressed its spring, which proved, as anticipated, precisely like the first. Then I inspected the nail. It was equally stout and seemed fixed in exactly the same fashion—hammered almost to its head.

“You may suppose I was perplexed; but to suppose that is to mistake the character of my reasoning. In the language of the chase, I had never once been ‘at fault.’ Not for a moment had I lost the scent. Every link of the chain remained flawless. I had followed the mystery to its final issue, and that issue was _the nail_. As I have said, it looked in every particular like its counterpart at the other window. Yet this resemblance, however decisive it appeared, counted for absolutely nothing against the fact that the clue ended here, at this very point. ‘There _must_ be some peculiarity,’ I told myself, ‘in this nail.’ I put a finger to it, and its head, together with roughly a quarter-inch of the shaft, detached itself in my hand."}

{"request_id":"4a6728af7bfa235a50d4eff3","rewritten_text":"Elizabeth was sad and dispirited. Her usual activities no longer gave her pleasure; any enjoyment seemed a desecration of the dead. At that time she believed endless grief and tears were the proper tribute to innocence so cruelly ruined. She was no longer the happy girl who had walked beside the lake with me when we were younger, speaking ecstatically about our future. The first of those sorrows that loosen our attachment to the world had reached her, and its shadow had extinguished her sweetest smiles.

‘When I think, my dear cousin,’ she said, ‘of Justine Moritz’s miserable death, the world and everything in it look different to me. Before, the accounts of wickedness and injustice I read or heard seemed like stories from ancient times, or evils someone had invented. At least they were distant: my mind understood them, but my imagination could scarcely grasp them. Now suffering has reached our own home, and people seem like monsters thirsting for one another’s blood. Yet surely I am being unfair. Everyone believed that poor girl guilty; and if she had committed the crime she died for, she would indeed have been among the most corrupt human beings. To murder the son of her benefactor and friend for a few jewels—a child she had cared for since birth, and seemed to love as her own! I could never agree to anyone’s execution, but I would certainly have considered such a person unfit to live among others. Yet she was innocent. I know it, I feel it; and you believe it too, which strengthens my conviction. Oh, Victor, when a lie can look so much like truth, how can anyone feel sure of happiness? I feel as though I am walking along a cliff’s edge, with thousands pressing toward me, trying to push me into the depths. William and Justine have been murdered, and their murderer escapes. He moves freely through the world, perhaps even enjoying people’s respect. But even if I were sentenced to die on the scaffold for those same crimes, I would never exchange places with that wretched man.’

I listened in unbearable anguish. Though I had not performed the acts, I was in effect the real murderer. Elizabeth saw the agony in my face and gently took my hand. ‘My dearest friend,’ she said, ‘you must try to calm yourself. God knows how deeply these events have hurt me, but I am not as miserable as you are.’"}

{"request_id":"fadf4da5951e200d390d8076","rewritten_text":"When I woke, the stars were still brilliant, and Taurus, high in the southern sky, told me the hour was midnight. Troubled dreams had filled my sleep. I fancied myself summoned to Timon's final banquet; I arrived ravenous, the dishes were uncovered, and hot water released its fruitless steam while I fled the wrath of my host, who took on Raymond's likeness. To my fevered imagination, the vessels he flung after me brimmed with foul vapour, and my friend's figure, warped by countless distortions, swelled into an enormous spectre with the mark of pestilence upon its forehead. Upward and upward the spreading shadow climbed, filling and seeming to strain beyond the adamant vault that arched over the world, enclosing and supporting it. The nightmare grew torturous. With a violent effort I cast off sleep and restored reason to its accustomed office. My earliest thought was of Perdita. I must go back to her, must sustain her, finding in despair whatever nourishment could best uphold her stricken heart, and drawing her away from grief's wild extremes through duty's stern commands and the gentle tenderness of sorrow.

The stars alone directed my steps. Turning away from the dreadful wreck of the Golden City, I struggled hard before at last escaping its bounds. Beyond the walls I encountered a party of soldiers, borrowed a horse from one, and hurried towards my sister. In that brief interval the plain had altered. The camp had been dismantled; remnants of the dispersed army gathered here and there in little groups, every countenance darkened, every movement expressing bewilderment and fear.

With a burdened heart I went into the palace and paused, afraid to step farther, to speak, or even to look. Perdita was in the middle of the hall, seated on the marble floor, her head drooping upon her breast, her hair in disorder, her fingers ceaselessly interlacing. Pale as the marble itself, her face was drawn in every feature by anguish. Seeing me, she raised her eyes in question. The faint hope in that glance was agony; speech failed before I could utter a word, and I felt a horrible smile crease my mouth. She read my expression. Her head sank once more, her fingers resumed their restless movements. At length I found my voice, but its sound frightened her. The unhappy girl had understood my look and would not for the world have had her dreadful sorrow given form and certainty by unalterable words. Indeed, she appeared anxious to turn my thoughts elsewhere. Rising from the floor, she whispered, “Hush! Clara has wept so much and is asleep now. We must not wake her.”"}

{"request_id":"932eb747fe91fd249fa5dc1a","rewritten_text":"At length we reduced the weight of the chest by taking out two-thirds of what it held, and could then, with considerable difficulty, lift it clear of the pit. We placed the removed objects among the brambles and stationed the dog to watch them; Jupiter sternly commanded him neither to leave his post on any account nor to make a sound before we returned. Carrying the chest, we hurried homeward and reached the hut safely, though after immense exertion, at one in the morning. In our exhausted condition, human strength could accomplish no more at once. We rested until two and ate supper, then set off immediately for the hills, equipped with three strong sacks fortunately available at the hut. Shortly before four we reached the excavation, shared the remaining plunder between us as evenly as possible, and, leaving the pits open, once more started home. We laid down our golden loads at the hut for the second time just as the earliest pale rays of dawn appeared above the eastern treetops.

We were by now utterly spent, but our overwhelming excitement would not permit proper rest. After three or four hours of troubled sleep, we got up, almost as though by prior agreement, to inspect the treasure.

The chest had been filled to its very brim, and examining its contents occupied the whole day and most of the following night. There had been no semblance of system or arrangement; everything was piled together indiscriminately. When we had carefully sorted it all, our riches proved greater even than we originally imagined. The coins alone amounted to a little above four hundred and fifty thousand dollars, their worth reckoned as nearly as we could from the tables then in use. Not a trace of silver appeared. Everything was old gold, of numerous kinds: French, Spanish, and German pieces, a few English guineas, and certain counters unlike any we had previously encountered. Several coins were exceptionally large and heavy, their surfaces so worn that the inscriptions defeated us. None of the money was American. The gems presented greater difficulty in valuation. There were one hundred and ten diamonds, not a small one among them, and several of extraordinary size and quality; eighteen remarkably lustrous rubies; three hundred and ten emeralds, all exceedingly beautiful; twenty-one sapphires; and one opal. Each stone had been wrenched out of its mounting and tossed loose into the box. The mountings, which we separated from the rest of the gold, seemed to have been battered with hammers, apparently to make recognition impossible."}

{"request_id":"38d69250cfe8e13e7f82fd52","rewritten_text":"They were her whole life, more precious than a place made sacred by love, more precious than anything else on earth. The boys greeted the news of our departure with childish delight. Clara asked whether we were going to Athens. ‘Perhaps,’ I answered, and her face lit up with pleasure. There she could see her parents’ tomb and the land filled with memories of her father’s glory. She had thought about those places constantly, though she never spoke of them. Remembering them had changed her childhood cheerfulness into seriousness and filled her mind with lofty, restless thoughts.

We also had dear friends, however humble, whom we could not leave behind. There was the lively, obedient horse Lord Raymond had given his daughter, Alfred’s dog, and a pet eagle whose eyesight had weakened with age. Yet we could not list the beloved creatures coming with us without grieving over our terrible losses and sighing deeply for everything we had to leave. Tears filled Idris’s eyes as Alfred and Evelyn brought first a favourite rose tree, then a beautifully carved marble vase, insisting that these must come too. They cried out at the pity of being unable to bring the castle and the forest, the deer and birds, and every familiar, treasured thing. ‘My affectionate, foolish children,’ I said, ‘we have already lost forever treasures far greater than these. We leave these things to save treasures beside which they are worth nothing. Let us always remember our purpose and our hope. They will hold back the flood of grief we feel over lesser things.’

The children were easily distracted and soon returned to thinking about the pleasures ahead. Idris had vanished. She had gone away to conceal her weakness, leaving the castle and walking down to the little park to find a place where she could weep alone. I found her with her arms around an old oak, pressing her rosy lips to its rough trunk. Tears poured down her face, and she could not hold back her sobs and broken cries. It hurt me beyond words to see the woman I loved so lost in sorrow. I drew her close. As she felt my kisses on her eyelids and my arms holding her, she became aware again of all she still had. ‘You are very kind not to blame me,’ she said. ‘I weep because a bitter, unbearable sorrow is tearing at my heart. Yet I am happy. Other mothers mourn their children, other wives lose their husbands, while you and my children are still here with me.’"}

{"request_id":"90dd503052f619b81f5df576","rewritten_text":"Perdita’s name went unspoken, yet I had no doubt that his love for her explained his wavering intentions. And who deserved love more than my noble-hearted sister? Who had a better claim to the hand of this self-appointed king than she whose very glance was worthy of a queen of nations? She loved him as he loved her, although disappointment suppressed her passion while ambition fiercely contested his.

That evening we went together to the House. Raymond knew the coming debate would examine and settle his plans and prospects, yet he remained cheerful and unconcerned. As we entered the coffee room, a hum like ten thousand swarming beehives overwhelmed our ears. Politicians stood in clusters, their brows anxious, their voices loud or low. The aristocratic party, comprising England’s richest and most influential men, seemed calmer than the rest, since the question would be debated without their participation. Ryland and his supporters stood near the fire. A man of obscure origins, Ryland had inherited enormous wealth from his father, a manufacturer. In his youth he had witnessed the king’s abdication and the merging of the Lords and Commons into a single House. He had welcomed these advances of popular power and devoted his life to securing and extending them. Since then the landowners’ influence had increased, and at first Ryland had been pleased to watch Lord Raymond’s maneuvers draw away many supporters of his opponents. Now, however, matters were going too far. The poorer nobles welcomed the restoration of monarchy as a means of recovering their lost authority and privileges. The nearly extinguished spirit of royalty stirred again in people’s minds. Willing slaves, declaring themselves subjects, they stood ready to bow their necks beneath the yoke. A few upright, courageous spirits remained as pillars of the state, but the word republic had grown tiresome to ordinary ears. Many—whether a majority would soon be known—longed for the glitter and spectacle of royalty. Ryland rose to resist. He declared that only his tolerance had allowed this party to grow; the time for indulgence was over, and with one sweep of his arm he would clear away the cobwebs that blinded his countrymen.

Raymond’s friends greeted his arrival in the coffee room with something close to a shout. They crowded around him, counted their numbers, and explained in detail why they now expected to gain this or that member who had yet to declare his position."}

{"request_id":"32e11247bf9b4c66393cd30a","rewritten_text":"Madame and Mademoiselle L’Espanaye were not killed by spirits. Those responsible were physical beings, and they escaped by physical means. How, then? Fortunately, there is only one way to reason through this question, and it must bring us to a definite conclusion. Let us consider the possible exits one at a time. Clearly, the killers were in the room where Mademoiselle L’Espanaye was found, or at least in the adjoining room, when the group came upstairs. We therefore need to look for exits only from these two rooms. The police have exposed the floors, ceilings, and brickwork of the walls in every direction. No concealed exit could have escaped their scrutiny. But rather than rely on their eyes, I used my own to examine the place. There were, then, no concealed exits. Both doors opening from the rooms into the hallway were firmly locked, with the keys on the inside. Let us consider the chimneys. Although they remain a normal width for eight or ten feet above the fireplaces, farther along they would not allow even a large cat to pass through. Since escape by any of the means already considered is absolutely impossible, we are left with the windows. No one could have escaped through the front room’s windows without being seen by the crowd in the street. The murderers must therefore have gone through those in the back room. Having reached this conclusion so unmistakably, we cannot, as reasoning people, discard it because of apparent impossibilities. All that remains is to show that these apparent ‘impossibilities’ are not actually impossible.

“There are two windows in the bedroom. No furniture blocks one of them, and it is entirely visible. The bottom part of the other is concealed by the head of the cumbersome bedstead, which has been pushed right against it. The first was found securely fastened from inside. Those who tried to raise it used all their strength without success. A large hole had been drilled into the left side of its frame, and a very thick nail was found inserted there almost up to its head. When the other window was examined, a similar nail was found fitted in the same way; a forceful attempt to raise that sash also failed. The police were now completely convinced that no one had escaped in either direction. Consequently, they thought removing the nails and opening the windows would be unnecessary trouble.

“My own examination was rather more thorough, for the reason I have just explained: I knew this was the place where every apparent impossibility had to be shown, in reality, to be nothing of the kind."}

{"request_id":"23c0ec90ba15bcd23671b510","rewritten_text":"To my mind this curious falsehood had been told with remarkable simplicity and beauty; but I had heard it only once, and that alters matters. Doubtless the others had enjoyed it when it was new.

Sir Dinadan the Humorist awoke first, and presently woke everybody else with a practical joke of a decidedly shabby sort. He fastened several metal mugs to a dog’s tail and let the creature loose. Frantic with terror, it raced round and round the place, pursued by the roaring pack of other dogs, who collided and crashed into whatever lay before them, producing utter confusion and a deafening uproar. Every man and woman there laughed until the tears ran down; some tumbled from their chairs and rolled upon the floor in transports of delight. They might all have been children. Sir Dinadan took such pride in his achievement that he could not resist explaining, again and again until it grew tiresome, just how that immortal inspiration had struck him. Like humorists of his particular sort, he went on laughing long after everyone else had finished. So elated was he that he resolved to deliver a speech—a humorous speech, naturally. Never, I think, have I heard so many exhausted old jokes threaded together. He surpassed the minstrels in badness, surpassed even the circus clown. It was a singularly melancholy thing to sit there, thirteen hundred years before my birth, and hear again those feeble, stale, worm-eaten jokes that had given me stomach cramps as a boy thirteen hundred years later. It nearly persuaded me that a new joke could not possibly exist. Everybody laughed at these relics, but then everybody always does; I had observed that several centuries afterwards. The scoffer, of course, did not laugh—by which I mean the boy. He scoffed instead; nothing escaped his scorn. Most of Sir Dinadan’s jokes, he declared, were rotten, and the remainder petrified. I approved of ‘petrified,’ believing myself that only geological periods could properly express the majestic antiquity of certain specimens. But this pretty conceit found no lodging in the boy’s mind, since geology was not invented yet. Still, I noted the remark down, intending, if I survived, to educate the commonwealth until it appreciated it. There is no sense in discarding a good thing merely because its market is not yet ready.

Then Sir Kay stood up and began stoking his history-mill, using me as its fuel."}

{"request_id":"27fa507dd9d87c1f6c194ac9","rewritten_text":"It was after two when a sudden turning brought me beside the lake, near a small inlet where a skiff lay moored. Our house was not far distant, and I caught sight of my father and aunt walking on the lawn. I sprang into the boat and, being well practised in such undertakings, pushed away from the bank and strained every muscle to row quickly across. My father has often told me that, as I approached in white, with only my tartan rachan over me, my hair flowing upon my shoulders, and the boat flying faster than he could have thought my strength capable of driving it, I resembled a spirit more than a mortal girl. I reached the shore; my father steadied the boat; I sprang lightly out, and the next instant found myself in his arms.

Now, indeed, my life began. The dull sameness of everything around me gave way to the most brilliant scene of pleasure and joy. My happiness in my father’s society surpassed even my eager expectations. We were constantly together, and our conversation could never be exhausted. During his sixteen years away he had lived among peoples scarcely known in Europe. He had travelled through Persia, Arabia, and northern India, entering the dwellings of their inhabitants with a liberty granted to few Europeans. When we wearied of discussing our plans for the future, his accounts of their customs, his anecdotes, and his pictures of the landscape filled hours of exquisite enjoyment.

Affection spoke in a voice so unfamiliar to me that I listened with rapture when he described his feelings for me through those long years of seeming neglect. ‘At first,’ he said, ‘I could not endure the thought of my poor little daughter. Afterwards, as sorrow lessened and hope returned, I could turn only to her. In cities and deserts alike, her small fairy figure, as I pictured it, was always passing before my eyes. The northern wind refreshed me with a greater sweetness and softness because it appeared to bear something of your spirit. Often I resolved to return at once and carry you away to a fruitful island, where we might live forever in peace. On my journey home, so many fears troubled my ardent hopes that impatience became an intolerable pain. I dared not imagine the sun shining and the moon rising upon your grave instead of your living figure. But no, that has not happened; I possess my Mathilda, my comfort and my hope.’"}

{"request_id":"c104d19a5b547589cf557121","rewritten_text":"They were her life, dearer than any place sanctified by love, dearer than everything else the earth possessed. The boys greeted our planned departure with childish excitement. Clara asked whether we would go to Athens. ‘It is possible,’ I replied, and pleasure illuminated her face. There she would see her parents’ tomb and the country filled with memories of her father’s glory. Silently and ceaselessly she had dwelt on those scenes. Remembering them had transformed her early gaiety into seriousness and stirred ambitious, restless thoughts within her.

There were many cherished friends we could not abandon, humble creatures though they were: the spirited, obedient horse Lord Raymond had given his daughter, Alfred’s dog, and a pet eagle whose eyes had grown dim with age. Yet listing the favourites that would accompany us brought grief for our heavy losses, and deep sighs for all we must leave. Tears sprang to Idris’s eyes as Alfred and Evelyn presented first a beloved rose tree, then an exquisitely carved marble vase, insisting that these must be taken too. They lamented that we could not carry away the castle and forest, the deer and birds, and every familiar, treasured object. ‘My loving, foolish children,’ I said, ‘we have lost forever treasures far more precious than these. We abandon these things to protect treasures beside which they count for nothing. Let us never forget our purpose and our hope: they will build an unbreakable barrier against the overflowing sorrow we feel for trifles.’

The children were soon diverted and returned to imagining the pleasures ahead. Idris had disappeared. She had gone to conceal her weakness, escaping from the castle to the little park below, where she sought solitude and freedom to weep. I found her clinging to an ancient oak, pressing her rosy lips against its rough bark. Tears streamed down her face, and she could not restrain her sobs and broken cries. To see the woman so dear to my heart overwhelmed by sorrow filled me with the deepest grief. I drew her toward me. As she felt my kisses on her eyelids and the pressure of my arms, she became conscious once more of everything that remained to her. ‘You are very kind not to reproach me,’ she said. ‘I am weeping, and a bitter stab of unbearable sorrow tears my heart. Yet I am happy. Mothers mourn their children, wives lose their husbands, while I still have you and my children.’"}

{"request_id":"24d04c4431ab028d0cbfaf46","rewritten_text":"The flickering gas lamps curved away into the distance. Outside a small house enclosed by walls stood a single horse-drawn cab, its driver asleep inside. He hurried toward Portland Place, glancing back from time to time as though afraid someone was following him. At the corner of Rich Street, two men were reading a small notice on a wooden fence. A strange curiosity drew him across the road. As he approached, he saw the word “Murder” in black letters. He flinched, and his cheek flushed deeply. The notice offered a reward for information leading to the arrest of a man of medium height, aged between thirty and forty, wearing a bowler hat, black coat, and checked trousers, with a scar on his right cheek. He read it again and again, wondering whether the unfortunate man would be caught and how he had got his scar. Perhaps one day his own name would be posted on walls across London. Perhaps a reward would be offered for him too.

The thought made him feel sick with horror. He spun around and hurried into the night.

He barely knew where he went. He dimly remembered wandering through a maze of shabby houses and losing himself in a vast web of dark streets. Bright daylight was breaking when he finally reached Piccadilly Circus. Walking home toward Belgrave Square, he passed the great wagons heading for Covent Garden. The drivers, wearing white smocks, had friendly, sunburned faces and coarse, curly hair. They strode along strongly, cracking their whips and occasionally calling to one another. A plump boy rode a huge gray horse at the head of a team whose harness jingled. Primroses were tucked into his battered hat, and he laughed as his small hands held tightly to the horse’s mane. The high heaps of vegetables looked like masses of jade against the morning sky—green jade set against the pink petals of some wonderful rose. Lord Arthur felt strangely moved, though he could not explain why. The delicate beauty of the dawn seemed unbearably sad to him. He thought of all the days that begin in beauty and end in storms. And these country people, with their rough, cheerful voices and easygoing manners—what a strange version of London they saw! A London without the sins of night or the smoke of day: a pale, ghostly city, a deserted town of tombs. He wondered what they thought of it. Did they know anything of its magnificence and disgrace, its fierce, fiery pleasures and its terrible hunger, or all that it created and ruined between morning and evening?"}

{"request_id":"4360ca0bf5ccd21449a13479","rewritten_text":"At the head of the stairs he regained his composure and decided to utter his celebrated burst of demoniacal laughter. He had found this exceedingly effective on several previous occasions. It was reputed to have whitened Lord Raker’s wig in the course of one night, and had certainly caused three French governesses of Lady Canterville’s to give notice before completing their first month. Accordingly, he laughed his most appalling laugh, until the ancient vaulted ceiling echoed again and again. Scarcely had the dreadful reverberation faded, however, when a door opened and Mrs. Otis appeared in a pale blue dressing-gown. ‘I fear you are quite unwell,’ she said, ‘so I have brought some of Dr. Dobell’s tincture. If you are suffering from indigestion, it is an admirable cure.’ The ghost stared furiously at her and immediately prepared to transform himself into a great black dog, a feat for which he was deservedly famous, and to which the family physician invariably ascribed the lasting idiocy of Lord Canterville’s uncle, the Hon. Thomas Horton. But approaching footsteps checked his sinister intention. He merely allowed himself to grow faintly phosphorescent, and disappeared with a profound sepulchral groan just as the twins reached him.

Once in his room, he broke down altogether and surrendered to violent agitation. Naturally, the twins’ vulgarity and Mrs. Otis’s blatant materialism were exceedingly vexing; yet his greatest sorrow was his failure to wear the armour. He had trusted that the spectacle of a Spectre In Armour would move even modern Americans, if on no more rational grounds than respect for their national poet, Longfellow. He himself had passed many a tiresome hour over that poet’s graceful, pleasing verses while the Cantervilles were in town. Moreover, the armour belonged to him. He had worn it to great effect at the Kenilworth tournament, receiving warm compliments from no less a personage than the Virgin Queen. Yet, upon putting it on this time, he had been utterly overcome by the massive breastplate and steel helmet. He had crashed onto the stone paving, badly scraping both knees and bruising his right-hand knuckles.

For several days afterwards he was seriously unwell, scarcely leaving his room except to maintain the blood-stain in proper condition. By looking after himself with the utmost care, however, he recovered, and determined upon a third endeavour to terrify the United States Minister and his family."}

{"request_id":"273dc5939dec15ac0b3d3a1e","rewritten_text":"It now showed that I was 132,000 feet, or twenty-five miles, above the earth. From that height I could see no less than one three-hundred-and-twentieth of the planet’s entire surface. By nine o’clock the land to the east had disappeared again, but first I noticed that the balloon was moving rapidly north-northwest. The curve of the ocean below was unmistakable, although drifting banks of cloud often blocked my view. I also noticed that even the thinnest clouds never rose more than ten miles above sea level.

“At half past nine I made an experiment, tossing a handful of feathers out through the valve. Instead of floating as I expected, they fell straight down together like a bullet, moving so fast that they vanished within seconds. At first I could not explain this remarkable sight. I could not believe that my upward speed had suddenly increased so enormously. Then I realized that the air had become far too thin to support even feathers. They really were dropping as quickly as they seemed, and their downward speed, combined with my own upward movement, had caused my surprise.

“By ten o’clock there was very little that needed my immediate attention. Everything was going smoothly. I believed the balloon was rising faster every moment, though I no longer had any way to measure how quickly its speed was increasing. I felt no pain or discomfort and was in better spirits than at any time since leaving Rotterdam. I kept busy checking my equipment and renewing the air inside the chamber. I decided to refresh the air regularly, every forty minutes, more to protect my health than because it truly needed changing so often. Meanwhile, I could not help imagining what lay ahead. My thoughts delighted in the wild, dreamlike regions of the moon. For once my imagination felt completely free, wandering wherever it pleased among the changing wonders of a shadowy, shifting land. First I saw ancient, venerable forests, jagged cliffs, and waterfalls thundering down into bottomless chasms. Then suddenly I entered places still and empty in the noonday light, where no breath of wind ever disturbed the silence. Vast meadows of poppies and slender, lilylike flowers stretched away into an exhausting distance, forever quiet and motionless."}

{"request_id":"553dcd3ef90748683a1f4868","rewritten_text":"During his brief visit this time, Emma had hardly seen him, though enough to get their first meeting over with and to feel that the wounded pride and self-importance now colouring his manner did nothing to improve him. Indeed, she was beginning to wonder how she had ever found him agreeable at all. Seeing him was so closely bound up with painful feelings that, apart from its moral value as a penance, a lesson, an opportunity for salutary humiliation, she would have been grateful for a promise never to encounter him again. She wished him every happiness; but his presence hurt her, and she would take greatest satisfaction in his prosperity twenty miles away.

The discomfort of his continuing to live in Highbury, however, would certainly diminish with his marriage. It would spare them many unnecessary anxieties and ease many awkward situations. A Mrs. Elton would provide an explanation for any change in their dealings; their former closeness could fade without attracting comment. They could almost begin their polite acquaintance afresh.

Emma gave very little thought to the woman herself. No doubt she was good enough for Mr. Elton, accomplished enough for Highbury, and handsome enough—probably to seem plain beside Harriet. As for her family connections, Emma felt entirely at ease. She was convinced that, despite his much-proclaimed claims and his contempt for Harriet, he had gained nothing there. On that point, the truth seemed discoverable. What sort of woman she was must remain uncertain, but who she was could be established; and apart from the ten thousand pounds, nothing suggested that she was Harriet’s superior. She brought no distinguished name, noble ancestry, or important alliances. Miss Hawkins was the younger of two daughters of a Bristol—merchant, of course, one must call him. But since the entire proceeds of his business career seemed so modest, it was hardly unjust to suppose that his particular trade had been modest in standing too. She had generally spent part of each winter in Bath, but Bristol was her home, the very centre of Bristol. Although both her parents had died some years before, an uncle remained, employed in the law. No more precisely respectable description was ventured than that he worked in the law; and the daughter had lived with him. Emma imagined him doing the drudgery for some attorney, too dull to advance. All the splendour of the connection seemed to rest on the elder sister, who had married extremely well: a gentleman with a very substantial business near Bristol, who kept two carriages! That was the grand conclusion of the account; that was Miss Hawkins’s crowning distinction."}

{"request_id":"490d9caea7590c8634185fc0","rewritten_text":"In fact, I had no worries about her. She was what people call a capable woman and could manage her worldly affairs without my help. To tell the truth, I believe she always considered me an idle boy, a useless extra, fit only for building castles in the air, and was rather pleased to be rid of me. It was dark when I said goodbye. Taking along as my assistants the three creditors who had caused me so much trouble, we carried the balloon, its basket, and its equipment by an indirect route to the place where the remaining supplies were stored. We found everything undisturbed, and I immediately set to work.

“It was the first of April. As I have said, the night was dark, without a single visible star, and intermittent drizzle made us extremely uncomfortable. My principal concern, however, was the balloon. Despite its protective varnish, the moisture was beginning to make it rather heavy; the powder, too, was vulnerable to damage. I therefore kept my three debt collectors hard at work, packing ice around the central barrel and stirring the acid in the others. Nevertheless, they continually pestered me with questions about my intentions for all this equipment and complained bitterly about the dreadful labor I demanded of them. They could not see, they said, what benefit they might gain from getting soaked to the skin merely to participate in such horrible spells. I grew anxious and worked as hard as I could, for I truly believe the fools thought I had made a pact with the devil and that my present activities were, in short, thoroughly disreputable. I was consequently very afraid they would abandon me altogether. I managed to reassure them, though, by promising to settle every debt in full as soon as I had completed the business in hand. Naturally, they interpreted these remarks in their own way, no doubt imagining that somehow I would acquire an enormous quantity of ready cash. As long as I paid everything I owed, with a little extra for their services, I dare say they cared very little what became of either my soul or my body.

“After about four and a half hours, I found that the balloon was sufficiently inflated. I therefore attached the basket and loaded all my implements into it, taking care to include the condensing apparatus, a plentiful supply of water, and a large stock of provisions such as pemmican, which packs considerable nourishment into relatively little space."}

{"request_id":"a5c542d385b77bb47e37d369","rewritten_text":"“By eight o’clock I had reached a height of seventeen miles above the earth. It seemed clear that I was rising faster and faster, and that a slight increase in speed would have been noticeable even without the ballast I had thrown out. The severe pains in my head and ears returned from time to time, and my nose still bled occasionally. Overall, though, I suffered much less than anyone might have expected. Breathing grew steadily more difficult, and each breath caused an uncomfortable spasm in my chest. I therefore unpacked the condensing equipment and prepared it for immediate use.

“The earth looked truly beautiful at this stage of my ascent. To the west, north, and south, an apparently smooth ocean stretched as far as I could see. Its blue grew deeper every moment, and its surface was already beginning to look slightly curved. Far to the east, but still clearly visible, lay the islands of Great Britain, the whole Atlantic coastline of France and Spain, and a small part of northern Africa. I could no longer make out a single building. Humanity’s proudest cities had vanished completely from the earth’s face. The rock of Gibraltar had shrunk to a faint speck. Beyond it, the dark Mediterranean stretched eastward to the limit of my sight, its shining islands scattered like stars across the heavens. At last, the entire sea seemed to plunge over the edge of the horizon into an abyss. I found myself standing on tiptoe, listening for the echoes of that enormous waterfall. Above me, the sky was pitch-black, and the stars shone brilliantly.

“About this time, the pigeons appeared to be suffering badly, so I decided to set them free. I first untied a beautiful pigeon with grey-mottled feathers and put him on the edge of the wicker basket. He seemed extremely distressed. He looked anxiously about, fluttered his wings, and cooed loudly, but nothing could persuade him to leave the basket. At last I picked him up and threw him about six yards away from the balloon. Instead of flying downward as I had expected, however, he struggled furiously to return, giving sharp, piercing cries as he did so. Eventually he managed to regain his place on the rim. Almost as soon as he reached it, his head sank onto his breast, and he fell dead inside the basket."}

{"request_id":"711eb85fdedbe8f9bb13da7e","rewritten_text":"We spent a few sorrowful hours waiting for eleven o’clock, when the trial was due to begin. My father and the rest of the family had to attend as witnesses, so I went with them to court. Throughout that miserable mockery of justice I endured terrible torment. The court would decide whether my curiosity and reckless experiments would lead to the deaths of two people. One was a smiling child, full of innocence and happiness. The other would suffer an even more dreadful murder, made more horrifying by the shame attached to it. Justine was a good young woman whose qualities should have brought her a happy life. Now all that promise was to end in a dishonored grave—and I was responsible! A thousand times over, I would rather have confessed to the crime she was accused of. But I had been elsewhere when it happened. Such a confession would have been dismissed as a madman’s ravings and would not have cleared the woman suffering because of me.

Justine appeared calm. She wore mourning clothes, and the seriousness of her feelings made her face, always attractive, extraordinarily beautiful. She seemed secure in her innocence and did not tremble, though thousands stared at her and cursed her. Whatever kindness her beauty might otherwise have stirred had been driven from their minds by thoughts of the terrible crime they believed she had committed. She was composed, but clearly maintaining that composure took effort. Since her earlier confusion had been used as evidence of guilt, she forced herself to look brave. On entering the court she glanced around and quickly found where we were sitting. A tear seemed to cloud her eye when she saw us, but she immediately regained control. Her expression of sorrowful affection seemed to declare her complete innocence.

The trial opened. After the lawyer prosecuting her had explained the charge, several witnesses were called. A number of strange facts worked against her. They might have shaken anyone who lacked the proof of her innocence that I possessed. She had been away from home all night when the murder took place. Near morning, a woman going to market had seen her close to where the murdered child’s body was later discovered. The woman asked what she was doing there. Justine looked very strange and gave only a confused answer that made no sense. She came home around eight o’clock. When someone asked where she had spent the night, she said she had been searching for the child and urgently asked whether anyone had heard anything about him."}

{"request_id":"416c7497de2feaf5acade075","rewritten_text":"I cannot express the ecstatic relief with which I turned from political quarrels in our own country and physical suffering in distant lands to my beloved home: that chosen dwelling of goodness and love, of peace and the exchange of every cherished feeling. Had I never left Windsor, I would not have felt these emotions so strongly. But in Greece I had been at the mercy of fear and terrible upheaval; there, after a time of anxiety and grief, I had watched two people pass away whose very names stood for greatness and virtue. Such suffering, however, could never enter the family circle that remained to me while we lived peacefully, secluded in our dear forest. The advancing years did bring certain small changes, and time, as always, left the mark of mortality upon our pleasures and hopes. Idris, so affectionate as a wife, sister, and friend, was a tender, devoted mother. Motherhood was no pastime for her, as it was for many; it was a passion. We had had three children, and the second had died while I was in Greece. That loss had mixed fear and sorrow with the triumphant joy of being a mother. Until then, these little creatures born of her, these young heirs to her fleeting life, had seemed assured of continued existence. Now she feared that the merciless destroyer would seize her surviving darlings as it had taken their brother. The slightest illness sent spasms of terror through her; any separation from them made her miserable. She had stored all her happiness in their fragile lives and remained constantly alert lest the stealthy thief steal these precious jewels as it had before. Fortunately, she had little actual cause for alarm. Alfred, now nine, was an honest, manly little boy, with a bright forehead, gentle eyes, and a temperament both kind and independent. Our youngest was still an infant, but his soft cheeks bloomed with health, and his tireless liveliness filled our home with innocent laughter.

Clara had outgrown the age whose speechless unawareness aroused Idris’s fears. Idris loved her, as everyone did. She combined such intelligence with innocence, such sensitivity with patience, and such seriousness with perfect good humour. Her extraordinary beauty and endearing simplicity made her a pearl among our most cherished possessions, a treasure of excellence and wonder.

At the beginning of winter, our nine-year-old Alfred began attending Eton. He saw this as his first step toward manhood and was correspondingly delighted. Studying and playing with others brought out the finest qualities in his character: his patient determination, his generosity, and his firmness tempered by self-control."}

{"request_id":"a87b8b18e81620ba3499bb5e","rewritten_text":"“Some time passed before I recovered enough to take care of the balloon’s usual needs. When I finally inspected it closely, I was greatly relieved to find it undamaged. All my equipment was safe, and luckily I had lost neither ballast nor food. In fact, I had fastened everything so securely that there was no chance of losing it. My watch showed six o’clock. I was still rising quickly, and the barometer put my height at three and three-quarter miles. Directly below me on the ocean was a small black object, a little longer than it was wide. It seemed about the size of a child’s domino and looked very much like one. Through my telescope I could clearly see that it was a British ship carrying ninety-four guns. It was sailing as close to the wind as possible, pitching heavily in the waves, with its bow pointing west-southwest. Apart from that ship, I saw only sea, sky, and the sun, which had risen long before.

“It is time I explained to Your Excellencies the purpose of this dangerous journey. You will remember that my desperate circumstances in Rotterdam had finally led me to decide to kill myself. I did not actually hate life itself. Rather, the miseries brought on by my situation had tormented me beyond what I could bear. I wanted to live, yet I was tired of living. In that state of mind, the treatise I found on the bookseller’s stall suggested a possible escape. At last I made my decision. I would depart but remain alive; I would leave the world but still exist. To stop speaking in riddles, I resolved to face whatever happened and try to make my way to the moon. Now, so that you will not think me madder than I really am, I shall explain as well as I can the reasoning behind my belief. Difficult and unquestionably dangerous as such a journey would be, I thought it was not wholly beyond what a bold person could accomplish.

“The first question was the moon’s actual distance from the earth. The average distance between the centers of the two planets is 59.9643 times the earth’s radius at the equator, or only about 237,000 miles. I say average distance because we must remember that the moon travels in an ellipse. The eccentricity of that ellipse amounts to no less than 0.05484 of its major semi-axis—that is, half its longest diameter—and the earth’s center lies at one of its focal points. If I could somehow arrange to meet the moon at perigee, when it is nearest the earth, the distance I have just given would be considerably reduced.”"}

{"request_id":"ed0be747830c9af334a9b69f","rewritten_text":"But the thought that sustained me when I began my work now only drives me deeper into despair. All my ideas and hopes have come to nothing. Like the archangel who wanted unlimited power, I am chained in an everlasting hell. My imagination was vivid, and I also had great powers of reasoning and concentration. Together, these abilities allowed me to conceive of making a man and to carry out that creation. Even now, I cannot calmly remember the dreams that filled me while my work was unfinished. In my thoughts I walked through heaven, sometimes rejoicing in my powers, sometimes burning with excitement at what they might achieve. Since childhood I had held great hopes and high ambitions. But how far I have fallen! Oh, my friend, had you known me then, you would not recognize the broken man before you. I rarely felt despair. I seemed to be carried forward toward a magnificent destiny, until I fell, never, never to rise again.”

Must I lose this remarkable man, then? I have longed for a friend, someone who would understand and love me. Here on these empty seas I have found him, yet I fear that I have gained him only to learn his worth and then lose him. I want to help him find happiness in life again, but he rejects the thought.

“Thank you, Walton,” he said, “for your kindness toward someone so miserable. But when you speak of new relationships and new loves, do you believe anyone could replace those I have lost? Could any man be what Clerval was to me, or any woman become another Elizabeth? Even when we do not love our childhood companions for any exceptional qualities, they have a hold on our hearts that later friends rarely gain. They know our childhood natures, which may change but are never entirely erased. They can therefore judge more confidently whether our actions come from honest motives. Unless dishonesty has appeared early, a sister or brother will never suspect the other of deceit. Yet another friend, however devoted, may find himself viewed with suspicion despite everything. My friends were dear to me not merely through long familiarity and shared experience, but for their own worth. Wherever I go, Elizabeth's comforting voice and Clerval's conversation will always whisper in my ears. They are dead, and in this loneliness only one feeling can persuade me to stay alive. If I were pursuing some great task or plan that promised widespread benefit to other people, I could live to complete it."}

{"request_id":"e09cd8ba6619b60d5cc85fd3","rewritten_text":"Many years ago, I became close friends with a man named William Legrand. He came from an old Huguenot family and had once been rich, but a series of disasters had left him poor. To escape the humiliation of his changed circumstances, he left New Orleans, where his ancestors had lived, and settled on Sullivan’s Island, near Charleston in South Carolina.

It is an unusual island, made almost entirely of sea sand. It is about three miles long and nowhere more than a quarter of a mile wide. A barely visible creek separates it from the mainland, creeping through a tangle of reeds and mud where marsh hens gather. As one might expect, the plants are sparse, or at least small. There are no large trees. The bristly palmetto does grow near the western end, where Fort Moultrie stands alongside some shabby wooden houses. In summer, these house people escaping Charleston’s dust and fever. Apart from this western tip and a strip of firm white beach facing the sea, the entire island is covered in thick sweet myrtle, a shrub much admired by English gardeners. Here it often reaches fifteen or twenty feet in height, forming almost impassable thickets and filling the air with its scent.

Deep within these thickets, near the island’s more distant eastern end, Legrand had built a little hut. He was living there when I first met him entirely by chance. Our acquaintance soon grew into friendship, for the solitary man had many qualities that interested me and earned my respect. He was well educated and unusually intelligent, but disliked humanity and passed through unpredictable moods of excitement and sadness. He owned many books, though he seldom read them. His main pleasures were shooting, fishing, and wandering along the beach or among the myrtles to look for shells and insects. His insect collection would have made even an expert like Swammerdamm envious. On these outings he was generally joined by an old Black man named Jupiter. Jupiter had been freed from slavery before the family lost its fortune, but neither threats nor promises could persuade him to leave young ‘Master Will,’ whom he considered it his right to follow and serve. It seems quite possible that Legrand’s relatives, believing his mind to be somewhat unsettled, had encouraged Jupiter’s stubbornness so that someone would watch over and protect their wandering kinsman."}

{"request_id":"ce562a3cfc3fce826344f6ed","rewritten_text":"As soon as Mrs. Musgrove found herself alone with Anne, she said, ‘Oh, Miss Anne, I do wish Mrs. Charles had some of your skill with those children. They behave like different creatures when you are with them! Usually, though, they are terribly spoiled. It is a pity you cannot teach your sister how to manage them. They are as fine and healthy as any children I have seen, poor little dears, and I say that without favouritism. But Mrs. Charles has no idea how to handle them! Goodness, they can be such a nuisance. I assure you, Miss Anne, it makes me less eager to have them at our house than I would otherwise be. I think Mrs. Charles is rather annoyed that I do not invite them more often. But you know how unpleasant it is to have children around when you must constantly tell them, “Don’t do this,” and “Don’t do that,” or can only keep them reasonably quiet by giving them more cake than they should have.’

Anne also heard this from Mary: ‘Mrs. Musgrove considers all her servants so reliable that questioning them would amount to treason. But I assure you, without exaggerating, her senior housemaid and laundry maid spend all day wandering about the village instead of working. I run into them wherever I go. I can scarcely visit my nursery twice without finding some sign of them. If Jemima were not the most trustworthy, dependable person alive, they would ruin her. She tells me they are always trying to persuade her to go walking with them.’ Mrs. Musgrove, meanwhile, had her own complaint: ‘I make it a rule never to interfere in my daughter-in-law’s affairs, because I know that would cause trouble. But I shall tell you, Miss Anne, since you may be able to put things right, that I think very poorly of Mrs. Charles’s nursery maid. I hear strange accounts of her; she is always out wandering. And I know myself that she dresses so extravagantly she could spoil any servant she meets. Mrs. Charles trusts her completely, I know. Still, I wanted to give you this warning so you could keep an eye on her. If you notice anything wrong, you need not hesitate to mention it.’

Mary also complained that when they dined with other families at the Great House, Mrs. Musgrove often failed to give her the place of honour her rank deserved. She saw no reason why being treated as one of the household should mean losing her proper place."}

{"request_id":"5d2dc87448ed06d1b6dfd779","rewritten_text":"When my tears stopped, reason and memory returned. I began to think more calmly about what had happened and what I ought to do. Only a few hours had passed, yet my life had changed completely. Since that morning, I seemed to have lived through years. My father was dead to me. For a moment I imagined him lying white-haired in his coffin, and myself, no longer young but nearing old age, mourning his death at its proper time. Yet that was not true. I was still young—oh, much too young! And to other people he was still alive. But I, in my misery, must never see him or speak to him again. I must flee him more urgently than I would my greatest enemy. Alone or among crowds, I must never look upon him again. This thought left me gasping with grief. It took such a hold on my mind that for a while I could think of nothing else. From now on, I decided, I would live in the bleakest isolation. I would go to the Continent and become a nun, not out of religious devotion, since I was not Catholic, but to shut myself away from the world forever. There I could weep alone, where the voices of the living would never reach me.

But my father—my beloved, desperately unhappy father? Would he die? Would he never conquer the violent passion that ruled him so mercilessly? Perhaps many, many years from now, when age had cooled the burning feelings tormenting him, he might become my father again. The thought eased my frown, and I felt a faint, sad smile soften the suffering on my lips. I wept to feel it. I dared to hope for a better future. Years would have to pass, but hope would make them fly. Even if they dragged painfully, they would still pass, and I would not have lost my father forever. Let him spend another sixteen years wandering in loneliness. Let him once again cry out his wild complaints to vast forests and mighty waterfalls in a foreign land. Let him face terrible dangers and hardships that break the spirit. Let the southern sun once more scorch his cheeks, worn by passion, and cold night rains fall on him and chill his blood.

To such a life I condemn you, miserable father! Go! Spend your days among savages and your nights beneath the open sky! Let your limbs wear out and your heart grow cold, until all youth within you is dead!"}

{"request_id":"e410b5b546a644e81f0bd95d","rewritten_text":"Do not misunderstand me: I was never truly mad. Even when my wild thoughts seemed to be driving me insane, I knew what was happening to me. I revealed those thoughts only in silence and solitude. The people around me saw none of this. They saw only an unhappy girl whose spirit was broken, who spoke softly and gently, and whose lowered eyes sometimes shed tears that she tried to hide. They saw someone who preferred to be alone, avoided attention, and never smiled. No, I never smiled. That was all they knew.

Well, I escaped. I left my guardian’s home and was never heard from again. The letters I left, together with other circumstances I had carefully arranged, led people to believe that I had killed myself. As a result, they searched less thoroughly than they otherwise would have. Soon every trace and memory of me disappeared. I sailed from London in a small ship heading for a port in northern England. Now that my attempt had succeeded and I was completely alone, peace returned. The sea was calm, and the ship moved gently forward. I sat on deck beneath the open sky and felt like a different person. I was no longer the wild, frantic, deeply miserable Mathilda. I was a young woman committed to a hermit’s solitude, who must work to keep her heart free of turmoil and unholy despair. The unusual dress I had chosen, resembling a nun’s habit; the knowledge that only I knew I still existed; the loneliness in which I would spend the rest of my life—all these encouraged gentle thoughts in my wounded heart. The breeze moving through my hair revived me. Quietly I watched sunlight sparkling on the waves and birds chasing one another over the water, their feathers barely touching its surface. I slept without troubling dreams and woke refreshed, ready to enjoy my peaceful freedom again.

Four days later we reached our destination. I did not want to stay near the coast, so I went inland at once. I had already decided what sort of place I would live in: an isolated house on a broad plain, far from any other home. There I could see the entire horizon and walk for miles without being disturbed by the sight of another person. I did not hate humanity, but I felt that my emotions could remain gentle only if I lived alone. I settled in a wide, lonely place, on a bleak heath scattered with stones. Short grass grew between them, and here and there a few rushes stood beside a small pool."}

{"request_id":"a9f4381081ce8121529851f0","rewritten_text":"Many years ago I became close friends with a man named William Legrand. He came from an old Huguenot family and had once possessed a fortune, but a succession of misfortunes had left him poor. To escape the humiliation of his losses, he abandoned New Orleans, his ancestral city, and settled on Sullivan’s Island, near Charleston, South Carolina.

It is a remarkable island, made almost entirely of sea sand, about three miles long and nowhere more than a quarter of a mile wide. A barely visible creek separates it from the mainland, seeping through a wilderness of reeds and mud much frequented by marsh hens. As one might expect, vegetation is sparse, or at least stunted. There are no large trees. Near the western tip, where Fort Moultrie stands beside a few wretched wooden buildings occupied in summer by people fleeing Charleston’s dust and fever, the bristling palmetto does grow. Apart from that western point and a strip of firm white beach along the seaward shore, however, the entire island is covered by dense sweet myrtle, the shrub so highly valued by English gardeners. Here it often reaches fifteen or twenty feet, creating an almost impassable thicket and loading the air with fragrance.

Deep within this thicket, near the island’s more distant eastern end, Legrand had built himself a small hut. He was living there when I first met him entirely by chance. Our acquaintance soon developed into friendship, for much about this recluse inspired interest and respect. I found him well educated and exceptionally intelligent, though distrustful of humanity and prone to difficult changes of mood, alternating between enthusiasm and gloom. He owned many books but seldom opened them. His principal diversions were shooting and fishing, or wandering along the beach and through the myrtles in search of shells and insect specimens. His insect collection might have made Swammerdamm envious. On these expeditions he was generally accompanied by an elderly Black man named Jupiter. Jupiter had been freed from slavery before the family’s financial reverses, yet neither threats nor promises could persuade him to surrender what he regarded as his right to follow his young “Massa Will” wherever he went. It is quite possible that Legrand’s relatives, believing his mind somewhat unstable, had encouraged this stubborn devotion in Jupiter so that someone would watch over and protect the wanderer."}

{"request_id":"e8ae449a87e4218b8498fab9","rewritten_text":"“Put down your weapons, fellow men, my brothers! If you repent, you will find forgiveness, help, and brotherly love. We care for you because you share our fragile human form. Each of you will find a friend and a host among our soldiers. Must we make enemies of one another when the plague, our common enemy, already hangs over us, rejoicing in a slaughter more cruel than its own?”

Both armies paused. Our soldiers kept a firm grip on their weapons and stared sternly at the enemy. The other men had not laid down their arms, more out of fear than any desire to fight. They looked at one another, each hoping someone would set an example, but they had no leader. Adrian leaped from his horse and went to a man who had just been killed. “He was a man,” he cried, “and now he is dead. Quickly, bind the wounds of those who have fallen! Let no one die. Let no more souls escape through your merciless cuts to tell God how brothers have murdered brothers. Dress their wounds and return them to their friends. Drive the tigers’ hearts from your breasts. Throw away these tools of cruelty and hatred. While our deadly fate gives us this moment’s pause, let every man become a brother, a protector, and a support to the others. Put down your bloodstained weapons, and let some of you hurry to tend these wounds.”

As he spoke, he knelt and lifted a man into his arms. Warm blood poured from the wounded man’s side, and he gasped. Both armies had grown so quiet that everyone could hear his moans. Hearts that moments before had wanted nothing but slaughter now beat anxiously with hope and fear for this one man. Adrian pulled off his military scarf and wrapped it around the sufferer, but he was too late. The man gave a deep sigh. His head dropped back, and his limbs went limp. “He is dead,” Adrian said as the body fell from his arms to the earth. He bowed his head in grief and awe. The whole world’s fate seemed tied to this single death. On both sides, the soldiers threw down their weapons. Even the veterans cried. Our men reached out to their enemies, and love and deep friendship flooded every heart. The two armies mingled, unarmed and hand in hand, discussing only how they could help each other. Their former enemies now stood together. One side regretted its earlier cruelty, the other its recent violence. Together they obeyed the General’s order to head toward London."}

{"request_id":"1ae931f8664fd7f883965dab","rewritten_text":"He did not mention Perdita, but I was sure his love for her explained his uncertain intentions. And who deserved love more than my noble-hearted sister? Who was more worthy to marry this man who would make himself king than a woman whose very glance was fit for a queen of nations? She loved him as he loved her, though disappointment had subdued her passion and ambition battled fiercely against his.

That evening we went together to the House. Raymond knew that the coming debate would discuss and decide his plans and prospects, yet he seemed cheerful and unconcerned. As we entered the coffee room, a noise like ten thousand hives of swarming bees overwhelmed us. Politicians stood in anxious groups, speaking loudly or in low, deep voices. The aristocratic party, made up of England’s wealthiest and most influential men, seemed calmer than the others, since the question would be debated without their involvement. Ryland and his supporters stood near the fire. He came from an undistinguished family but had inherited enormous wealth from his father, a manufacturer. As a young man he had witnessed the king’s abdication and the merging of the Houses of Lords and Commons. He had welcomed these gains in popular power and spent his life strengthening and extending them. Since then, landowners had become more influential. At first, Ryland had been pleased to see Lord Raymond’s schemes draw supporters away from his opponents. But matters were now going too far. The poorer nobles welcomed the prospect of a restored monarchy, expecting it to return the power and rights they had lost. The nearly dead enthusiasm for royalty was awakening again. People were ready to become willing slaves, appointing themselves subjects and bending their necks to the yoke. A few upright, courageous spirits remained to support the state. But ordinary people had grown tired of the word republic, and many longed for royalty’s glitter and display. The outcome would reveal whether they were a majority. Ryland prepared to resist. He declared that the party had grown only because he had tolerated it. Now his patience was over, and with one sweep of his arm he would clear away the cobwebs blinding his countrymen.

